      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>INDUSTRIAL PARTNERSHIP BETWEEN EMPLOYERS AND EMPLOYED,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>Sir George Livesay's Views.</head>
        <p>Sir George Livesay's Views.</p>
        <p>rhat a real Industrial Partnership between Employers</p>
        <p>d Employed would solve all the practical problems uising out of the relations between capital and labour, i a fairly obvious. Such a partnership would be more than merely profit-sharing, for it would mean a genuine and harmonious working together of two classes whose interests are so often supposed to be in opposition to each other, although they are, in fact and in principle, identical. Sir George Livesay, whose good work at the South Metropolitan Gas Company give him a right to speak upon this subject, recently read a paper upon "-Industrial Partnership and the Preventiqn of Distress," at a special meeting of the Council of the Charity Organization Society (Times 31st March), which was full of interest and euggestion. He was Able to point to the great practical success of his plan in his gas company, where some 4,000 of the men employed there have £170,000 of their own in the concern, either as stockholders or depositor, —or £4O per man. As one result of this partnerships there has been no strike nor any trouble of any kind amongst the men since it was tarted in 1889, and there is no reason why there should eveiehe again. In truth. one effect of a genuine industrial partnership between employers and employed, would be to put down strikes as needless survivals of barbarous times, and, to a great extent, to do away with trade unions altogether. They would, of course, become unnecessary if there was no, fighting to be done to keep up wages, or to keep down hours, whileloven their existence as benefit societies' would soon be shown to be of doubtful value in the face of the workmen's thrift as proved by his investments. • It is a common thing to speak of the thrift of the French people ; but the true basis of this thrift is their ownership of the land upon which they work and live. Peasant proprietors must be thrifty, or they would cease to exist, and this property in the soil is the keynote to their success. It is also the remedy for sodialism ; and the wild doctrines of socialists, which form a danger to this country, as they do to, Germany especiallk, come to nothing as soon as the workers have some property,some capital, some stake in the commonwealth of their own.. The real source of all trouble between employers and employed, is because the wage-earners, even if they are. well paid, have no partnership in what should be the common property of both. Good wages are quickly spent, and modern teaching is all against thrift as a form of saving by self-denial. But when this saving takes the form of a share in the enterprise carried on by the combination of employers and employed, as rep- resenting the capital and labour that is required in every undertaking, its whole character is altered. Where a workingman holds a definite amount of stock in a gas company, or in any other similar concern, his interest in its success and its growth is personal and permanent, and he is not tempteciSto do, or encourage, anything that would injure his property. He becomes, of course. to the extent of his holding, a capitalist, and thus he • unites in himself the interests of capital and labour in a way that he can, and does, thoroughly understand and appreciate. Sir George Livesay said that the thriftless- ness of the average workingman, on which it is so usual •to lecture him, really arises from the fact that he regards it as a hopeless task to accumulate money, and so does•not try. But perhaps, the main point is, thatemployers gener- ally should be brought to see the benefits of the system ,and to take it up in earnest. Almost every large concern and enterprise is nowadays carried out upon the 'lines or Joint-stock Companies. But if, besides this bringing together of small capitalists, which is, after all, the basis and the meaning of_ all joint-stock endeavour, we could include the workers themselves,the strength of ¶the resulting partnership would be immensely greater. The arrangement of capital in the form. of stock or shares, offers every facility for the men to take up small holdings in the concern of which they are also working :members. Amongst employers there is thefear of what may be the result of.bringing their workmen into =partnership; but the practical answer to this objection iiothat, whorevei the thing has been fairly done, it has worked ontsuccessfully. It has been found, in fact, that themen have responded to the trust placed in them by their masters And so both parties have pulled together in harmony. Anether difficulty is a sort of suspicious attitude on the part of the men, which is - too often fostered by • their Trade Unions, who know: that if industrial Partnerships become common, there would soon be an end of the power og the Unions. It is, how- ever,•quite possible that even the Trade Unions might be brought to fall into line with both masters and men wherever these -Industrial Partnerships are firmly established. It is not pretended that any plan of this kind could be adopted for casual workers or unskilled labour, and it is only feasible where there are trained artizans having permanent employment, and who are of a class able to appreciate its many financial and social advantages.—From "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette."</p>
      </div>
