      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>– • REV. ALFRED WELLS AND THE PUBLICAN.</head>
        <p>- • REV. ALFRED WELLS AND THE PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at the Runcorn Police Court before Messrs. A. Thomas (in the chair) .and .1. Pritchard. William Huskin, landlord of the "Rose and Shamrock," Mersey-street, was summoned for having permitted drunkenness on his licensed premises on March 31st.— Defendant pleaded not guilty and was defended by Mr. A. Browne, Warrington.—Thomas O'Neil was summoned for having been drunk on licensed premises.—Superin- tendent Ennion stated that on the date in question the Rev.A.Wells was in Mersey-street when he saw a drunken man go into the defendant's house. He watched for a few minutes, and as he did not come out, he then very rightly came and reported the matter at the police station. He (Superintendent Ennion) and P.C.Wilding along with Mr. Wells, went to the defendant's house, ajid in the bar six men were sitting. The wife of the licensee was standing behind the bar. He noticed that one of the men was drunk, and at the same time Mr. Wells said "this it the man." Mrs. Huskin said, "he is my brother. He has not been served here; he has come to see if he can lodge with us," The defendant then came in and witness drew his attention to the condi- tion of the man. He said, '1 haveordered him out.but he would not.go ; he has not been served here." irhe man was then ordered out and he went.</p>
        <p>'The Rev. Alfred Wells corroborated Superintendent Ennion's statement.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: Is it not a fact that a Vigilance Committee was formed in Runcorn, and a monition passed that all its members should assist the police? Witn,e3s: I haven't heard anything about it. Mr. Browne: Why did you I lay the sneak by running off to the police? Witness: I appeal to the banch whether it is the proper term to use to me. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne; Did you or did you not play Vie sneak? Super_intondent_Ennion : - Don't answer it.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: I think you kid better leave it to the magistrate. _</p>
        <p>Sir. Browne: I pass on; the public can judge. In reply to further questions witness said he did what he thought was right and would do the same again if necessary. __ _</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. Browne, SuperintendentEnnion saici ;here was no suggestion that the man was served with drink.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brown : Are you proud to posses § these allies ? Superintendent Ennion : No, sir ! I simply do my duty. Mr. Browne submitted there was no ease, and sympa- thised with the Superintendent in his unfortunate posi- tion. The man O'Neil was a brother-in-law to the defendant and had slept on his premises the night pre- vious. He had had no drink from him and must have obtained it elsewhere. Defendant did not know he was in the house until the police came in. ,</p>
        <p>After further evidence the Bench dismissed the case against llnakin, tho landlord, but tined O'Neil ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FA HAM ES.—Aecording to "Kemps, Mercantile Gazette," the New Bankruptcy Proceedings in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending .April 11th, was 90. The number in the corresponding week of last year was 85, showing an increase of s,being a net increase in 1903, to date, of 51.</p>
