      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, April 17, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, April 17, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</p>
        <p>FERGUS H U ME.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Mystery ot a Hansom Cab," "The Pagan's Cup," "The Crime of the Crystal," "Woman—the Sphinx." etc.. itt.c., etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHAPTER XIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
        <p>AT SALCOMBE</p>
        <p>Dora did not tell her mother why she was going to Salcombe, although she had to inform her of the journey. She knew, without Mr. Hannafora telling her, that the ex-housekeeper was a clever and unscrupulous woman; also that Mrs. Tre- mewan was given to babble. It might be that Mrs. Bedowen had left a soy behind her in filo Gray House, to report all tfiat was saifi and done. If Mrs. Tremewan were told of the intended visit she would be sure to chatter after her art- less fashion, and the information would certainly be carried to the ears of Mrs. Bedowen, who would then be on her guard. Dora. desired to take her by surprise, an for this reason, amongst others, she held her tongue.</p>
        <p>As yet the girl -could not believe that there was any connection between the death of her father and the visit of Mrs. Bedowen to the man at The Eight Bells, much less ttat the so-called Captain Max had anything to dollyith the crime. Mrs. Bedowen Wouid certainly not have urged him to commit an act which would result—as it had done—in her losing a good situation. Indeed, if she had known that Captain Max intended such a thing—presuming him to have so intended—she would have placed Tremewan on his guard Dora thought tut the matter in this way, and it was with no very great hopes Of learning anything definite that she went to Salcombe. Still the plight of Will was so dreadful that it was‘ries- sary to grasp even at this straw. Besides what Hannaford had said .relative to .3frs. Bedowen knowing the secrets of the late master of The Gray House was perfectly true. What the bond between her 'father .and the housekeeper had been, Dora 'could not guess. but there was some such bond • as was proved 'by a hundred incidents 'that now occurred to her memory. To Mrs. Bedovren her father undoubtedly trusted his secrets,. and it might be that amongst these could be found some reason for the enactment • of the tragedy. But the question w.,.be whether Mrs Bedowen -would speak out, and Clear up the mystery—al- ways provided that ihe was able to do so. Dora could not be sure on this point without having an interview, and thus she sought one as speedily as possible after her viSit to Will's father.</p>
        <p>- To excuse herself in a feasible manner, Dora stated to Mrs. Tremewan that she was going to get a breath of fresh air at Salcombe. The ex- cuse woks a feeble one:but she could think of none other. She might 'indeed have said she intended to visit a friend, but "in•that event Mrs. Tremewan in•her eagerness for -society might have proposed to come too. Therefore the girl made the above- mentioned excuse, WhiCh would have deceived no one but a fool like 'her mother. "Fresh air!" wailed Mrs. Tremewan, "as if you do not get enough of it here. I never hoard of such a thing, and me so lonely. You never think of your poor devoted mother, Who has done 90 much for you Dora. If I can't have the society of my own daughter whose society am I to have?" "I will stay if you -Wish it, mother," said Dora, knowing well that her offer would not be accepted by .the offended matron. _ _</p>
        <p>"No thank you, Dora. I am not so reduced for company that I have to beg for it on Tnv bonded. knees. I ask 'favours from no one, least of all frunt a thankless child; and besides, I have some letters to write. Paul Vane is coming •to pay a visit of condolence." _ _</p>
        <p>`.` Paul," cohoe I DarA., who had. no great love for her cousin, "I am sure. he need not give him- self the trouble. Mv poor father never liked him."\</p>
        <p>"Francis was so prejudiced:" moaned Mr. Tremewan, "he never would do justice to the many admiltible Qualities of Paul. A mo-A talented man."</p>
        <p>"Talented," repc.ated 'Dora with a bitter laugh, that effeminate, featherhead. He has enouvh to live on, and lie Simply plays with art... He calls himself an arti9t too. Such cles:cration. II be had been a man Milliner--"</p>
        <p>"Just like your father," said Ml's. Tremewan without waitin, for her to finish, "so rrejudieed. Well, if you do not like Paul I do, and I shall write to say we will receive him with pleasure." "I shall not," retoite-1 Dora. "hut as you please mother. He will talk of chiffons to you I have fl) doubt."</p>
        <p>"You' cruel girl." wept the widow. "Go to Saloon-the and leave me to my sorrow. Mrs. Gil- lard is coming to talk about my new dress." Dora shrugged her shoulders and went away very satisfied. She knew that Mrs. Tremewan would be quite happy with the Colonel's wife, and that the whole afternoon would be passed in turn- ing over materials, and in discussing bargains. The wonder was how Mrs. Gillard could manage to get away from her ferocious Colonel. How- ever, the main thing was that Mrs. Tremewan would be provided with amusement during the absence of her daughter. It was with an easy conscience that Dora went to Salcombe. Frivo- lous as her mother was, she did not wish to neg- lect her. Indeed, she did not look upon her as a parent, but as a shallow-natured child, who required to be humoumd. And meek Mrs. Gil- lard was the very person to do that. - _ _</p>
        <p>_ The drive on the coach_ to Salcombe was ex- tremely pleasant. The day was like spring, and there was a gentle wind blowing under a blue and sunny sky. Breathing the life-giving air of the moorlands and lookirnr down on the glorion. strach of country. Dora felt her spirits rise. She felt certain that Will would be saved, and that she would be the one to save him. All the wav she chatted to the driver, an amiable person, with a fund of information and an eye for a. pretty woman By the time the coach dropped down the road into Salcombe Dora was iu a very happy frame of mind in spite, of her troubles. The driver, with a tact which did him credit, never alluded to the murder of Mr. Tremewan, although he knew well enough the name of his passenger. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>At Salcombe Dora had a. cup of tia at ; con- venient baker's shop, and then issued forth to seek the cottage of Mrs. Bedowen. It was perched on a rocky promontory some little dis- tance away from the more crowded part of Sal- combe, and commanded 'a splendid view of the mouth of the harbour. A line of fir trees divided it from the road, and in front the ground shelved down to a series of black rocks, which lay pell- mell in the waters. On stol my days the sea round these jagged rocks boiled like a witch's cauldron But a stone wall protected the terraoe on which the cottage was built. and it was a sight which fascinated stongers to look down on the hell below when the winds bellowed up the harbour. But On this day the sea was quiet, and murmured gently amongst the rocks. Dora. knocked at the green door of the cottage, and after a few moments' delay it was opened by Mrs. Bedowen herself. When she saw her ,young mistress, she started back in astonishment, and liked up her fat hands. She looked well and harpy, and wonderfully young for her age. Her dress was the same as ever, and she wore spec- tacles, and held a stocking in her hands. Evi- dently she had been knitting when disturbed. "Whatever brings you are, Miss?" she de- manded in no very hospitable tone. "I have come to ask you a few quetions,' said Dora quietly. Mrs. Bedowen bit the top of one of her knitting needles and frown.</p>
        <p>"You refused my friendship when It 6frered she said at length; "therefore I don't see howl you can expect me to answer your questions." "If you do not choose to speak of course I can't« make you," said Dora," but as my father loft you enough to live on I think you should try to help( me to avenge his death." "I thought the law was going to do that Misa Dora," said the woman with an ugly glitter in: her eyes. "Isn't Mr. William Hannaford in prison?" "I intend to release him from prison." "How can you do that, miss." "J shall do it with your assistarce." _ _</p>
        <p>"With my assistance..." Mrs. Bedowen totgise4 her head. ; "How do you know that I will give it to you miss, and 'how can you tell that my as- sistance will be.wcLrtkanything wlleu you get it?" _</p>
        <p>Dora shrugged her shotilders-and turned aside to took down the bay. She saw a smart little steam yacht at anchor no very gr:at distance away. It had attracted her eye, because while Mrs. I3edo- 'nen- been aneak.hur she had looked not at,</p>
        <p>tmr. •t- ya,cnt DOI% thonghti ...citing of this at the moment. though .afterwardoi it recurred to her recollection But at preventi she merely looked away, as she did not like the 4 intent gaze of the ex-housekeeper. "If we aroi to talk Mrs. 8e:1°1,7m," said she, lot us it &amp;VIII. I can't discuss business here." Mrs. Becloweit hesit.tted, and half stood asidei that Dora might enter. Then she dragged: a chair out of the -passage at her back and set iti down beside her visitor on the verandah. "Sit down out here miss," she said; "for my part E will stand. I can't ask you into my cottage as I. do not look upon you as- my friend. Your own fault, Miss Tremewan," and she stood in the door- way with a mocking smile Oil her full lips. "As you please" answered Dora, seating- her- self. "I have no wish to force myself into your house But in the interests of the man who is in, gaol--" "The man you love," said Mrs. Beaowen, nodding and smiling at the blue sky. "I accept the correction—in the interests of the man I love, I wish to know why you paid: a visit to The Eight Bells."</p>
        <p>The smile disappeared from the housekeeper's lips, and she looked surprised. But although&lt; Dora watched keenly for any sign of fear, she stw none. Either Mrs. Bedowen had nothing to con- ceal or she was capable of concealing her feelings. in a most wonderful manner. "However did you, find that out Miss ?" she asked with a sharp gknc"Never you mind. You were there on the night my unhappy father was murdered. I am certain of the fact."</p>
        <p>"In that case there is no necessity for me to tell you anything." "I wish to know from your own lips if you were there," insisted Dora.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bedowen laughed scornfully. "You are not so certain as you pretend to be," she said. mockingly. "There is no reason that I should telt you of my movements as they c:an have no in- terest for you. However, as you think I was at The Eight Bells I may as well inform you thlit I don't even know of such a place." "Oh, yes you do," said Miss Trersevran ob- stinately; "you asked me just now how I came to find out. Those words show me • that you were at the place and that you did not expect iyour visit to be discovered." "Indeed," sneered the housekeeper, keeping a wonderful control of her temper. "You know all about it apparently Miss. Perhaps you will give me the name of the parson 'who said that I was there, so that I may bring a libel motion against him. The idea," cried Mrs. Bedowen with virtuous indignation, "the idea of saying that a reepectable woman like me was in a low publichouse at such a late hour too !" "Ha!" said Miss Tremewan. with a flash of triumph, "you said just now that you knew of no such place. Now it seems you are aware that it is a public-house. Moreover I said nz.rthing about the lateness of the hour." "You declared that I was there at night. As to my knowing of the place now I come to think of it I heard the name in connection with a mat- ter,of your father's."</p>
        <p>"What matter was that?" demanded Dora eagerly. "Nothing that can do the deld or the living any good," said Mrs. Bedowen sullenly. "You had better ask me no more questions Miss, for I decline to anwer."</p>
        <p>"You have not answered any /go far as I am aware," retorted Dora; "however, I will give you one piece of information Mrs. Bedowen. You went to see a, certain Captain Max. Ah, yon start ! You know the name."</p>
        <p>"Neither the name nor the man, Miss," replied the housekeeper with a stolid face; "someone has been imposing on you." "We will see if the someone will impose on the police." "Ho !" Mrs. Bedowen laughed in her most insolent manner, "if you think to frighten me, Miss, you were never more mistaken in your life. The polifye, indeed ! Perhaps you will give me in chorge for the murder." Dora rose and bit her lip. She saw that she had 'bungled the interview by showing her sus- picious too plainly. Undoubtedly ..Mrs. Bedowen was boo clever for her If she persisted in her denial' there was no means of forcing her to speak out, as there seemed to be no evidence 'against her 'plausible enough to justify her arrest on suspicion. "Will yo'i not tell me about this Captain Max ?" she asked beseectingly; "in- deed, I want to know. My father was good to</p>
        <p>- "Good !" burst out Mrs. Bedawen in a black fury. "He was a devil, and. all the good I had at his hands was very small indeed. But for me. -your father Miss Tremewan, would be-" She said no more, for she was suddenly pushed aside, and the doorway was filled by a small, slender man with a dark, handsome face. He flashed a look of anger at Mrs. Bedcwen, at which she winced. Then he addressed himself politely 'to the astonished Dora, who could not. understand -his sudden appearance, and the mastery which-- she guessed from the glance he possessed over Mrs. Bedowen. He was neatly dressed, and looked like a gentleman. The way in which he removed his Homburg hat. showed her that he was conversant with the manners of good society. At first she took him for a lodger of Mrs. Bedowen's, but he soon dispelled that illusion. "I beg your pardon," he said, smiling to show very white teeth under his slight black mous- tache, "but I think I heard you asking for Cap- tain Max. Allow me to present him to you."</p>
        <p>Dora was almost too astonished to speak, and looked at Mrs. Iledowen to see how she took being found out in a lie. The housekeeper, how- ever, did not glance at the airl.• She was staring with a frown at Captain Max, and it as she Who spoke first. "I am engaged with this lady," she said.</p>
        <p>"I am aware of the fact," replied the Captain pleasantly., "As I have been sitting inside the house all the time I could .hardly help nverhearing your conversation. You will -,yardon my doing SO," be added, turning to Dora, "but I hop.l my frank confession will excuse the offence."</p>
        <p>"As you plea.se," said Mrs. Beilowen; "I will have nothing to do with it," mid she made as to flounce inside. Max stoppei her, politely but firmly. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"t"ou had bettiT WO it. 1 think," he said. "Nfis Tremewan must be (Us:abased of the idea. that I or you killed her father."•</p>
        <p>"I never said that," remark:2d Dora sharply; and Max smiled.</p>
        <p>"Well ! Perliaps I am putting the case a trifle strongly, Miss Tremewan. But •eonfes!! now. You came over in the hope of finding out that I, a stranger in Ilartbritlg-.•, had something to do with the matt,r."</p>
        <p>"I did think so," responded • Miss Tremewar briefly.</p>
        <p>"And GU what assumption " said Max iiftinp his errebrows. - - -</p>
        <p>".1(ou axere at the Eight Bells on the night ms father was murdered." "I was. What then ?" "Mrs. Bedowen came to ee you."</p>
        <p>"She did. No, no Mrs. iieflowe-n," he ai,' quickly, as the housekeeper was ahortt to c;on. tradict his statement ; "why make srch a to dc about the matter. . You came to see me cn that matter connected with yOur late husband." .</p>
        <p>...... "I know I did," she said; thinking it Lfet ti assent• although she did not know Ithat her clever friend was driving at. "1 aline to tell you that he Is/a.s dead," rtirsin. Max with his eye on her, "and to say that with his dying breath he begged you to forgive hen for the way in which he had treated you." "Yes," said Mrs. Bedowen, casting up her eyes.</p>
        <p>"You must understand, Miss Tremewan," said the Captain, addressing us c:sivorsation to thf youn.Or lady, "that Mrs. liedowen had the misfortune, like many another estimable lady, tr. be married to a scamp. Tie was 'put in prison on a charge of forgery and escaped. I. met him in South America, and there be died—in a low brawl I believe. He told me his sad history, and how h:e had left his wife penniless. Also he acklei that a. kind gentleman called 'rremewan had taken pity on her and had made .14er his haasEtkeeßer He asked me to see his wife, and tell her that ha was sorry for his wicked behaviour. I under- took the commission and came here in my yacht. There she is," he added, pointing to the little vessel which had before attracted Dora's atten- tion. "I came here, as I say, and enquired for Mrs. Bedowen. I wrote a letter to her—" "It was Addressed in red ink," put in Mrs. I Bedowen, recollecting that Dora had handed her the same.</p>
        <p>"I remember," raid Miss Tremewan ;. then, after a pause, added, "but the writing was not that of a gentleman, nor even of an educated man."</p>
        <p>"You are right, Miss Tremewan," put in the ready Captain; "the letter was not actually written by me. I had hurt my hand, and my man Tom wrote it. He did his best, poor fellow, but he used red ink in default of black, and his writing I regret to 83Y. is oven to criticism. In</p>
        <p>re".:Zet i amen Mrs. Isectowen to let me oat and see her. She was unwilling that your father should hear again about her sad history, as he had had trouble enough about it, so she came to see me at the Eight Bells, where I was staying." "Yes Miss," said Mrs. BoWen, suddenly add- ing to this graceful fiction; "I had a lot of trouble with my husband, and when I became your poor father's housekeeper, he insisted that I should. try and forget him. I went to the Eight Bells secretly, I admit, Its I did not wish your father to know that I Was going to hear about my late husband. I also said just now that I had not been to the public-house and knew no one called Captain Max, because it is my own business, and none of yours."</p>
        <p>"You see, Miss Tremewan," said Max, sweetly; "our good friend here expresses herself roughly but plainly." "I express myself right enough," said the house- keeper sharply, "and now Miss that you know the truth, what have you to say ?" Dora was indeed puzzled to know what to say. The story was plausible enough, and the two had related it glibly. Yet she had a conviction that they were telling her a pack of lies. However, on the face of it, she had no option but bo assume belief, for she could see no way of challenging the statements. "I have nothing to say," she replied, rising to depart; "I have made a mis- take I see. All I ask is if you, Captain Max, knew my father?" He shook his; head. "I regret to say that I did not," said ; "but I should have liked to make his acquaintance and should have done so but for Mrs. Bedowen's foolish fears about her husband.." "You have no idea who killed him P.' "No. I heard of the death here. But as I knew nothing of him, or indeed of this place, until I met Mrs. Bedowen's husband in South America, I can hardly be expected to know who murdered Mr. Tremewan. I hear that a man is in prison now for-" "He is innocent," broke in Dora, and Mrs. Bedowen laughed tauntingly. "Let us hope so," said she; with a sneer. "Certainly," said Max, with a frown, "let us hope so, for the sake of Miss Tremewan here. I wish that she may be able to clear him, and that the marriage will take place before the year is Out. I wish I could help you, Miss Tremewan, but upon my honour lam unable to do so. I was at the Eight Bells on that night, but being a stranger to Dartbridge, I did not venture out." "And I," said the woman quickly, "was in bed at ten o'clock. No, Miss, try as you may, you _cannot fasten this crime on me." _</p>
        <p>"I did not accuse you, Mrs. Bedowen." "Not in word, but your every look accuseq.me."</p>
        <p>"I am sorry," said.' Dora with dignity; "I see That I have made a mistake in coming here. I -wish you good day Mrs. -Bedowen. Captain Max. I thank you for your explanation." "Only too delighted, murmured the. gallant Max, raising his hit; "permit me to escort you to the gate. Or, if you like," he added, "I will take you back to Dartbridge in my yacht I see that the tide is high."</p>
        <p>"Thank you," responded Dora, icily; "I prefer to go by coach, and alone. Good day ! ' and she passed rapidly out of the gate, while the Captain looked after her with a cmiet smile. "Why did you make me out a liar ?" cried the housekeeper. " Becau.se I heard you making a mess of it," he peplied, coolly; "as it is you have aroused the girl's suspicions. However, I hope that my story will convince her that she has found a mare's nest."</p>
        <p>"She does not believe one word of it," said Mrs. Bedowen, viciously ; and her uneasy look was reflected in the face of Captain Max.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV.</p>
        <p>PAUL VANE'S CLAIM.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bedowen's. surmise was correct, fat Dora did not believe one word of the story so cleverly and rapidly invented by Captain Max. She returned by water to Dartbridge, choosing a secluded part of the steamer, so as to think over what had been said. It was near the sunset hour, and the surrounding scenery was bathed in rainbow hues. The water was like glass, and the sole ripple which disturbed. its surface was that left by the steamer. Miss Tremewan absently watched the creaming wake, but all the time she thought of her late interview. Her perplexity was great. "I don't believe that explanation" she said to herself. "Mrs. Bedowen may have had a husband, and he may be dead for all I know. But it certainly was not on that account she called to see Captain Max. There was no reason why he should not have seen her at The Gray House,—unless indeed he had something which lie wished to conceal from my father. But whatever these two people know they will not tell me. I can do nothing. And Will— poor Will. What am Itodo ?" She looked up to the radiant sky seeking an answer, but she ,received none, and relapsed into a dejected frame of mind. That Captain Max and the housekeeper knew more than they chose to confess, she was ,certain, but she saw no means of compelling their confidence. Perhaps if she had accepted the friendship of Mrs. Bedowen the woman might have been more inclined to help her; but under the cir- cumstances, she would assuredly do nothing. Yet after all even had she been inclined to tell the truth— assuming that she knew it it might be that she could not do so.without in- criminating herself. The mystery was deeper than ever and Dora could see no means of getting Will out of his trouble. No help was to be obtained from those she had seen. It only remained to tell Mr. Hannaford of her bad fortune and to consult with him as to what was to be done. That same evening she went to see Mr. Hannaford but found that he had gone to London. He had left a note for her saying that he was consulting a famous criminal lawyer on his son's behalf, and would return in three or four days. With this Dora was forced to be content, although she could not help wishing that he had .remained until she had reported to him the result of her visit to Salcombe. Also she could not conceive what evidence Mr. Hannaford could have to lay before counsel, and without evidence of a tan- gible sort there was no chance of getting a reasonable opinion. However, the old gentle- man doubtless had some strong motive for his sudden departure. and all that Dora could do was to possess her soul in patience until such time as lie returned. Mrs. Tremewan did not question Dora about her Salcombe visit. She was taken up with her new dresses, and with plans for a trip abroad. Also she had received a letter from Paul Vane; informing her that be would come to Dartbridge to see her on important business. What the business was lie did not state, and Mrs. Tremewan was very much puzzled.</p>
        <p>"He and my poor dear dead -Francis never got on" she said to Dora, "so I do not under- stand that he can have anything of a business - nature to speak to .me about. Of course if he expects anything under the will, I must refer him to my solicitor. But he had no reason to think that Francis would leave him a legacy." "Paul is one of those persons who expect a great deal" said Dora. " It is usually those sort of persons who are disappointed" retorted the widow with un- usual shrewdness, "and if Paul thinks that I'll give him a penny he is 'Noery much mis- taken. I have barely enough to live on." Dora made no reply.. She noticed that since Mr. Tremewan's death her mother had become much fonder of money than she had been and had shown a capability . dealing with pecuniary matters which was ie *uprising as it was unexpected. Formerly the household expenses had been on a genergus scale, as Mrs. Bedowen was fond of good living and had re- ceived full permission from Tremewan to buy, as she thought fit. Now Mrs. Tremewan cur- tailed everything, and grumbled at the least expenditure. She got rid of Mrs. Bedowen and was furious when she learned about the annuity. However, since she was forced to pay this, she made up for it by scraping and screw- ing in other ways. Two other servants were dismissed, the horses were sold, and the food purchased was barely enough to keep the re- duced household alive. Mrs. Tremewan even went so far as to sell . the garden stuff, and made arrangements for the sale of fruit. Dora was amazed to find how clever her mother was in dealing with the situation. Formerly she had thought her a weak, giddy, frivolous woman, but now she altered her opinion. Mrs. Tremewan even grudged Dora money for dress. " I'm sure you have a larger wardrobe than you require ' she complained, "if you were fond of pretty things it would be efifferent, but anything pleases you. The cheapest things are what you like.' "Tn that case why grudge me a trifle of</p>
        <p>er:Jtiey r sasicen unra in</p>
        <p>ffrrs. Tremewan drew herself up with dig- nity "I grudge nothing Dora" she said; "any- thing you require you will have. But be careful." . "You are rather contradictory mother " retorted the girl with a dreary laugh. , It was useless to argue with Mrs. Tremewan, and the poor girl felt that she had quite enough worries without argument with her mother's never ending tongue. But it must be stated that with all this economy Mrs. Tremewan never denied herself anything she fancied. She re- presented that her nervous state was such that she must be humoured, and furthermore added that she was not responsible to anyone for what • she did with her income. "It has all been left to me" said Mrs. Tremewan. Dora said nothing. In a day or two Paul Vane arrived. On returning from a visit to the Gillards, Dora found him having tea with her mother. She had not expected his visit quite so soOn and was much surprised when a tall figure ad- vanced towards her with outstretched hands. r" My dear cousin" cried Mr. Vane "how de- lightful to see you again. How well you look. Really." Paul was a handsome man of thirty-five, slender and tall with a clean-shaven face and marvellously delicate complexion. He wore his fair hair rather long, and was particular about the whiteness of his hands. In a brown . velvet coat, a turn-down collar with a lavender scarf, and grey trousers, he made a sufficiently picturesque figure. His manner was languid, he was insolent when occasion offered, and he had a capability of talking about nothing with a wealth of adjectives. In, short Mr. Paul Vane of West Kensington, artist, was the sort of man whom other men take a delight, in kicking. His opinion of himself was " great " in the American sense of the word. Dora hated him, and contrasted his effeminate attire with Will's rough tweeds; his languid airs with her lover's breezy, manly manner. " De- testible wretch" thought Dora as she took a seat as far away from Mr. Paul Vane as she could. get. "I hope he won't be here long, and I hope mother has not asked him to stop in the house." Mrs. Tremewan had not. Such an invitation would have been against her new policy of economy. Much as she liked Paul's society, she was not prepared to board and lodge him for the sake of it.. "Paul is staying at the Nelson Hotel dear" she said to Dora, while Vane fluttered round his cousin with a cup of ,tea, "he is comfortable there."</p>
        <p>"As comfortable as I can be in these bar- barous wilds," smiled the languid Paul. "Dear Dora, let me give you some bread and butter. No? A trifle of cake then. No? Oh surely you love sweets, dear cousin." " I hate them " snapped Dora restlessly. "And please be sparing of your adjectives Paul. I don't like. compliments." "Can beauty be ever too highly complimen- ted" sighed Paul, sinking gracefully into a chair—the most. comfortable one in the room. "Dearest Mrs. Tremewan what do you say? I cannot call you aunt, for you are so young— so lovely, so exquisitely perfect."</p>
        <p>Mrs. 'tremew'an sniired and nodded and blushed. This was the sort of conversation she liked and appreciated. "As an artist Paul you are of course susceptible to the power of beauty" she said in a mincing tone. An- other cup of tea Paul ? " Vane wiped his lips with a lavender silk pocket-handkerchief and waved his hand grace- fully. "Thank you; I have had enough. There is much to speak of dear Mrs. Tremewan. And you, sweet cousin Dora ? " "What about me ? " she asked so sharply that he started.</p>
        <p>"How abrupt you are dear Dora," said he in an injured tone, "permit me to remind you that nerves "</p>
        <p>"Au !" sighed Mrs. Tremewan sympa- thetically " nerves ! " "I'm tired of hearing about nerves," said Dora rising, "and I don't see why you should have any Paul. You have no troubles."</p>
        <p>He winced. 'Man is born to trouble as the sparks fly upward," he remarked, sententiously. "I have my share of it. Not that my woes are to be compared to yours, dear cousin. I hear from dear Mrs.Tremewan that your friend, Mr. William-" _ _</p>
        <p>_ "He is more than my friend," sinterrupted Miss Tremewan, sharply; "I am engaged to marry him. Now, mother, it is no use your putting your handkerchief to your eyes. your know he is innocent." "I know nothing of tti* spri," said Mrs,. Tre- mewan acidly, "and I think it is very unkind of you to remind me of my darling husband, your most unhappy father. If Will did not kill him, who did ?' _</p>
        <p>"Ah !." sighed Vane, in his turn., with a. side glance at Dora; "who did ?" ." That is what I intend to find out," said Dora. quietly, "but it is no use our talking on that subject, Paul. What about yourself? Why have you come down here 1" Mrs. Tremewan drew herself up. "I rather think. Dora, that it is my business to ask that question of Paul." "I beg your pardon, mother; shall I leave you alone with him ? " "There is no need, dear cousin," said P4l in his softest tones. "Indeed, I should prefer that you should stay and hear what I have to say. It concerns you as well as dear Mrs. Tremewan." "How does it concern me ?" demanded the girl, sharply. "You shall hear," 'replied Vane, placing a chair; "allow Me. MTS. Tremewan, 1 hope. you are comfortable" "Thank you,. Paul, in my own houm I am always comfortable," said the widow. "Ah now you are angry," said Vane, seating himself; "that is wrong. Anger brings wrinkles, and wrinkles on your lively face--" • "Come to the point," interrupted Dora, wear3r of this rubbish; you can keep your compliments for another occasion." "Don't be rude, Dora," said Mrs. Tremewan, who liked the style of conversation. "Paul is talking most reasonably. But you must explain why you have come," she continued to Vane, with a fascitoiting smile. "Business !" '</p>
        <p>_ . "Yes, dear Mrs Tremewan," he answered sweetly, ." business. Painful business !" "Painful ?" Dora looked up; "have you found out who killed my father ?" Paul's delicate cheek grew white, Ind for the moment he could not speak, so abrupt had been the question. But he mercly shook his head, and Mrs. Tremewan gave a little shriek. "Dora, how can you," she sobbed; "my nerves." But Dora paid no attention. Her eyes were kill fastened on the disci-Apposed face of the visitor. "Painful business ?" she repeated, "and not about my father's death ? Yet it concerns me and my mother Well ?"</p>
        <p>Vane wiggled. He hated disagreeable things, and the business he had come about was very dis- agreeable. He wislied now that he hail left it to his solicitor. But as he had Commenced the matter, he was forced to go through with it. Looking down to avoid Dora's penetrating eye, he spoke without any of his usual airs and graces. "Five years ago," he said, "I found a man in the street one bitter winter night in December—" "When it generally is Winter," said Dora, deri- sively. "Are you about to tell us how you acted the Good Samaritan ?" . .</p>
        <p>"That amongst other things," said Vane, so slgnifica.ntly that she felt uneasy. "I took this man up to my rooms, and gave him food and drink. He soon revived, and told me his story. It seems he was a kind of connection of mine— by marriage,". added Vane, with a glance at Mrs. Tremewan.</p>
        <p>"Really—by marriage," she repeated, seeing that he expected her to. say something; "and why did he tell you ?" .</p>
        <p>V4ne looked a trifle disappointed at seeing her so iinmovcd. However, he gently Waved his hand, and continued his story. "You must permit me to. tell my tale in my own foolish way," he said, gracefully; "this man was ill, and I may say he thought himself dying, otherwise he certainly would not have to).d me *hat he eid. tell. He was not. a man given to telling his life's history to the world. I toc,k him by surprise, as it were, because he was ill; and it was chance that brought him across my path. I clammy if</p>
        <p>the meeting were written about in a novel no one would believe it to be true. However, the meeting really did take place, and a most extra,- ordinary meeting it was. That I, of all the mil- lions in London should have been chosen to succour this man, and to hear his &amp;tory---7 " What is all this about ?" interrupted Miss Tremewan ; "I don't understand." "Nor I," said her mother, with a calm smile;* so calm that Paul threw a vicious glance at her. "But it is very interesting." "I think you will find it so later on, dear Mr!, Tremewan," said Vane, in his most silky accents. "This Man staved with me for the night. and</p>
        <p>w't I, 'A five pound note in his pocket,— given by my most unworthy self—he took hisf•- leave. i have not seen him since. But before• he left me he swore me to secrecy as to his life! story. I now propose to jell that .story to you; and to my d.,,ar cousin." "Why should you break faith with the man said Dora, coldly. "I am not breaking faith now," responded Paul, with a bland smile; "he swore me to secrecy only during the life of my uncle Tre- mewan."</p>
        <p>The widow looked up, and for the first time a! tremor sh 4 ok her figure, an expression of auxietY crossed her face. 'What had my late husband to do with this man ?" she askel, and her hand closed n:•1-,'ous1y on the foltis of her dress. "I don't know if he had much to sio with him," replied Paul gently; "in fact, for your sake, dear Mrs. Tremewan, he kept out of the way of my uncle."</p>
        <p>" don't understand," gasped the widow, with an ashen face, "you are talking nonsense. There is 'nothing in my "Not even „your marriage with Walter Pill- age .?" said • 'Vane, gently. Dora s',-arted to her feet, and ran towArds her mother. But Mrs. Tremewan, who had alrehdy risen, waved her back. She was quite changed from the graceful, foolish woman of old times. Her face was drawn_and set, her lips were white and dry, and when she spoke her voice was broken and harsh `.` What do you know about Walter Pillage ? ". she demanded. "Only this, dear Mrs.—shall I say Tremewan ?" ." Why should you not ,give my mother her proper title ?" said Dora, sharply. "Why, indeed, should I not ?" replied Vane rising; "I shall go on with my • story when Mrs. _</p>
        <p>The widow gave a shriek, and threw herself on Paul. Seizing him by the lapels of his coat she shook him with nervous fury and force. "You liar !" she cried, wildy ; "why do you call me that ? My husband is dead—he was—"</p>
        <p>"Ah ! you acknowledge the marriage" said Paul, quite unmoved, and looking into her agitated face so near his own.</p>
        <p>-"Yes. No—that is—oh! great Heavens, what do you mean ? Speak, can't you ? Dora, your arm—oh, Dora, lam afraid—l am afraid," and she clung to her daughter, trembling like a wind- blown leaf.</p>
        <p>"What is it you say?" asked Dora, 'reserving her calmness. "Put it in as few words as possi- ble, and go." Vane bowed. "Certainly, dear cousin," he said, quite at his ease; "it is unpleasant for me to speak out, but necessity knows no law." "In Heaven's name, speak out 1" cried Mrs. Tremewan, stamping her foot. . "Briefly then," continued Vane, coolly; "you married Pillage in London before you became Mrs. Tremewan-"</p>
        <p>." I did !" cried the widow, recklessly; "what of that ? Walter Pillage died at sea. 'He was drowned in the Indian Ocean. After his death I married my second husband."</p>
        <p>"Wallet :Pillage did not die at sea," said Vane, slowly; "he was not drowned. He escaped in a boat to the Asian coast, and was for several years detained in captivity. He escaped to Eng- land' and then found that you had married my untle. He therefore held his tongue, and would have gone on doing so but for the chance that threw him in my way on that winter's night." "Was it Walter Pillage you rescued ?" asked. Dora, scarcely crediting,her sense At this strange dory.. "It was Water Pillage," assnt,d Vane, and Mrs. Tremewan gasped; "he was ery ill. Ha thought himself dying, else I am sure he would not have told me .his story. I need not tell vou how it came about that he made me his confi- dent-" "Because you rescued him?"</p>
        <p>"Partly on that account, and partly because he karnt that I was the next heir to the Tremewan Estates. He would have retra...ted his statement the next day, when he felt stronger. Irtimately he was forced to admit his identity as I threatened to see my uncle on the subject. However, at his earnest request I promised to say nothing until my uncle died. For five years I have kept that promise. No ewe knows the secret Now that my uncle is df.a.l I am relieved cf my oath, and I come," Paul lot.ked frout or,e to the other 14ith a bland smile, "to claim the property." "To claim the property," • echoed Mrs. Tr&amp; mewan looking aghast.. "Certainly. As Pillage is still alive—or was, five years ago, you, Mrs. Tremewan are his wife, and not the widow of my uncle I have wen a copy of the will. It leaves the estates "to my wife, Amy.' I need hardly state that such a wording deprives you of the property You are Mrs. Pillage, wife or widow Jf tia• man, I know not. But you certainly are not Mrs Tremewan —you never were." - -</p>
        <p>Mrs.Trernewan stared at the man in a bewildered manner. She did not quite compre- hend his meaning. The shock confused her reasoning powers. But Dora understood, and her active mind leaped to ihe true meaning of this revelation. The blood ebbed from her cheek. "And I ?" she asked in a low voice, scarcely able to speak. "Dear cousin t" said Paul, gently; "for you are my cousin, in spite of your illeg--" Mrs. Trernewan sprang to her feet. "No," she cried, shrilly. "TI won't 'nee it. I won't—" "Unfortunately, I nnot spe7k otherwise," replied Vans, with. hypocritical I..gret. "I have broken the painful truth as gently as I could. There is no need to make thin zi4 more disagree- able than they are. I am sure you -will agree with me on that point." "You are a villain," gasped the widow. "Because I claim my rights ?" "Because you did not speak of this v-hen my husband was alive."</p>
        <p>Paul shrugged his shoulders. "I should only have made trouble by doing so," said he; was just as well to allow my uncle to remain in his fool's paradise. Besides my promise tc--" "Promise," echoed Mrs. Tremewan, with scorn; •" as if you would have made it or kept ii unless it had suited your purpose. Oh, I quite understand. If any husband— for I still call, Mr. Tremewan my husband, in the absence of prool to the contrary—if my husband I say, had known of this, had he been assured of its truth, be would have so worded his will as to mike matters safe for me and my daughter. You—you delayed speaking until it was too late •' "And this," said Paul‘ raising his eyes, "is what I get for trying to do good. Of course your anger is pardonable. But when you are some. what calmer, Mrs.' Pillage—" "I won't be called by that name," cried the woman in a fury 7 "you have yet to prove what you say is true." "I shall prove it in due time." "Is Walter Pillage alive ?"</p>
        <p>"I can't say. But he was alive five years, ago. and I can prove that. As you have been married" —he sneered, "to Tremewan for over twenty years: you can see that--"</p>
        <p>Mr. Tremewan stared at him wildly, and slipped gently to the. floor. Paul calmly put on his hat.</p>
        <p>"Yes," said Dora. kneeling beside her mother's unconscious form, "you aan go now—you havet done your worst," and Paul Vane went.</p>
        <p>To be Continued</p>
