        <head>HOLYROOD PALACE, WHERE THE KING AND QUEEN WILL STAY.</head>
        <p>HOLYROOD PALACE, WHERE THE KING AND QUEEN WILL STAY.</p>
        <p>THE KING'S COMING SCOTTISH VISIT.</p>
        <p>The greatest satisfaction is being expressed 'throughout Scotland at the prospect of the 'visit of the King and Queen to Edinburgh alld Glasgow. The royal head-quarters will 'be Dalkeith Palace, which has been placed at their disposal by the Duke and Duchess of tuecleuch during their visit. It had origin- ally been intended that they should occupy the old royal Palace of Holyrood, but it was discovered that it was not in a sufficiently sanitary condition after so many years of emptiness. However, their Majesties will hold a Court there during their visit, and receive their ScAttish subjects who have not been "presented" in town. The Court will be a very brilliant function and, by the King's own wish, entirely Scotch. Holyrood itself 'Was originally an abbey founded for the Augustinian canons by David I. of Scotland iH" ri2B. The abbey was several times burnt by the English, and at the Reformation the Order was dissolved. The foundations of the Palace, apart from the Abbey, were laid in cot by James IV., who made Edinburgh the capital of Scotland; and from that date onwards it was the chief seat of the Scottish sovereigns. Mary, Queen of Scots, took up her residence at Holyrood upon her return from France in 1581 and five years later %tine the historic tragedy of David Rizzio. •=itie Palace ceased ,to be a permanent royal vesidence upon the accession of James VI. to the throne of England in 1603. However, in 1822 George IV. held his court</p>
        <p>SIR lAN HAMILTON'S NEW POSITION.</p>
        <p>The appointment of Sir lan Standish Mon- teith .Hamilton to be Quartermaster-General has given very general satisfaction. It is acknowledged all-round that one more fitted for the position would be hard to find in the ritish Army. Sir lan Hamilton's name was very prominent during the war in South Africa, where he was one of .Lor d Roberts's right- Land men in the advance upon Johannesburg. In Jan., 1901, he became Lord Roberts's Mili- tary Secretary, and at the end of the same year returned to So. Africa to act as Lord Kitchener's Chief of Staff. Earlier cam- paigns in which he saw fighting were those of '7;</p>
        <p>LIEUT.-GENERAL SIR lAN HAMILTON, The New Quartermaster-General</p>
        <p>served in the Soudan, Burma, Chitral, and Tirah campaigns. He has been decorated for every campaign in which he has taken part. At the conclusion of Peace in the Transvaal he returned to his duties at headquarters. He was born at Corfu a little over fifty years ago, and is the eldest ion of Colonel Christian Monteith Hamilton. In 1887 he married Six John Muir's eldest daughter. He was educated at. Cheam and Wellington College, and entered the army tin 1873. The pay attached to his new post is •••••-,100 a year. As Military Secretary he received 1,500. He is an author as well as a soldier, his books being "Icarus," "A Jaunt a Junk," "Fighting of the Future, and Ballad of Hadji."</p>
        <p>in Afghanistan, and in South Africa 12 years ago. He also</p>
        <p>SPRING CLEANING REVOLUTIONISED. t Xo longer need spring-cleaning have any -hertors for the housewife. An English engineer naB changed the old order of chaos into a (Lew one that no longer makes spring-time ue bugbear of marrie life that it used to 43e,L_• As a matter of fact, spring cleaning will ze abolished. In the future a person will 'ave his house cleaned once a month, or tcZlee a quarter, as may be necessary, and 'tzink nothing more about it than having the 'sweep in. The Vacuum Cleaner Company, Limited—for that is the right title of the tonlmercial institution which works this patent --cleans houses by air. An air pump worked</p>
        <p>rnE INDIARUBBER TUBING SEEN EXTENDING "OM THE WINDOWS TO THE CART CONVEYS ALL .r E DUST TO THE LATTEE BY MEANS OF SUCTION. In, a portable truck exhausts the air„ and `ne suction thus created draws out of the carpets, curtains, mattresses, etc., every part- tb icle of dust that may be lodged there. And Special charm of the operation is that .carpet need not be taken up, nor a cur- l'ain taken down. The work is quick and quiet, and, most important of all, no dust is :11sturbed. As may be seen by our illus- .tration the machine stops outside the house. from this machine runs indiarubber tubing 'llto the house, and at the end of this tithing is a long, nickel-plated nozzle, with an elongated mouth. The pump works in 11-le street, the nozzle is run gently over Carpet by a man. All the dust, dirt, mi- .:"2"3es, bacilli, pins, fluff, etc., are drawn hoes, the pipe into a receptable attached I_o the machine. The amount of dust that a'irks in the average carpet is amazing. Half b re_ -41ndredweight will be removed from a staurant or a hotel smoking-room carpet. Its • „ Majesty's Theatre, Haymarket, was clean tan turoughout by the Vacuum Cleaner Corn- not so long ago, and over four or'redweight of dust was removed from it. f". an average ten-roomed house the clean- in g can be easily done in a single day. It will and everything down to the chairs, sofas, til:u footstools. All the dust will be removed; nifre will be none left to re-settle after the `ll have taken their departure. There is a..n.other side to this dust destruction. Dust, fis well known, is one of the great sources of th:ction, and analysis of the dirt removed by - ' " some of the most he carpets and the he King and Queen been pleased to be )ut by the companyJ Lajesties that instal- - the royal palaces.</p>
        <p>-..,uurn shows that! Ilangerous bacilli lurk •in t: dark corners. of a house, Tl Alexandra have graciously tresent a; the work carried c ialpressed their m ---amus are now placed in al</p>
        <p>LONDON'S COMING MOTOR 'BUSES.</p>
        <p>The motor 'bus is not quite a stranger London as it is, though at present it take rather the form of waggonette than the steree .typed omnibus or tram type of vehicle. Hove ever, those already in use have given considet able satisfaction, and financially have proves a succes4 ; and it is now felt that it will no be long before the motor must form the prim cipal mode of conveyance for the public. Eaci month sees the automobile nearer perfection ie its various forms; and there 'is a strong feeling growing up in the trade that tht fortune of the future lies rather in the lest showy but more useful type of heavy vehicle than in the racing machine or even the touring car. One of the leading firms in thie line is American, the Fischer Motor Vehicle</p>
        <p>TYPE OF THE NEW MOTOR 'BUS TO RUN SHORTLY</p>
        <p>IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>Syndicate, which is building omnibuses for the London General Omnibus Company and the London Road Car Company; ariu now arrange- tents have been made for constructing 'buses and other vehicles under the Fischer systam in London. Several of their 'buses will now be on the streets very shortly, and will mark an era in London locomotion. Each 'bus, apart from accumulators, fuel, and water, will weigh under three tons, and look very much like the ordinary 'bus without horses. They will carry thirty people, and generate their pwn motive power. A hydro-carbon motor Irives the electric dynamo, and the power is :ransmitted by motors geared to the back heels. The London General Omnibus Com- pany is further busy experimenting with a motor tractor, with which they wish to replace :he present fore-carriage of their 'buses. Thus .hey hope to obviate the loss of all their pre- ent stock. Anyhow, it will not be long now )e.fore horseless omnibuses ,are a famiiiar sight</p>
