      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>GREAT SCHOOLMASTER.</head>
        <p>A GREAT SCHOOLMASTER.</p>
        <p>Canon G. C. Bell, who has, for oyer a ',aorta- of a century, filled the position of lead master of Marlborough College with tbility which, perhaps no one else has been or !ver will be able to exercise, is about to -ctire. His tame, unlike that of most school- rasters, was not :ontined to his .01°01, and very</p>
        <p>;eneral regret is !xpressed at</p>
        <p>his indication</p>
        <p>)1 his advancing</p>
        <p>nuch as present %tariburians de-</p>
        <p>ire his presence</p>
        <p>n the old role, )oth past and</p>
        <p>)resent Marlbu-</p>
        <p>7iwis are agreed</p>
        <p>hat to retire is</p>
        <p>lie wiser course.</p>
        <p>For Canon Bell las had a :trenuous life,</p>
        <p>ind now needs Ind merits rest.</p>
        <p>He was born in</p>
        <p>:832, and edu</p>
        <p>:ntel at Christ's !, tai, Lincoln College, and Worcester , Oxford, where he had a brilliant He was Fellow Lecturer and Tutor</p>
        <p>Worcester College from 1857 to 1865, in</p>
        <p>year he waTS appointed -second master .f Dulwich College. Three 'years later he .cepted the head-mastership of Christ's ' o ta I, and in 1876 he -became head-master N,arlbOrough College. The council of the ollege have placed their appreciation of Bell's services on record in the f01•,v,.-txg •,v,.-txg resolution. "In accepting the resig- at on of Mr. • Pell the Council of Marl- rrogfl desire to place on record</p>
        <p>'tieep regret at losing. -a. master who for ~..-nty-seven years has presided over the -e.,l:et.7e with the greatest devotion and the conspicuous success. By his work as !. teacher, his wisdom as a ruler, and the ,--I)pv relations he has maintained with the his colleagues, and pupils through- 't that long period, they feel that Mr. Bell y); not only carried on the high traditions ,1 the mastership, but has rendered services tof college and the community, the value which it is impossible to exaggerate."</p>
