      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NEW THAMES BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW THAMES BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The; new bridge at Kew is now nearing com- pletion. ; and, when finished, •it will form a, ‘.•;•ry important connecting link between MiddleSCX and Surrey. London is daily .-0-owing further and further out, tilk nowa- days Kew may be considered quite a partof it: and it will now serve as an additional point for the entrance of traffic, which is badly needed. The new bridge is of great carrying capacity, which for this purpose is a point of the utmost</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>KING EDWARD VII. BRIDGE, KEW:</p>
        <p>importance, as now a considerable share of the heavy traffic between Middlesex and Surrey will be able to make use of this new channel. The Thames has for many years been bridged at this point, but quite inade- quately for practical purposes. Moreover, the late structure would not compare with the new one architecturally, in which respect the latter has a great deal to commend it. The King has promised to open the new bridge in person; and Queen Alexandra will accompany him on his visit to KewdTHe whole place will naturally be en fete in hononr of their Majesties, as it is not often that such a visit is paid to Kew J The Kin,' has also consented to the new structure Wing named after himself, and it will be known as 14 King Edward the Seventh Bridge.' --•</p>
