      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>- -•- . . - -</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, April 17, 1903.</p>
        <p>up considerably. and Dickenson and Harris were nearly over. Warrington continued to have the best of the game despite the fact that they were playing against the wind, but no scoring took place, and the interval arrived with a clean sheet. Half-time—Warrington nil, Wigan nil. In the second half, with the wind behind them, it was generally anticipated that Warrington would put on a substantial score, but events turned out differently. The Wien forwards played up with great dash and gave their backs plenty of opportunities. The Warrington backs, on the other hand, failed to play up to their reputation and many chances were allowed to slip. A judicious kick across by Duckworth almost resulted in a score by Harris who missed grounding the ball by. inches when it was emir the Wigan line. Edmunds. made alutile drop at goal, the ball rolling dead. Shortly ; afterwards the backs got koino• but Isherwood proved. the weak link in the chin and failed to take the pass. I Erratic passing among the backs 'threw away many oppor. tunitios and the players' hands seemed too cold to hold the ball. By good forward play,Wigan at length reached the Warrington 25 and in the last minute of the match Bothwell surprised everybody by dropping a goal. Warrington made a desperate spurt from the kick-off ; but the whistle sounded for time with Wigan leading by a dropped goal (two points) to nil. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WARRINGTON AMBULANCE MEN ENTERTAINED. Hot-pot Supper and Smoker.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AMBULANCE MEN ENTERTAINED. Hot-pot Supper and Smoker.</p>
        <p>A pleasant evening was spent by the members of the Warrington CorAs of the St. John Ambulance Brigade on Tuesday, when they responded to an invitation of Captain George It. Crosfield to a hot-pet supper and smoker which were held at the Pelican Hotel, Butter- market-street. An excellent spread was provided by Mr. W. Chalmers, whose catering gave every satisfaction. The place of honour was occupied by the host, Cap- tain George R. Crosfield, and amongst those present were :--Oaptain Waring, Councillor T. J. Hulse, Dr. Adams, Dr. Smith, Superintendent Baldry, Mr. Hallard, Third Officer Taylor, Sergeant Banning and others. Letters of apology for non-attendance were read from Mr. Roger C. Parr, J.P., C.C., and the - Chief Con- stable (Mr. Luke Talbot.) After supper, several toasts were proposed and duly honoured. Mr. Hallard delighted the company with his humorous songs and recitations, and it was not surpris- ing that encores were demanded. Songs were also con- tributed by Mr. T. Humphreys' Third Officer Taylor, and Privates Hollingsworth and Pemberton. The accom- mniments were played with efficiency by Mr. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>THE TOASTS.</p>
        <p>CA.PTAINi CROSFIELD AND THE BELGA DE</p>
        <p>The loyal toasts were proposed from the chair, and were drunk with musical honours. Dr. ADAMS briefly submitted "The health of the Chief Commissioner, the Deputy Chief Commissioner, and the St. John Ambulance Brigade," the toasts being well received.</p>
        <p>captain WARING proposed "The health of the Host, Captain Crosfield." In doing so he said he had known Captain Crosfield for about seven years as a most en- thusiastic ambulance man, and a few years ago when the first corps had been fonned,his dear fatherhad pre- sided over their meeting. That was aboutisix years ago. Captain Crosfield, however, was a very busy man, but he still took a great interest in their work, and that was not the first time he had presided over a-meeting of ambulance men, although it was the first time he had presided at a meeting of the 'whole corps as a body, and he was sure they thanked him most heartily for serving them in that liberal way. He hoped they would have a smoking concert every year, and that Captain Crosfield would be among their guests. He thanked him ,most heartily, and he would ask them to drink the toast with musical honours.</p>
        <p>The toast was enthusiastically received and the com- pany sang" For he's a Jolly good fellow." Captain CROSTIELD, in responding, thanked them for the hearty way in which they had drunk the toast, and said he did not underrate the value of ambulance work, but he thought it was a great mistake to go into any- thing unless he could give his whole heart, soul and time to it,and as he felt that he could not give sufficient time to it, he had resigned. He was, however, only too glad to render what assistance he could to the corps because he felt that the work they were doing was a very splendid work indeed. (Hear, hear.) There was a great deal before them to do in the way of consolidating the corps and in general organization which they had not as yet acconiplished. Their officers were giving their attention ,to a scheme that was most important to Warrington which would materially increase their usefulness should a big accident happen in the town. He daresay they were, aware that on the occasion of a big railwaY accident at Preston a few years ago, the Preston corps, a very fine corps indeed, rendered inestimable service. Preston doctors had told him that their service had been of the greatest value, and saved an immense amount of suffer- ing if not of life. He felt sure that when their scheme was complete they would be able to render like service in Warrington should a big accident occur, although he hoped they would not be visited by such misfortune. , When the scheme was drawn up the corps would be, thoroughly exercised in it, and every man would lmow exactly what to do in the moment of emergency. There would be no rushing to and fro for missing stretchers or keys, but each would go straight to his place. That was a very important matter in which the Warringtem corps had to • perfect themselves. This summer Warrington would embrace all the corps in Lan- cashire. He was sure that this review would have very excellent fesults in that it would make Warrington people realize how far advanced that corps was getting and what was the position it had attained in the Brigade, and give them an interest in the strength and importance of the St. John Ambulance Brigade generally. Each one of them would do his best to make the review a success, and the otherambul- ance men who would come,would be sure of a really hearty .welcome in the borough of Warrington. At present they were doing very useful work in various ways, and Mr. Hulse told him that many accidents on the football field had been attended to by them, and he was sure the football authorities must be very glad to see them there. One of the most important features of ambulance work was the disciplinary training which it gave. It also provided the members with a definite purpose of a useful kind, which was absolutely disinter- ested and apart from their ordinary work. Captain Crosfield concluded by expressing the hope that they wonld respond to an invitation to a similar 'function next year. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE CORPS.</p>
        <p>Superintendent BALDRY, on being called upon to speak, said he thought the • corps in Warrington ,was making satisfactory progress in every way. - Their average attendance was satisfactory when they con- sidered everything, and would tally very well with that of other corps. Alluding to the coming review, he said the men were already t iking a keen interest in working for it,and he thought their work was well up to the standard. He was surprised to find that many of the ambulance men aftar their return from the front had not come back tc the corps, and of those who went out only six were now efficient members. He founil that the same thing had taken place in other places, rind he was at a loss to understand it. All present were effici- ent members, and he thought that when the review took place they would be pronounced as well. (Hear, hear.) Third Officer Ta.v.,oit observed that the Warrington Corps was not behind in•anything, and he hoped as time went on to see their members increased. (Applause). Sergeant BANNING described the work of the oorps as being both as noble and as disinterested as any work that could be undertaken. He expressed the hope that their numbers would be oonsiderably increased. Allud- irrg to the new ambulance classes, he said he was hoping that they would receive several recruits from these classes. Among them he had noticed many promising men. (Hear, hair). • Second Mae HULSE said he would like to sod the corps 100 strong—(hear, hear)—and he thought that by the quiet efforts that were being made they would have their 'wish. Classes were being hold at Latchford and Plidgate in coinection with their own corps, and a 'strong class was also being held in connection with Sir Peter Walker's brewery. They had received several promises of recruits from these classes, and he thought they might get what they wished for. Alluding to the review, he mentioned that all the Warrington officers were members of the Executive Committee, and he hoped the men would put their shoulders tb the wheel before the 20th June, and would give their loyal assist- ance, and that they would be able to show Lancashire what Warrington could do. He hoped that thorg whom they had sent to the front who still held aloor would rejoin them again. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>s „ Captain CitiisriELl) spoke of the great service ren- dered to the corps by the doctors of Warrington, and paid an eloquent tribute to their work. Proceeding, he said he hoped those who had gone to the front would not think, from a false standpoint,that they had nothing more to learn. That was a mistake, but he also hoped that they would give the corps the benefit. of their experience gained abroad. He submitted the toast " The Surgeon of the Warrington Ambulance Corps." Dr. ADAMS and Dr. SMITH responded, and a hearty vote of thanks to the artistes concluded the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>FALLING IN THE STREET.</head>
        <p>FALLING IN THE STREET.</p>
        <p>Tt is not pleasiuit to think it sudden spell of giddiness may make you tall in the street. But lots of people have to face this dangerous weakness. Mrs. W. Riley, of 32. Ingham-street, Bury, Lancashire, was one.</p>
        <p>Four years ago she bud acute kidney trouble, suffered great pain' and was, at times, nimble to stand erect. The most disconcerting feature of the whole trouble was giddiness, attacks of which caused the lady to fall in the streets several times.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Riley writes Wally she completely cored herself by taking Dodds Kidney Pill. Everv;symptom Mrs. Riley had was due to the kidneys, and were symptoms of kidney disease. The remedy, of course, was to cure the kidneys, and there is nothing so good for ill kidneys as Dodd's Kidney Pills. They have cured thousands everywhere, and are infallible for the many diseases due to the kidneys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BOYS lit It •II ED FOR TR EFT.—Two boys named George C. Coulson, and John William Coulson, who reside #t 9, Chapel-la ne, Stockton Heath, were charged with stealing a silver watch, of the value of 235., the property of John Livingstone, of 21, Chapel-lane.—The evidence showed that Mrs. Livingstone left the house to go to Warrington on Wedneeday afternoon, on April, Bth. She put the key of the door under the mat so that her children could get into the house on returning from school. She ret tuned at about five o'clock the same afternoon and half-an-hour later saw the chain and medal belonging to the sviitch, but the watch could not be found. Informa- tion was given to the police and the boys were appre- hended by Acting Sergeant Bowyer. In reply to the charge George Coulson said "Yea," and his brother said he did not steal the watch, but only took it to Warring- ton and got a boy to pawn it. It transpired that 'John Coulson got a boy named Edward Wallace, who is employed at Wilderspool brewery, to pawn the watch at Warrington for him and they got ss. on it at Mr. Domville's,. pawnbroker, Knutsford-road.—The magistrates ordered the elder defendant to receive nine strokes of the birch And the other boy six strokes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>RUNCORN v. BATLEY</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BATLEY</p>
        <p>1 The above teams met at Irwell-lame on, Monday after- noon before a poor gate. Runcorn played a representa- tive team Harrison—a second team lad—taking the ; ! place usually occupied by Houghton. Runcorn lost the : toss and commenced operations againstr sun and wind. . . A fine run by Richardson placed the visitors on the de- ; ! fensive where a score was nearly effected. The ball' was quickly sent back. Davies obtaining possession and ; beating Richardson and Harrison he slipped over at . the corner the same player landing a fine goal. On the 1 leather being set in motion Price handled and making i a brilliant run threw to Butterworth who touched down 1 between the posts. Harmer added the necessary points. ! Batley was kept on the run and after several near shaves ; Moran, after a bout of passing, beat man after man, and scored. Farmer again added the major points. Batley i ; pressed but a neat run by Richardson cleared the danger. , 51 1 The visitors kept up the pressure, Runcorn being pena- lized. „Davies. before the men got into position, kicked ! the leather to the bottom corner and following up , scored ii smart try. The same plaYer failed at goal ; i Both teams attacked in turn, Ruhcorn missing severe , 1 fine chances. Half time, Runcorn, ten points; Batle i eight points. Aided by the wind Runcorn forced the pace. Price marked, after desultory play, Farmer fail- ing at goal in an easy position. Richardson ' twice had hard lines in failing to get through. A fine kick by the same player found touch near the corner but Settle cleared the danger. A moment later JO/WS nearly did the trick, slipping into , touch when he was all but over. A mark gave the : visitors a chance but Davies kicked•wildly. At last 1 Jolley set the rear division to work, Butterworth dodging through. Farmer failed at goal. A minute , after Richardson got the ball and slipped between the ; sticks,Farmer easily placing a goal. Uninteresting play ' that followed livened up with a fine sprint by Auty who just failed to get in. Final—Runcorn, eighteen points; Batley, eight points. -—• • •</p>
        <p>- At Widnes—Widnes four. points, and Broughton Range's nil. At Huddersfield—Fluddersfield eight points, and Leigh two points. At Hunslet—Hunslet five points, and Hull Kingston Rovers nil. . . _</p>
