      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>At Oldham—Oldham five points, and Swinton two points. At Salford—Salford eleven points, and St. Helens three points. At Hull—Hull twenty-one points, and Brighouse Rangers two points.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. WIDNES The m6eting of these two 01(1 rivals on Saturday on the Wilderspool enclosure drew a crowd di about 7,000 spectators to witness the match. The teams turned out as follows:— Warrington.—Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dicken- son, Harris, three-quarters; Duckworth and Edmunds, half backs; Boardman, F. Swift, Morrison, Forster, Edmondson, Jolley, Harmer, Coleman, forwards. Widnes.—Tilley, back; Hughes, Rispen, Bown, I . Peacock,three-quarters ; Lallay and Taylor, half backs; WIGAN A v. WAR,RINGTON A. Aspey, Macdonald, Cochran, Riley, Webster, Power, ' Leyshon and Lynch, forwards. Played on Monday at Wigan, on wretched ground. A strong gale blew from goal to goal, and Widnes,who • The visitors commenced hostilities, a scrimmage being won the toss, took advantage of it. Dickenson kicked formed in the centre. The visitors had the advantage off, and Lalley failed to return. Penalties to both in the pack, but their backs were not very sure when it teams were to the advantage of Widnes, and passing be- , came to handling . the leather. Wigan now attacked, tween Laney, llispan, Bown, and Peacock was spoiled by. through Hartley, who made a grand run, and gave to a forward pass to the latter. Hughes dribbled splen- Rowlands, who knocked on. Wigan had the advantage didly past Dickenson and Fish, but Hallam saved. A in the back division, and Hartley placed his side in a somewhat long attack was brought to an end by a i good position by a strong run, but a free kick relieved minor. The drop out still found the Chemicals aggres- matters. A scrimmage was formed in the Wigan 25, sive, but a long kick again caused the ball to roll dead. Ibat Winstonley made a fine run and kick. The Wiganers A forward dribble by Boardman and Jolley made strong efforts to break through the Warrington followed, and Dickenson and Harris gained defence, which resulted in several fine attempts to score. still further ground. Hughes at. this point left the Healey gave his side a fine opportunity, but die ball field hurt, and Aspey took his place on the wing. A went dead before I3ayman could reach him. At half- spell of mid-field play followed until a- free kick by time there was no score. In the second half Warring- Bown gave Widnes another footing in the Warrington ton had the advantage and Watkins kicking across to the half. Edmunds, Duckworth, and Dickenson took the open, Ashton scored near the post. Knight placed the ball again to centre, but neither side up to now had, goal, the Warringtonians thus winning by five points shownrany exceptional brilliance in play. Aspey, from to nil. a free kick, sent the ball to the line, but Hallam ran out I with it, and passing to Dickenson and the latter to Harris they removed all danger. Boardman also caused a mild sensation by dodging a number of opponents but his pass to Fish was forward. The next minute Harris dashed away, and had reached the Widnes quarter-line before Tilley grounded him. The home team got still nearer the line, but a free kick again sent them back. Aspey made a strong run, but Hallam floored him only a yard from the line. The succeeding scrim- mage enabled Duckworth to kick in safer quarters. Isherwood also broke away finely, and passed to Morrison, and the Widnes quarter flag was reached. Laney, however, came away with a rush, and kicked across, Hallam • making a splendid save. Half-time arrived with a clean sheet.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION. WIDNES "A" V. WARRINGTON_" A."</p>
        <p>This match was played at Widnes on Saturday after- noon in the presence of a small gatheriug of spectators. A strong wind blew up the pound and there were fre- quent showers. The game was opened by the visitors, who had to play against the wind in the opening half. The homesters were not at full strength, but throughout the half they kept their opponents busy. There was no score although just before the interval Barber was held up on the line after a good effort. In the second half the play was still of a fairly open and brisk character and the homesters, despite the wind, continued to press strongly, though without success until three-quarter time. The visitors, who were rather the superior in the pack, also had many °handles, but neither side availed themselves of these opportunities, and the game ended without a score.</p>
        <p>Webster restarted, and with scrambling play the centre was reached. Good forward play by Widnes sent Warrington back, and Tilley, by a free kick, which was mulled by Dickenson,almost caused disaster to Warring- ton. A tree kick by Dickenson gave Warrington a footing in their opponents' half, and Edmunds made a pretty run. which gained still further ground. War- rington were being continually penalised, and this kept Widnes near the centre, and play was very scrambling. A charge down off Lalley's kick by Edmunds and good following up by Edmondson, however, again put War- rington on the aggressive and Harris made a strong run, Rispan and Peacock bringing him to the ground. A score at length came when Fish, kicking across to Harris, enabled the latter to ground the ball over the line. Fish kicked the goal.Warrington stillattacked,and a round of passing by the Warrington backs ended in Fish rounding all opposiliion and scoring at the corner. The same player failed at goal. Dickenson made one of his usual dodgy runs after the drop out, andalmost caused another dis- aster to Widnes. The result was :—Warrington 1 goal , 2 tries (8 points), Widnes nil.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE JUNIOR CUP. SEMI-FINAL. LATCH FORD RANGERS V. RUNCORN HORNETS.</p>
        <p>This match attracted over 1,000 spectators to the Wilderspool enclosure on Saturday morning. Playing with a.strong wind, the Rangers pressed continuously, and Brown scored an unconverted try. Faulty back play spoiled a favourn,ble opening, and at half-time the Rangers led by a try to nil. During the second half play was of an even description. Latchford again pressed in the last few minutes, and Bate was held out- side the line. Final :—Rangers, 1 try; Hornets nil.</p>
        <p>HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. RUNCORN. About 6,000 spectators saw the Rovers beat Runcorn by eleven points to five. Gordon, Barry, Reed, and Sin- clair scored for Hull, and Price and Butterworth for Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ST. HELENS v. HUNSLET.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>A close game at St. Helens resulted in a draw of five points each. Briers and Mercer scored for the home club, and Walsh and A. Goldthorpe for the visitors.</p>
        <p>BATLEY v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>A stiff struggle between these teams at Batley ended in a pointless draw.</p>
        <p>BRIGHOUSE v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>At Brighouse Beaks scored for the home team, who won by three points to nil.</p>
        <p>SALFORD v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Salford had the better of this game at Weaste, and won by a try to nothing. Griffiths scored.</p>
        <p>LEIGH v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>Played at Leigh, Swinton winning by eight points to four. Davies and Molyneux scored for Leigh, and Lewis, A. Valentine, and vans for the winners.</p>
        <p>HALIFAX V. HULL.</p>
        <p>About 9,000 people attended this match, at Halifax. Jones, Bartle and Little scored for the home club, who won by eight points to love.</p>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>On the Huddersfield ground the homestere won by six points to three. Sharrat ran in for Bradford, and Holroyd and Larard for Huddersfield,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>MONDAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>MONDAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>\VARRINGTON v. WIGAN. THE HOME TEAM DROP Two POINTS.</p>
        <p>There WM a crowd of something like 6,000 spectators, 2,000 of whom came from IVigan, on the Wilderspool Enclosure on Easter Monclity to witness the match between Warrington and Wigan. The weather was cold but fine, and a strong wind blew diagonally across the field. Warrington played the same team as did duty on Saturday, and Wigan was represented by the follow- ing : Mason, back ; Barr, Bothwell, Isherwood and Rouse, threequarter ; Anderson and Gleave, half-backs; Ball, Barton, Halliwell, Hatton, Beasley, Collier, H. Duffy and Ellison, forwards. Wigan won the toss, and Dickenson kicked off against the wind. After the usual exchanges,Hallam had his kick charged down, and Rouse kicked over the line, but before he could reach the ball it rolled dead. Another raid Was made by the Wiganers, and Barr three times kicked dead. A long spell of unin- teresting play followed, but still the Warrington backs got away and Fish almost got over he being pulled down a few yards from the line. The game now livened</p>
        <p>No More Backache</p>
        <p>The millennium here Fels Naptha has brought it, so far as selling and using it go. Men like it as well as women: the money- back soap.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Runaway Horse.</head>
        <p>A Runaway Horse.</p>
        <p>Excitement was caused in some of the main streets on Monday afternoon by a runaway horse. Mr. Fred Miller, a coachman in the employ of Mr. P. N. .A.very, Laurel Bank, Grappenhall, was driving a chestnut horse attached to a four-wheeled vehicle along Battersby-lane, when the horse became restive and bolted. Miller .tried to pull the affrighted animal up; but the near side rein broke and the horse dashed along Church-street, Buttermarket-street, Sankey-street, Parker-street, Wilson Patten-street, .and Arpley-road at a furious pace. On reaching Bridge Foot it came into contact with the iron railings and fell. P.C. Bennett; with the assistance of some men, succeeded , in getting the horse up, and it was taken to the Lower Seven Stars yard. In the meantime Miller had been , taken to the Infirmary in a cab, and although badly shaken, no bones were bi oken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Wheelwrights' and Smiths' Society.</head>
        <p>Wheelwrights' and Smiths' Society.</p>
        <p>During the Easter holidays the Warrington branch bf this society was visited by the Executive Council, and on Saturday evening the annual dinner was held at the White Bull, Horsemarket-street, Mr. Joseph W. Weaver presiding.—During the evening several speeches were delivered,the chairman stating that when they started their branch they had 10 members. That number had now increased to about 50, so that they had gone on fairly well, although there were still many outside he would like to see with them. He spoke of _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>the el3ellent work done by Brother Meacock, who, he said, was one of the hardest workers for the good of the, branch.—" Success to the Society" having been drunk, the general secretary (Mr. John Lamb )-spoke of the work, aims and-objects of the society. The mem- bership at the close of 1902 was 1,592. and the total income £3,100 14s. Old., of £3ll is. 5-!,d. more than the previous vear.. Out-of-work benefit amounting to £726 13s. had been paid, which was 3:271 15s. 6d. over the amount paid in 1901.—Mr. Fred Stott,secre- tary of the Trades and Labour Council, said this was just the time working men should keep close together and become more determined, so that it 'night be possible to get the existing condition of things altered. As trades unionists they wanted justice ; at the pre- sent time it was quite clear they were in a very unfair position in comparison with the employers.' In fact,it was illegal for working men to do that which it was perfectry legal for employers to do. They wished to be:put in the same position they were in before the recent law court decisions were arrived at.; that was that trades unions could not be sued as a body for 'acts of individual members.</p>
        <p>The Most Nutritious.</p>
      </div>
