      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <p>Mr. CHARLES MR. CHARLES BOOTH'S BOOTH has nearlY . GREAT WORK. brought to an end</p>
        <p>the formidable undertaking which he commenced more than tea years ago. He has described the social and reli- gious life of London in.a series of volumes ',which are a kind of library in themselves. To the exposition of the poverty of the great city snd its various grades lie has devoted four volumes. To classify the widely diverse " religious influences.' at work in the great city has occupied so large a number as seven volumes, the last of these being taken up by a recapitulation of what the other si% have set forth. There will be a final volum which will probably consist of a summary of results and a mass of suggestions. The last of the seven volumes dealing with " religious influences" will be almost generally read with a feeling approaching to despair. It pro- duces a sense of failure and futility. it conveys the impression that London in the bulk is iin per- vious to the influences of religion. Mr. BooTh does not ask us to take his own word merely, or that of a band of industrious investigators. He quotes authorities. There is, for instance, a rector of a working class parish who says that although there is a little atheism among the poor there is "no sense of sin." To have done something wrong means to have come within the policeman's grasP• The people call themselves Christians they are, nevertheless, generally indifferent t° religion, and this indiflerence extends to practic- ally eirery object beyond physical needs and enjoy- ment. That is surely a very remarkable picture-- a most crushing illustration of the evil effects of life in tenements and slums. "There has been no improvement in ten years," says one of Mr. BOOTH'S assistants, and this in spite of a consider- able multiplication of religions agencies, and au apparent increase of earnestness and solicitude. Along with the religious indifference of which we have just read, there may he great personal loyalty to this or that clergr man. There are those, we are told, who " would fight for their :parson against KENSIT," though "they do not care whether lie stands on his head or his heels before the altar. Vague beliefs er° held which, though they In Mit nothing in their lives, yet will cause men to side against the seed" larists for, as they say, attempting to take aWf'Y our--religion." A startling glimpse into sore hidden depths of ignorance which survive all kind° of teaching,secular or religious! Mr. Boom thin" that the High Churchmen, among the clergy °f the Establishment, have most influence on the lives of the poor.- The Evangelicals, 11(fr says, give "a worn-out presentment of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>'°°!Pel," and have a somewhat blindly self- eatlafied piety. The Broad Church attracts "neither the ardent few nor the careless many." The personal influence of certain clergymen counts for much, no doubt; but the National Church is - doing very little effective work among the London Poor, it would seem. "All branches of the Church ci England fail alike," says Mr. Booth. There are many clergymen who agree with him. "When Church does seem to get hold," says one, "it is by lavish expenditure." Another remarks that its influence is almost entirely founded on expec - • tations of personal benefit—doles of money victuals, clothes. .The survey is rather melancholy, wherever we diP a into Mr. Boom's pages. The Congrega- tlonalists, he tells us, are successful only in the ' uuddle-class neighbourhoods. "When, owing to acme change in the social character of a neighbour- hood, old supporters leave, the chapels fall into -disuse, and one by one are either closed or pass I.nto other hands." He nutes a lack of religious intensity even among ministers, though, of course, he is not speaking of ministers generally. There are the most noble exceptions. Among the lloPtists, we are told, there is persistent effort to maintain unity of doctrine. There is, also, a very real democratic feeling. The members take an nwe part in the services. Here is an instance 4-Pastor, after a severe illness, was away for; semslue months, but everything was kept going and Pulpit entirely filled from the congregati9n during his absence." Admitting the earnestness ,ancl devotion of the Wesleyans, Mr. BOOTH con- iictids that there is something unsatisfactory and tlf,real about Wesleyanism as a religious uuence, though he finds it difficult to rut letc words what he actually means. The rtimitive Methodists are declared by a Congre- gational minister to be "the only ones amongst who touch the poor at all." It might be Ituagined that something similar could be aaid of the Salvation Army. That is not Mr. toors's view, however. He says that as regards aPreading the Gospel in London in any broad measure, "the movement has altogether failed." !t Is a tremendous indictment of the churches,th is 'impassioned statement of fact as to what is being done by the churches among the L3nion poor Mr. BOOTH'S presentment of the case will be, and Indeed, is being, hotly controverted. We should 'most of us be pleased to learn that there has been some exaggeration—that failure is not written in :quite such large letters as Mr. BOOTH would lead us to believe. Whether that be so or not, the .?hurches will be compelled, by the reports of this investigation, to put searching questions to them- selves as to whether they are doing their duty in. 'quite the tight way.</p>
        <p>THAT Mr. WYNDHAM'S Land 'Rasa LAND Bill holds out no hope, and PURCHASE. indeed, contains no possi- bility, of a permanent 801U- ti" of the Irish land problem is, unhappily,proved ,11,7 What has just occurred it Belfast, where a `)rinvention of Ulster farmers and labourers has .tePudiated the proposals of the measure as they at Present stand, and has declared in favour of ea4ler terms of purchase than those which are given the Bill. This is what was to be ,expected. hen a bill, the object of which is the buying out th• landowners, is applauded by the landowners 1-47'1'elees it may be suspected of being greatlY cii lav3nrabie to the . seller than to the pur- -411 aser. In the case of Mr. WYNDHAM'S Bill, a state is supposed to step in and illake up the difference between an equit- able selling price and. the amount paid to the owners of the latter; but though the tate is proposing to provide £12,000,009 as a gift er a bribe, that willow:it does not, in fact,cover the Whole of toe margin between what the tenant ehould be required to pay and what the landlord te receive. Toe bill iixas the rate of Pnrehasa much too high. The tenant knows that .?he land is nt wOrrh what he will have to pay for I,yt• The Ashbourne Acts wete more favourable. at a Government which fixes the purchase price IA the land at from twenty-two to thirty-two Years' value in rent proposes to exercise compel- • Over the tenant—to compel him to buy 1,14 land, that is to say, at what he knows to an excessive price. It is .not proposed to 'eTeise any compulsion on the landlord. He :all sell or not, as he pleases; and, indeed, it Nattee Of public notoriety that certain landlords th° have lived on very unhappy terms with their .etlants do not intend to sell. The Ulster farmer Intlist that if there is compulsion on ons 'de; there shall be compulsion on the other ; and Litt Must be obvious even to the authors of the bill ,LQat the Irish land question cannot possibly be sattled by a measure Which leaves the best land °f the country in the heeds of its present owners. • BULL is required to be generous to no &amp;lee- .4l:cre Purpose. A sum of £12,000,000 is demanded to him as a means of pacifying Ireland. If the Pe,eification of the cluntry could he guaranteed the price would not be excessive. There is a deep '4esire among Eeglish people to effect a settlement 13f the Irish question at almost any cost. ut the proposal of Mr. WYNDHAM'S bill is that We shall pay the price without getting the settle- ,not. The tenants who purchase their farms will arequired to pay for them at such a rate as must Inevitably load to future discontent, and the "llers of the best land of the country will keep 'tile Whole land question open by refusing to sell. I,he tax-payer will have made his sacrifice in vain. '`.lo bill which is so much of a landowners'eneaanre that it fixes the minimum price et which the tflan t can buy his farm will do what Mr. WYNDHAM hopes to achieve. The Ulster farmers 14.°130u0ce the fixing of a minimum price in i bill 13.IIPPosed to be based on a voluntary principle as linflefensible and intolerable, and we agree with heal. Mr. WYNDHAM'S measure is unfairly eighed against the tenant and hi favour of the landlord. The Irish Secretary is probably con- is of this. His measure is not all that he 1/9°llld himself like it to be. He is a member of "Morris' Government, and must shape his, I),r°Pesals accordingly. The point is, however,' at any measure of Irish Is ad legislation which t° be accompanied by a large payment to the _s_ndowners out of the national Exchequer, 'must im.ord a clear prospect of the settlement of the rlah land question. The present measure settles tit °thing. The fact that it is rejects Iby the Ulster trners has the highest significance. It means beit.t the tenants of Ulster; at any rate, are, not to r°l'ead into buying their farms at a price in ex- of their value. If lin WYNDHAM'S bill to to e°rclielly accepted by the liish peop!is it must 1,31 considerably amended; and if it is not *ccepted it will only bring about the re- ,c`'nl4.tleacence of land agitation in new forms. The let Secretary may be assured of the assistance</p>
      </div>
