      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>‘ of the Liberal party in amending the measure. All the best feeling of the country bas been aroused to hope for a final settlement of the root-grievance of Ireland. If the bill should be insisted on in its present form that expectation will be disappointed. In spite of the millions exacted from the taxpayer, the Irish land question will remain.</p>
        <p>The Warrington traders have every reason to congratulate themselves upon the judgment of Mr. Justice Byrne in the action which was taken to enforce their claims against the Manchester Ship Canal Company with reference to the insuffi- cient dredging of the Mersey. If they had lost the action their ancient river rights at Warrington would have been of little value, for their carrying trade with Liverpool would have been conducted with the greatest difficulty,and probably brought to a standstill. Warrington's existence as a port may be said to have largely depended`upon the result. Had the Ship Canal Company been successful in their attempt to shuffle out of their obligations to maintain a dredged channel in the river, the traffic on the waterway at Warrington would have been confined to barges of small draught. In a short time there would not have been a sufficient depth of water to float the vessels which now navi- gate it, and when we state that one firm alone his ten vessels plying between Warrington and Liver- pool and doing a carrying trade of over 12,000 tons a month, the importance of a navigable river at Warrington will be readily understood.</p>
        <p>The judgment of Mr. Justice Byrne is clear and unmistakeable. He states explicitly that the Canal Company have failed to fulfil their obliga- tions and that the statutory channel of a minimum width of 50 feet and a depth of eight feet at low water of spring tides has newr been maintained. This is the burthen of the :complaint which War- rington has for years been pouring into the lid- sympatbetic ears of the Canal Company. The obligation of the company is certainly an onerous one, but at the time when the Ship Canals Bill was before Parliament the promoters agreed to the proposal of maintaining a dredged channel in Che Mersey at Warrington and pooh-poohed the .suggestion that the construction of the Ship Canal was likely to interfere with the scour of the river. Events have proved that the Ship Canal has seriously affected the river as a navigable waterway and it is now for the Ship Canal Company to fulfil the statutory obligations which they accepted in order to fa iilitate the passing of the Ship Canal Bill.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Charity Tent of the Indepen- dent Order of Rechabites appears to be making; encouraging headway, and last week the mem- bers were favoured with a visit from Bro. Duncan Kirkwood, of Belfast, the High Deputy Ruler of the Order. Few people, we think, will be disposed to belittle the advantages which accrue to the workingman, who by enrolling himself a member of a benefit society is able to make ample pro- vision for the almost inevitable "rainy day." The Order of Rechabites is, however, worthy of special commendation for the great help which it lends to the temperance cause. Total abstinence is one of the first conditions of membership, and year by year the organization fulfils a praiseworthy ob- ject in swelling the ranks of the army which is en- deavouring to stem the drink evil. Bro. Kirkwood is a very energetic worker on behalf of the Order and his earnest address will not readily be forgotten by those who were privileged to hear it. One point which he dwelt upon deserves special attention, He was able to assure his hearers that as far as he knew not a single friendly society or trade union branch in Belfast held its meetings at a public- house. This is a bright example which might with advantage be emulated in Warrington. As Bro. Kirkwood pat it, public houses are hardly the places for workingmen to meet in to save money. Happily the founders of the Order of Rechabites were determined that their society should not in any way be associated with public-houses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EDU CATION COMMITTEE. APPOINTMENT OF FOUNDATION MANAGERS.</head>
        <p>THE EDU CATION COMMITTEE. APPOINTMENT OF FOUNDATION MANAGERS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of tho Warrington Education Committee will be held on Monday evening next, in the Town Hall, commencing at six o'clock. The following recommendations of the School Management Committee will be submitted for confirm- ation:—</p>
        <p>That Mr. Alderman MONKS be appointed Chairman of this sub-committee for the year. . . . .</p>
        <p>That the Education Conunittee be asked to recommend the Council to appoint the following persons foundation managers of the respective schools.— Fairfield, N. Bennett, Councillor A. Market Gate, Taylor, S. 286, Manchester-road. Hamilton-street, N, Davies, Councillor 17, Welling- ton-street, Willcocks, Rev. E. J. Gramma'. School. Heathside, N. Smith, J, Fulton 20, Bold-street, Dodds, Councillor J. Penketh House, near Warrington, _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>INTER-WORKS COMPETITION.-DRAW FOR THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>April 17, Hadfield Bone Works v. Salt. Union Ltd.; April 18, Highfield Tannery v. Hazlehurst Works; April 20, Australian Alum Works v. Castner Kellner ; April 22, Ship Canal Engineers v. Runcorn Docks. RUNCORN TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The -following resolution was passed at a recent meet- ing of the Runcorn Trades and Labour Council:—" That this Council views with regret the failure of Sir Edward Clarke, K.C., and Mr. Llewellyn Williams to bring about a settlement of the dispute between the North Wales Quarrymen and their employer Lord Penrhyn; and urges his Majesty's Government to enter a protest against the conduct of Lord Penrhyn in thus refusing all overtures to a settlement either by conciliation or arbi- tration. Further, this Council desires to urge upon all similar institutions in Englandand Wales, the desirabil- ity of making a special appeal for financial help, not only to affiliated societies, but to all who desire freedom of conscience and liberty of combination."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>INTERESTING PRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>On Saturday Mr. John Williams, draughtsman at the United Alkali Company (Wiggs) Works, son of Mr. Williams iron founder, Widnes, was the recipient of a splendid overmantel, carvers, table knives, prongs, and dessert spoons, the gifts of the officials and workmen on the occasion of his marriage to Miss Derbyshire, of Cr9we. Mr. J. B. Houghton made the presentation. Mr. Williams will leave Runcorn shortly, having been ap- pointed draughtsman and engineer at Sullivan's works, Widnes. On Tuesday, Mr. Williams and Miss Derby- shire were married at the Parish Church. They were the recipients of many handsome presents. A WORTHLESS CHARACTER.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes'S'es'sions, OH 'Thursday, before Messrs. Timmis and Paton, John Townsend, a hardened loafer, living in Fisher-street, Widnes, was charged with neg- lecting his wife and two dhildren. The/evidence of Mr. Knowles, who prosecuted, and Sergeffnt Ratcliffe, Police-constable Cummin, and Inspectors Holder and Hackett tended to show the prisoner's utter worthless character. Frequent situations were found him, but he was too fond of drink to work, and consequently his home was one of starvation. He begged hard for another chance, and persuaded his wife not to give evidence against him, but the bench, however, took a different view of the case, add sentenced him to three months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>/ RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>/ RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>As will be seen from our columns the Urban Council h lye framed and fomarded the petition to the County Council for the absorption of Halton and Weston. A careful perusal of the scheme shows that a very strong case indeed has been made out why the two townships should join hands with their "business centre." There can be no question but Halton and Weston are depend- ent, more or less, on Runcorn, and it seems only fair that if they enjoy the privileges they should also share the responsibilities. Moreover, Runcorn appears willing to treat the townships in a particularly handsome manner respecting the rates. The majority of Rnncorn- ians favour the schemeland hope the petition will be successful. •</p>
        <p>The holidays passed off very quietly indeed in Runcorn the cold weather interfering considerably with merry- making. On Monday large numbers journeyed to Overton where Mrs. Parker-Hoose had provided attrac- tions for adults as well as juniors. A good number of cyclists crossed the bridge from Lancashire to Cheshire, but with this exception Runcorn streets wore a singu- larly deserted appearance. On Good Friday the usual services were held at the variolts places of worship and were largely attended.</p>
        <p>The New transporterseems to be making satisfactory progress. Already some of the iron work which will support the car has been erected and at the present time the bridge wears quite an interesting appearance. Many "locals" think that the bridge cannot be finished in time but there is really nothing to warrant such an asmmption. So far, matters, on the whole, have pro- gressed very favourably indeed.</p>
        <p>It will be seen that at the annual meeting of the Urban Council Mr. Taylor was unanimously re-elected to the position of chairman. Needless to say Mr. Taylor thoroughly deserves the honour of again being the first citizen of the town. In the past he has always striven to do his best for the Council and the people of Run- corn and in the future there is no doubt that he will strive quite as earnestly for the all round betterment of the town. Runcorn is fortunate in possessing such a gentleman as Mr. Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>On Monday Runcorn engaged Batley and vanquished them. The match was but poorly attended. 'lnterest, in fact, has d;ed out of the game—and almost out of the team—with the triumph of Halifax. There was nothing particularly brilliant in the contest. Runcorn were the superior team and thoroughly deserved the honours.</p>
