        <head>DEATH OF MR. W. PILKI4GTON, OF</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. W. PILKI4GTON, OF</p>
        <p>RUBY HALL.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce that. Mr. William Pilkingtory J.P., of Roby Hall, a partner in the well-known glass firm of Messrs. Pilkington Brothers, of St. Helens, died: athis residence, Roby Hall,.at ten minutes to four on Thursday morning. Mr. Pilkington, who was seventy- six years of age, had been seriously ill for about six ' weeks. He was the oldest magistrate in the St. Helens district, having occupied that position for nearly fifty year. He was the lord of the manor of the parish of Sutton, St. Helens, and was a generous supporter of the • • Sutton Church and Schools. He was born at Eceleston Exi-thesTow:e SKETen. PARTv. Hall an March 11th, 1827. In 1851 he married Miss This party gave a representation on Thimehy even- Elizabeth Lee Watson, the newly married couple taking big of the comedy " Adventures of a love letter."— There was a very fair audience, and the proc'esds are to urbandistrict. Heearly associated himself with eccles- up their residence at Sutton Grange, then a.pretty sub- be'devoted to the Earlestown Medical Aid Society, and iastical affairs. He was of a very generous disposition the local y.m.C.A. and not long -go gave a sum of £2,000 for a parish This CHURCH ARMY', EAItLEsTOWN. vicarage. Industrially and commercially, he took a On Wednesday evening a gathering was held in the great lead in local affairs, but his disposition was of a • Mission Hall, Earlestown, to welcome Nurse Brown, of somewhat quiet turn, and, therefore, he did not relish : the Church Amy, who has come from Gateshead to fill in an active way the turnmoil and strife of politics. He the vacancy caused by the transfer of Nurse Irwin. was, however„ one of the best known and most highly i There were about -100 people present, and the nurse had esteemed members of the Conservative Club, his son, an enthusiastic reception. She gave an excellent ad- Lieutenant-Colonel W. Len Pilkington, being now pre- dress and created a . good impression. The Rev. T. R sident. The deceased gentleman leaves four sonsand Matthews also gave a brief address. , three daughters. • • • GAMBLING AT B meroxwooo. The funeral will take place on Monday morning next, I At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wed- at Roby Church. The service will commence at eleven nesday, Joseph Matthews was fined 7s, (id. for plying o'clock, and on its conclusion the cortege will proceed at pitch and toss at Burtonwood on the , 4th mat,— to St. Nicholas's Church, Sutton, where the interment Richard Welding. William , Duxbury Edward Robson- _ . will Lake place. , John Donnolly, James Darbyshire and Ashton Gutt- Mr. Pilkington's deAth created the greatbst sorrow ' ridge were charged with a similar off ence,also at Burton throughout St. Helens and district, and the flags at the i wood, the first three being fined 7s, 6d. each and the works, the Town Hall, the Gunble Institute, and the ' last three ss. each. Conservative Club were placed at half-mast. The de- I P.S.A. ceased gentleman was a native of St. Helens, and took, !, The Earlestown and District P.S.A. held their usual for a long number of years, a- very active part in the I weekly meeting in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, conduct of the glassworks, and many pleasing incidents lon Sunday afternoon. There was a very fair attendance are related by the older workpeople illustrating his I over which Mr. Singleton presided. -.qr. Jackson gave happy and generous disposition and his sympathy for lan excellent address on the Crucifixion. Mrs. Thomas those under hint. I rendered several vocal items and Mr. Morgan contri- 411e I buted. recitations. Miss Williemson accompanied at Crime in Lancashire. . I the piano and Mr. E. Smith presided at the organ. TIIErr OF Marrs AT N•tw!ToN. . .. On Thursday Colonel Moorsom, Chief Con U. the Warrington lot 1 i a A rrtngton County Petty Sessions on ednes- Lancashire, 4sued his repori on crime in the comity I day, George Anderson, of Newton, was charged with for the quarter ended the 31st ult. It states that the 1 p having on the Bth inst. at Newton stolen a pair of. apprehensions have been,2,579, or 33 less than in the ' 1 men's boots, of the value of ss, 11d., the property of preceding quarter, the decrease being in simple Thomas Powell, 19,Birch-street, Newton.—Prisoner larceny, and 107 more than iii the corresponding i lodged with the prosecutor, and on the boots being qnness. i ntssed,information was given to the police.—Prisoner uarter of 1904, the increase being in drunke The sulnmonses by the police number 3,266, 01'7.10 Nis arrested a house in Athol-street., and was found mbe wearing the boots. A fine of 155.. with the option less than in the preceding quarter, and 699 less than of 14 days' iinprisonment, was imposed.' in the corresponding quarter of 1902, the decrea:e EasTut AT EinLEsTowN. being chiefly in drunkenness and highway offences. The weatherat Earlestown and Newton was not at all The summonses bv parish authorities and- others have of a holiday character. Hail, raid, and snow, was the been 6,579, or 1,269 more than in the preceding quarter, order all through and anything like outdoor amusements and 43 more than in the corresponding quarter of Was out of the question. There was a large exodus of people but only to places within easy reach, such as 1992, the increase being in summonses for rates andl Liverpool, Manchester, Blackpool &amp;c. and the station married women's maintenance orders. -,• . Presented a very busy appea ranee on Monday night when the holiday makers returned home. In the town there was no at-traction of any kind and people had to travel 'afield to seek recreation. The Viaduct Works and Vulcan Foundry resumed work on Tuesday morning but Messrs. Vicar's and Glee's men were not satisfied and extended the holiday over Tuesday. EARLEsTOWN AVEsi.EY.‘N BIBLE CLASs. On Monday the amnial pic-nic in connection with the above class took place, to Windermere. The party, numbering 32, left Earlestown at 5.55 a.m., during a snowstorm, which was not calculated to raise their spirits. They reached Windermere about 10.40, the elements then being all that could be wished. They walked to Bowness and had dinnerat Reid's. After dis- posing of the meal, they rowed on the lake, and passed the time in various pleasant ways. Tea was partaken of at Reid's, and at 6.10 p.m. they entrained for home, reaching Earlestown at 10.30. The outing was thoroughly enjoyed, like arrangements, which were in the hands of Mr. E. Goslin, were in every way satisfactory. ' POLICE (las Es. At the Newton Occasional Court on Saturday before Mr. T. J. Gillespie,' Tivina4 Walih, collier, of Newton-street, Parr was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Houghton-street on Friday night and was fined ss. and costs. Thomas Brown and Samuel Green, tramps, of no fixed abode, were charged with being drank and disorderly in Earle-street, Earlestown, on Friday. Fined 2s. (id. and costs each. or seven days im- prisonment in default. George Thome; Deane of Earle- street was also charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday' he was fined ss. and costs. Committed 14 days. At the Enrlestown Occasional Court on Monday, , Michml McDermott of Birch-street, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the list lust, and was fined 2s. 6d. and costs.</p>
        <p>KING Env MID'S Formai.—E ven kings are not e eiapt from the foibles of ordinary mankind, and here is an interestingsidelight on a little weakness of our own good King, Edward VII. A certain Royal lady was at an afternoon ti-party, and one of tie guests told a de- cidedly amusing little story. " oh. "cried the lady in question, clapping her hands," that is capital. I Must tell it to the King. No, "she decided after a moment's reflection, " I won't, for if one tells the King a good story he forgets in a day Qr two that it was told to him. and goes about repeating it to everyone as his own."— M.A.P.</p>
        <p>NEW BISCUIT.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>SWEET. " r " SHORT. RICH.</p>
