      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>THEFT FROM A WARRINGTON NEWSPAPER OFFICE.-</p>
        <p>BOY IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Wednesday,before Mr. Birch%ll (presiding) and other magistrates, Alfred Fay, a small boy aged nine years, living at 11, Pinner's Brow, was charged with stealing from the office of the "Evening Chronicle" in Quetn-street on Tuesday after- noon, a brown leather bag containing £1 4s. 11d., the money of John William Taylor.—He pleaded guilty.— The Chief Constable stated that the aefendant was a remarkably precocious boy, and one. of the most adroit young thieves and liars he had met for some time. He was one of a gang of bandits, who sold evening papers. Mr. Taylor, the manager of the office, placed the bag containing the money in the till behind the counter at 4.30, and shortly afterwards had occasion to leave the. shop for a few minutes. When he came back the bag and money were missing. Mr. Taylor circulated the news of the loss among the other newsboys and a wit- ness named Btrker, 16 years of age, who was in the em- ploy of Mr. Taylor, went to Scotland-road where he saw the defendant. He brought him back to the office, and the police were sent for. The money was found on him. He was taken to the Police Station and he (the Chief Constable) examined him,. He slipped through one chain of lies after another; and, in fact, he (Mr. Talbot) never heard anyone lie so glibly and with the same facility of imagination and invention. It appeared that the boy was practically running wild and it was in his own interests that he (the Chief Constable) brought- him before the Court —The mother of the boy said he had pilfered for a year or two.—The Chairman said the lad wouldreceive six strokes of the birch rod, and he hoped the puniehment would be a reminder to him. He would be kept in the Police Stationuntilsix o'clock. If brought before the Bench again he would be sent to a reformatory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Minor Cam. Warrington Borough Pan Court.</head>
        <p>Minor Cam. Warrington Borough Pan Court.</p>
        <p>SA:rulto.kY.—Befor3 Birchall (in the chair), Alderman, Monks, Dr. Fox, and Mr. R. T. Fairclough. . CONSTABLE ASSA ULT ED.—Willi= Coyne was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Barbauld-streat at 12.20 the previous day and with assaulting P.C. Cooper.—Tne officer said the pHsoner was exceedingly drunk and made use of very filthy language in Barbauld- street. Witness took him into custody and when they reached St. Austin's-lane prisoner became very violent and struck witness in the face and kicked him on the hand. They struggled on the ground and ultimately P.C. Dyson. canto upon the scene. They had to carry the prisoner part of the way to the Police Station and the handcuffs had to be used.—The Chief Constable said prisoner had been before the Court 26 times and was an habitual thief and loafer.—He was sentenced to a month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>HF.Tt 60Tit APPEARANCE.—EIizabeth Ellen Fleming, a well-known character, was charged with stealing from the person of George Cummings in Dial-street at 11.20 p.m. on Thursday the'sum of 12s. 7d. She pleaded guilty —lt appeared front the Chief Constable's statement that prosecutor met the prisoner as he was returning home and walked along Dial-street with her. Suddenly she th rust her hand into one of his trousers pockets and after taking 12s. 7d., made off. She was arrested in Jolley's Court. This was prisoner's 60th appearance.—The Chief ConstAble stid that efforts had been made to reclaim the prisoner, but without success, owing to the evil influ- ence exercised over her bya man with whom she resided rhe police intended to make it very hot for the man— Prisoner was sentenced to three months hard labour.</p>
        <p>MONDA V.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair). Alder- man Monks, Dr. Fox, and Messrs. Birchall and Thorn-</p>
        <p>ton. THEFT FROM THE MARK !Tr.—Elizabeth Holbrook, a married woman, residing in Watkin-street, was charged with stealing articles of clothing of the value of Bs. from the st.ll of Jaws Rothwell in the Market-place at 4 o'clock on Saturday afternoon.—The Chief Con- stable said that Mr. Thomas Eiward Lee, a well-known draper carryine on business in the Market-place, saw the prisoner loitering about his stall in a very suspicions manner on Saturday morning. He cilled the attention of Inspector Place to her, and, evidently noticing that she was tinier observation, prisoner =debar exit from the market. She returned in the afternoon and Mr. Lee saw her loitering about Mr. Rothwell's stall. He watched her very closely and when the prose2utor's back was turned he saw the prisoner take some articles of clothing from the stall, slip them under her shawl, and make off. Mr. Lee followed her into Queen-street and accused her of having stolen the goods. She became abusive and foul-mouthed but later seemed to be seized with panic and dropped the bundle of clothing and ran away. Mr. Lee followedher to her residence in Watkin- street and subsequently gave information to Superin- tendent Monaghan. Prisoner was arrested at her house and when charged admitted the theft.—Market thefts had recently become very frequent.—The magistrates im- posed a fine of 10s. and costs (el in all) with the option of 14 days imprisonment.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY—Before Mr. llirchall (in the chair), Alder- man Monks and Mr. Geddes. ALLEGED ROBBERY IN LOVELY-LiNE.—John Evans, last from Wigan, was charged with stealing from a house in course of erection, in Lovely-lane, between the 9th and 14th April, a number of joiners' tools, of the value of 40s.—The Chief Constable stated that on Thursday night the house, which was almost completed, was used as a. storehouse for the tools of the joiners who were working upon some other premises. The place was left secure, on going_to work on Thursday night. and on Friday evening some of the workmen returned to work. When they had finished they again locked the place up, and on Tuesday morn- ing they found the house had been entered by bne of the windows, whlch had been reached by a ladder, brought from another house close by. A large number erf tools were missing including some valuable handsaws Information was given to the police and the descrip- tion of the articles circulated, with the result that it was ascertained that the prisoner had been detained at Wigan where he had been endeavouring to pledge some hindsaws.—Prisoner was remanded until to-day (Satur- (l7.74 •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EMPIRE'S GRANARY.</head>
        <p>THE EMPIRE'S GRANARY.</p>
        <p>[B Mit. H. WHlTilksi].</p>
        <p>, The imminence of war has, during recent months, caused much anxiety to British statesmen. Meetings have been held, and attention has been called to the precarious condition of the food supply. It has been pointed out that the food supply on hand would,in the event of war, be consumed in a very short time, and this feature, added to the increased cost, would, if nothing else happened to do it, bring about a speedy collapse. I .suppose it may be said with truth that Great Britain is never at peace. There are always a few little wars going on in some corner of her dominions, but these give no anxiety, they are scarcely known of, it is always the possibilly of war with those nations that take greatest pleasure in add- ing to the number of those monstroiities of the deep, the m )dern iron-clads. And yet the experiences of the past year or two almost seem to justify us in allowing the thought to develop that however much the taxpayer may dread the having to find the money for these costly wars, the war minister has still a greater dread in undertaking the managemeat of the conflict so much talked of, and which in the judgment of rnost.thoughtful men, would be the eclipse of our civilization.</p>
        <p>Again, war or no war. the food question is one of serious importance. It has brought Ireland to, what seems, just at the moment, within measurable distance of a settlement of the land question. If ;the owner will not behave in a reasonable and sensible way with his land, he must stand on one side and let someone else see what can be done. It is the same .thing in Great Britain, the private, or individual ownership of lands without the recognition of the nation's needs, or the ~:necessity caused by private needs, causes the unproductiveness of the land as a whole. Hence the immensity of the figures we see every little while, spent in importing food-stuffs from other lands. The same features are to be seen very often in the United States, but the immensity of the country in comparison to its population partially obscure the facts. Some little time ago the Colonies met through their representatives, in London, their object being to devise means whereby the Colonies, and the Mother Country could be drawn nearer together. Little or nothing came of the movement, because, although there were brainy men anxious and willing t) do something in the way of Imperialism they all had their pet, though unworkable theories. Could these men have had an object lesson given to them, and a st;:aight question put ,to them, the scales would doubtless have fallen from their eyes, and they would with one voice have cried out " Eureka here is all We want ! "</p>
        <p>American millers are agreed that right through Manitoba wing west through the wilderness that was deemed fit for nothing, but for hunting and enriching the Hudson Bay Company lies a land big enough and rich enough in soil to make an empire's granary. Land that will grow rye from seven to eight feet in height would soon enable a population to export cargoes double those of the American shipments to-day. In 1902 the wilderness referred to above produced 75,000,000 bushels of the finest wheat in the world. At the pre- sent rate of American immigration into Canada,there. will in ten years be farmers enough to produce 250,000,000 bushels of wheat. But a small portion of this will be required for home consumption. In Manitoba and the North-West Territories 260,000,009 acres of arable land to-day await the plough. As pioneers in the taking up of all this land it is calculated that there are on the spot 70,000 people of American extraction. Surely something could be done in the way of pe )pling these lands if in the large towns of Great Britain a few men of affairs could be prevailed to set their hands to the task. When we think of the immense sums of money spent in South Africa during the war, and to be spent now on these peace terms, when we als ) think of the price to be paid to the Irish landlords for the land they no more made than the air they breathe or the water they sometimes drink, we may, surely think it possible to find the cast of the first binitiatory steps in selecting and .placing families on these lands.</p>
        <p>Tlie American immigrant does not by any means go out there because of -dissatisfaction with their own country. He is before all a speculator. He can sell his own farm in the States for from 30 dols. to 40 dols. an acre in many cases, and can buy as good land under the British flag for from 7 dols. to 10 dols. an acre, starting afresh under favourable circumstances with ready cash in hand. A. certain prop3rtion of each near settlement is of crown lands on which "the homesteader" may secure an allotment on the pay- ment of a vominal fee of 10 d )Is. for 160 acres of land, and after a residence of three years, in compliance with homestead regulations, receive an absolute title. Or he may purchase lands from the larger grants owned by the Canadian Pacific, and Canadian Northern railroads or buy a farm from one of the many land companies, chiefly American which have recently acquired large tracts of land from the railroad company. The total land sales during the summer Of 1902 were: fivefold those Of 19-)l,lwith prices steadily rising. Land selling at 12s. an acre five years ago now brings in treble that price. American syndicates are forming to buy large tracts for bonanza farming, that is for farming on a larger scale. These new farmers are getting twenty bushels of wheat an acre, They attribute this to the line soil and the long solar light of the northern summer day. It is not to be thought that wheat growing.is the only occupation of the farmer. Dairying, ranching and cereals give variety enowth to the farmers life.</p>
        <p>I have said 'enough to skow that the " we-hare-got- no-work-tl-d)" queitiou has a practicAl remedy, and not only a remedy, but Canada can promise the s ilu- tion to the food question if Great Britain, and in fact the British Empire will only " Wake up." H. WHITHA.M, , Ifarch 30th, 1993.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>BRAVE MAN LEARNS FEAR. 1</head>
        <p>BRAVE MAN LEARNS FEAR. 1</p>
        <p>Terrible Experience on a Chimney.</p>
        <p>"I have carried out some daring jobs. It was on the Leicester Waterworks chimney, that T first knew what fear was."</p>
