rs be in a position to supply all field £2 us. 6d. or one month, and issued a warrant for the arrest of Buckley. these cars ?—" Motoring Illustrated." i</p>
        <p>So spoke Alfred Garner, of 57 Borthwick-road, Hendon, an expert builder. "As you know," he went on, "scaffolding is erected inside and out of such a shaft, and the bricks are laid from within. When I looked from the top and saw Leicester ' below me, I grew nervous and dizzy, and had tf - I. was ill. The giddiness arose in an attack of indigestion, and " • treatment failed to restore me • .? e 1... tal Dr. Williams' pink pills were ed. At that time I had learned . dread the sight of food. Every Atle thing I took seemed to -oduce acidity. However light y meals, I used to feel as if a ton 4E41 eight was An my chest. I grew sctrernely weak for want of food. idesall this I grew verynervous. I**4lL7 I thought I was going to die. At ,st. when four doctors had had a go me, I read in a newspaper of a ,ng lady who had suffered almost ,ctly as I had, and had been atched from the grave by Dr. pink pills for pale people. eat afternoon I commenced ray traPt.; • rst box of Dr. Williams' pink pills ad I will swear before any judge in gfliatt ' le land that if I had not commenc- -724."•••^z_. .* '1q;7:;;;&amp;..-1 ri then I don't think I should have ived another week. That terrible Ser:Li-flie feeling of dizziness and nervousness 4 11111,177,-,-,;, began to leave me. Soon I could eat a hearty meal without pain. could enjoy a good night's rest, and ' 7 my food, which for a long time did harm instead of good, nownourished</p>
