      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>THE FINANCIAL POSITION OF LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The interest which is taken in the affairs of the Parish Church, was fully borne out by the large attendance at the annual vestry meeting held in the National Schools on Tuesday evening. The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) presided. He was accompanied on the platform by Mr Craik (Rectors warden) and Mr. E. A. White (vestry clerki.) A SATISFACTORY BALANCE SHEET. Mr. J. G. Craik said he had been asked by Mr. Dew- burst, who was away to make a statement with reference to the accounts. The total receipts were £691 18s. 11d. and the expenditure £704 13s. 7d., leaving a balance due to the bank of £l2 143. Bd. That, amount Mr. Craik ex- plained, was merely a temporary deficit, but it repre- sented their position at the end of 1902, although they were shortly afterwards on the right side and were on the right side at the present time. (Hear, hear.) . That was very satisfactory, as last year their receipts were over £5O less than they were the preceding year on the ordinary churchwardens accounts. They received £3O less from the parochial tea party and the ordinary voluntary collections were £2O less,although the special offertories were £76 2s. Bd. in exoess of the previous year. (Applause.) The receipts year by year had shown a steady increase. Owing to the repairs in the church last year, the expenses • were very heavy. The gas account had been considerably larger owing to the men having to work such a long time. • They had also had extra cleaning to pay for; but whilst so many improve- ments were being effected Inside and outside the church they did not forget .the claims of the clock. (Hear, hear.) The face and hands had been painted and gilded. He thought they would agree that the church wardens had dealt with the money entrusted to them to the satis- faction of the parishioners. (Hear, hear.) Mr. S. Davies proposed the adoption of the accounts. llethought they ought all to he gratified with the statement which had been made, and the results which had been obtained during the past year. While they. 'tv are a little on the wrong side he thought that, con- sidering the amount of work which had been done, the large edificf) theyhad had to maintain.and the necessary c ills upon their resources, they had every reason to be thankful that they were not very much more on the wrong side. The congregation was not a wealthy one, consisting mostly of the middle-class and the working class and therefore he considered the amount collected very good indeed. With reference to the restiamtion fund, he was sorry to find that it was behind ; he under- stood the Rector and the churchwardens were respon- sible to the bank for a deficit of £4OO and while it remained interest would have to be paid, which was a very serious item. Within the last few days a request had been made to the Rector for p3rmission to put something in the church. He did not know whether the Rector would make any announcement about the matter that evening. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Maso-n seconded, and remarked that the financial report was in the circumstances a very satisfactory one indeed, and one which, he thought, would be very acceptable to all present. The motion was carried. THE ‘VA.RDE.NS AND SIDESMEN.</p>
        <p>The Rector said he was sure it would be as great a satisfaction to the meeting, as it was to him, to be able to say that he had prevailed upon Mr. Craik to act as his warden for another year. (Applause.) Mr. Craik was deservedly popular with,them all and it had always been his (the Rector's) desire to have as his warden, a person whom the people would like, just as much as he (the Rector) would. Mr. Craik acknowledged the compliment and said that Mr. Atkin had consented to act as his sidesman. Mr. T. Mason proposed the re-ele3tion of Mr. John Dewhurst as people's wa.rdenland referred to the interest which Mr. ,Dewhurst took in everything belonging to the church. He knew from the experientbe he had had of Mr. Dewhurst that he would continue to do what he could for the church. (Applause.) Mr. Willia..n Price eaconded, and the motion was carried unanimously. Mr. Craik announced that in Mr. Dewhurst's absence he appointed Mr. S. Davies as Mr. Dewhurst's sidesma.n for the ensuing year. Eighteen gentlemen ware proposed as sidesmen, but only eight ware required. The voting resulted as fol- lows :—Messrs. Thomas Mason, T. J. Hulse, Robert Lowe, Samuel Taylor, Edward Isherwood, W. L. Williams, W. Rogers and F. G. Taylor. Mr. Wilson was appointed the representative sides- man for St. George's Mission and Mr. Charlesworth for St. Clement's. LAY DELEGATES, VESTRY CLERK AND AUDITORS. Mr. R. T. Fairclough and Mr. James Wood were appointed the lay delegates. Mr. Craik proposed the re-election of Mr. E. A. White as vestry clerk: Mr. White he said, was very efficient awl gave every satisfaction. (Applause.) The proposition was carried unanimously. Mr. E. A. White,in reply, facetiously observed that he noticed they did not doable the vestry clerk's salary. (Laughter.) Messrs. P. C. Price and J. T. Waittle wara elected a uditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ARCH I DIACONAL VIS ETAT lON,</head>
        <p>ARCH I DIACONAL VIS ETAT lON,</p>
        <p>The Re?tor announ3ed that the Archdeacon of War- rington would hold his annual visitation at the Parish Church on Wednesday, April 29th, at 11 a.m. He hoped all the officers would attend.</p>
        <p>THE COST OF. BLOWING THE ORGAN</p>
        <p>Mr. Daintith asked what wa,3 the cost of the blowinc, of the organ by the electric motor—the difference in the cost compared with the old method. Mr. Craik said he could hardly give an answer yet. The account theyha,d received for one quarter was very satisfactory, but he Ifould not express an opinion at the present stage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>INTERESTING SPEECH BY WU. RECTOR.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING SPEECH BY WU. RECTOR.</p>
        <p>The Rector remarked that there had been a steady in- crease in the regular offertories of the church during the last few years. He had taken the last four years— he was not including extraneous surns--but what was given at tho offertories. and he &amp;mai thtt the offer- tories had been as follows:—Cl93, £5Ol, £5ll, £566. (Applause). He wished very earnestly to bring before them the matter of the church restoration. He asked for £3,000 and a year ago the amount received was £1,660. This year that sum had bean increased to £2,030, but they still needed £135. TOL was a large amount, and he asked those who loved the church, and had not yet given, to consider the matter and see if they could subscribe. They had overdrawn their ac- count at the balk and they had to pay interest on it. If they could not get anything more in the way of sub- sCriptions—though he hoped they could—he asked all of them to do their utmost to make the parochial sale in October a very great success. It would extend over three days, and they would have at least £lOO to raise. They knew what a great success the parochial tea had been. They tried two nights last year but it was not the success they could have wished, and so they had unanimously decided to go back to one night in November this year, hut they must have ,a stroll('' and representative committee. They wduld all be thankful to God for the wonderful mission held just before Lent. He could not have wished better men to come amongst them. There were numbers who could testify, men and women, to the great blessing they received from it. He was thankful for the attendance at the men's services in Lent. They chose the very ablest preachers they could get and they had large congregations of man, and those who attended the Bermes should . feel that the best way they could show their thankfulness was by regularly- attending the church, particularly upon Sundays, and giving all the help they could to true religion. It would be a matter of great thankfulness to them to know what a large increase had taken place in the number of their coin- municants. The number, like the offertories, had gone steadily up, and this year the number of communicants was 876 on Ea.iter Day, which was an increase of 60 over last year. (Applause.) Their services too were a matter of thankfulness. He did not believe any cathedral could have a more beautiful service than they had, and they owed the deepest thanks to Mr. Darby and the choir. (Hear, hear.) His assistant curates had worked most indefatigably. (Applause.) Tne congre- gations at the two missions ware as good as they possibly could be, awl the Bible class under the care of Mr. Dickenson had risen to one hundred men. They all regretted Mr. White's illness, but he (the Rector) was pleased to hear good accounts of him,, (Hear, hear.) PROPOSED AIENIOIII.U. SCREEN T./ FALLEN SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>'Mr. Daintith asked for information about the pro- posed screen at the west ead of the church.</p>
        <p>The Rector said that Colonel Fitzher'aert had taken the deepest interest in the church. He wanted to erect a screen at the west end as a memorial to the officers and men who had fallen in the war, but there was some who belonged to other communions, and it was felt that if a monument was put up in tho Parish Church it would not commend itself to those of other religions, and that was the chief reason why the idea had fallen through. Whether there would be a secular monument put up in the open air he did not know, but the. 3rd South Lancashire Regiment want to pug up a screen at the west end of the Boteler Chapel as a memorial to their officers and men who had fallen in the late war. He had submitted the design to their architect,and when he had heard what he had to say he should call the church officers together. (Applause.) A screen at the west end was a necessity, and if they had not got £435 to raise for the completion of the Parish Church the officers would have pressed for a screen, but they dared not face a further expenditure. As soon as they could raise the £435 and clear the debt off the church he thought the officers would be anxious to have a screen there. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. Cr:tik, 'seconded by Mr. P. G. Taylor, the Rector was thanked for his services in the chair.</p>
        <p>The Rector, iii reply, said that before he came to Warrington he was afraid of vestry meetings. He had heard they were of an unpleasant character, bat his ex- perience of them in Warrington had be3n very pleasant. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>ST. ANN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting in connection with the above church was held on Tuesday evening. There were present :—The Rev. James Davies (Vicar), the Rev. D. Kerriu (Curate), Mr. Woods CVestry Clerk), Mr. William</p>
        <p>Thomas (Lay Reader), and Messrs. Frank Taylor, Edwin Winter, George Armstrong, Samuel C. Daniels, Joseph Bunch Quarter Master Sergeant Henry Taylor, H. H. Bretherton, (Churchwarden) and S. Dykes (Caretaker). The Vicar presided and elected for his warden Mr. Frank Taylor and eventually Mr. H. H. Bretherton was re-elected }people's warden. The lay representatives appointed were Quarter Master Sergeant Taylorand Mr. George Armstrong. After the election of officers the accounts for the past year were read. The year com- menced with a credit balance of us 41d., and there was received in offertories and subscriptions £ll3 15s. Old. The expenses which included old liabilities of over £2l amounted to £lll us. 51d. leaving the church wardens with a balance in hand of £2 14s. 111 d. The accounts this year Compared with last, show an increase in the receipts of nearly .27. A vote of thanks was passed to the Vestry Clerk who returned thanks. The meeting then terminated. ST. THOMAS'S, STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting in connection with this church was held at noon on Tuesday. The Vicar (Rev. H. V. Pigot) presided and there were also present the Rev. A. Markham Evans (curate), Messrs. Henry Greenall, J. Richardson, F. Pearson, I'. Sharrocks, T. R. Westbrook,J. Jeffs, F.Timperley,F. Rose,A. Down,and T. Jackson.</p>
        <p>Tif E ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richardson (people's warden) presented the state- Itient of accounts from which it appeared that the income included £227 18s. Bd. from general offertories, including the harvest festival, as against £217 Os. Bd. last year, and £3O 17s. Id. from special offertories as compared with .258 Hs. 2d. last year. Various other receipts brought up the total income to 1.323 13s. 7d. Last year the income amounted to £3OB 3s. Id. After paying fexpenses there was a balance due to the wardens of £2 Os. 10d. Mr. Richardson said the balance sheet was the most satisfactory one which had been presented. for some time. The offertories were slowly but steadily increasing and he hoped they would continue to do so. The exterior of the church was in a better state of repair than it had been in for a long time and there ought not to be much expense on repairs during the ensuing year. Mr. Jackson expressed the opinion that the lighting of the church might be more economically carried out by _a re-arrangement of the gtls. burners: _ .</p>
        <p>:Mr. Richardson did not tiiink it would be worth while incurring much expense in this direction as the advisability of lighting the church electrically might have to be consideredat some future date. An improve- ment in the lighting of the et end of the Char= was certainly required. On the motion of Mr. Pearson, seconded by Mr. Timperley, the accounts were adopted.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said he was sure he would have the hearty support of the vestry when he asked Mr.. Greenell to aocept the office of Vicar's warden for another year. He did not think there could be a more popular appoint- ment even if it were made by the parishioners them- selves. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Greenall's services had been most valuable ,and he had pleasure in nominating him for the position for another year. Mr. ()treenail said he would be very glad to do What he could to assist the church in any way. On the motion of Mr. Westbrook, seconded by Mr. Slaarrocks, Mr. Richardson was unanimously re-elected people's warden. The following sidesmen were re-elected on the pro- position of Mr. Jackson, seconded by Mr. Greenell : Messrs. Arthur Down, T. J. Lee, J. H. Clarke, John Jeffs, T. R. ‘Vestbrook. J. A. McGregor, W. Mclntyre, S. W. DaHow, Thomas Suarracks, Leonard G. Davies, James Dunbobbin, and John Parkes. Messrs. Sharrocks and McGregor were re-elected lay representatives to attend the ruri-decanal and diocesan conferences. THE MEMORIAL TO TFIE LATE MR. R. DAVIES.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said that at last year's vestry meeting a resolution was passed expressing the opinion that souls' memorial to the late Mr. Robert Davies should be placed in the church. A committee was appointed and many meetings were held during the year. The busi- ness had been carried through with singular unanimity and he hOped the vestry would approve of what had been done by the co.nmittee in the same way. The com- mittee consulted the family of the late Mr. Davies as to taeir wishes in the matter. At first the members of the family suggested that they should contribute to the memorial, but afterwards they said they thought it would be better on several grounds to leave the matter entirely in the hands of the parishioners and friends. They also suggested that there should be a memorial to their father and mother. Mrs. Robert Davies was a native of the parish, and lived there all her life. The suggestion was accepted by the committee, and it was unanimously decided that a stained glass window should be pnt in the Church. The work had been entrusted to Messrs. Shrigley and Hunt, of Lancaster, and had been carried out in a way which he thought would give satis- faction and pleasure to everybody. The amount collec- ted for the memorial was a little over .£lOO. The win- dow itself cost £lOO, and the bass tablet an additional £5 or t:6. He thought they would be able to •pay for that, and also he hoped that when the outstanding sub- scriptions were collected they would be able to carry out the other suggestion that was made, and purchase new service books and lectern Bibles for the clergy. Mr. Greenall said the window was a beautiful piece of work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Richardson: It appears to have given satisfac- tion all round. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the members of the committee for their services and the meeting ex- pressed approval of their work.</p>
        <p>THANKS TO THR CHOIR.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to the organist (Mr. F. Rose), deputy organist (Mr. A. Downs), Mr. Sharrocks and the members of the choir for their services during the year. In moving the resolution Mr. Greenall said the musical services of the church were a very great credit to the choir.</p>
        <p>The Vicar was thanked for presiding, and in reply alluded to the good work which had been done by the Rev. A. .1\ 1.. Evans during the few months he ha d been in the parish. The Rev. A. M. Evans expressed _his thanks for the kindness shown towards him by the Vicar and the parishioners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY.</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY.</p>
        <p>Th 3 R3V. E. C. E. Carleton presided over the annual vestry meeting in connection with this Church, which was held on Tuesday evening. There werealso present: —Messrs. J. H. Halton, T. Welsby, H. Ferguson, R. W. Rowson, W. Gill, P. Randall, H. W. Naylor, and G. F F. White (vestry clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ACCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>THE ACCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>The statement of accounts showed that the income amounted to £293 14s. 2,111., and the e7tpenditnre to £295 I4s. 31d., leavint;a btlance in the bank of 195.. I Ed. On the motion of Mr. Gill, seconded by Mr. White, the accounts were flossed.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS,</p>
        <p>The Vicar nominated Mr. J. H. Halton as his warden, and said the Church could not have been better served than it had been daring the past year by both wardens. Is Mr. T t., . 'wasWe re-elected people's warden on the motion of M 'aylor, seconded by Mr. Rowson. Mr. Halt9n minated Mr. W. J. Forshaw as his sidesman, and Mr. Welsby nominated Mr. J. Harding. The other sidestnen were elected as follows :—Messrs, E. Dyke, H. .Ferguson, J. Gerrard, IV. Gill, John Hesketh, Joseph Hesketh, T. Hunt, H. W. Naylor, E. Rose, R. %V. Rowson, G. F. F. White,J.Dunn,J.Hignett, W._Shirlow, H. Milling, G. Gill, and J. Boweock.</p>
        <p>Messrs. A. W. Rowsiin and W. Gill were elected as lay representatives; Mr. H. W. Naylor, auditor; and Mr.G. F. F. White, vestry clerk.</p>
        <p>CIIIJRCH RENOVATION.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said the p trishionerS would like to know how the renovation scheme was proceeding. He wee glad to assure then that it was slowly progressing, and he hoped before very longlhe church would be com- pletely renovated. (Hear, hear.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>VICARAGE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>THE VICARAGE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The Vicar all-n-ded—R,W this scheme and said they would require something like £l,OOO before the deficit could be wiped off. Mr. Giltasked if it wts intended to do anything to raise this money during the year. The Viczr said he did net wish to hurry matters and he did not think they would be doing anything for some time. He hims)lf was p tying the interest on the borrowed money and w.ts content to do so. At some future date they - would make an effort to raise the £l,OOO. A vote of thanks was accorded to the church wardens and choir and the chairman having been thanked for presiding,the meeting concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting in connection with this church was held on Wednesday evening, the vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth) presiding. Them were also present :—Messrs. W. A. Proud (people's warden), 3. Iddon, C. Skinner, W. P. James, E. Tddon, A. Byrom. J. J. Siddall, and G. Rolle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>A CCOUNTS.</head>
        <p>THE A CCOUNTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. A. Proutprented the statement of accounts which showed that the incoma amounted to .£l3B 1.25. :id. made up as follows :—Ordinary collections, £64 Bs. Bd. ; special collections, £3.; proceeds of parochial tea party, 1:39 3s. 7d. At the commencement of the year there was an adverse balance of £3 17s. 24d.' but after paying all expenses and handing over £lB to the Curate's Fund,and £6 16s. 6d to the Church Improvement Fund, there was a balance in hand of 18s. 8.1,d. He considered the state- ment to be very satisfactory. (Hear. hear.)</p>
        <p>THE VICAR AND THE YEAR'S PHOORESS.</p>
        <p>1 The Vicar said he was sure they would all agree that , the statement of accounts was most satisfactory. He I was glad to state that the ordinary collections for . church expenses had shown an increase. That was more I than satisfactory,because during the year several people 1 who had been very regular attenders had left the parish. The fact that there was a balance in hand showed that , the back-bone of the church wan getting stronger. Other people had taken the places of those who had left, the parish and at present, he thought, the church j was going on magnificently. The number of communi. • cants .on Easter Day was in excess of former years, in Ifact, during the past four years the attendance at Easter Communion had steadily increased. They would be glad to hear that an assistant clergyman—the Rev. P. M. Forrest—would com- mence work in the parish at the end of June. For two years Mr. Forrest worked at St. Thomas's, I Bethnal Green, London, which was a poor parish, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>- _ _ . . .</p>
        <p>had been accustomed to heavy slum work. He (the Vicar), was perfectly certain that they would give Mr. Forrest their hearty support and make him feel that he had no cause to regret having come to work amongst them. Mr. Forrest was excellently recommended by his former Vicar and by the Bishop of London as a clergyman who was well able to work in any parish in England. In conclusion,the Vicar alluded to the great loss which the parish sustained in October last by the death of Mr. W. H. Trevett, the Vicar's warden, who,he said, was a very earnest, kind and sympathetic supporter of St. Barnabas' Church.</p>
        <p>Mr. Proud endorsed the Vicar's remarks in regard to the death of Mr. Trevett, and said they had lost a very hard and conscientious worker and a colleague whom they all missed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The Yicar nominated Mr. James Iddon to act as his warden during the ensuing year. On the motion of Mr. J. Iddon, seconded by Mr. Byram, Mr. Proud was unanimously re-elected people's warden.</p>
        <p>The Vicar nominated Messrs. A. Byram, P. Sudlow and W. P. James as his sidesmen, and Mr. Proud nominated Messrs. C. Skinner, E. Iddon, and T. Lowe.</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. J. Siddall, G, Rolfe, and J. Tomkins were elected sidesmen by the vestry, and Messrs. Skinner and James were appointed lay representatives.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS,</p>
        <p>The Vicar proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Proud and the sidesmen for their services during the past year. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- Mr. Byram seconded and the motion was agreed to. A vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding con- cluded the meeting. '</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting for St. Peter's Church was held on Wednesday evening. The Vicar (Rev. B. Percival) presided, and amongst those present were : Rev. D. James (carate). ULpain Sniptoa (Church Army), Councillor John Roberts, Mr. Bamforth, Mr. Joseph Taylor, and Mr. Peter Edgar (vestry clerk.) Letters of apology for nou-attendance were read from Mr. T. S. Steel, Mr. Kay, Mr. Hands and Mr. Lashford. —The statement of accounts was presented by Mr. Bamforth, from which it appeared that the collections during the year, including a balance brought forward of 81 19s. 4d., amounted to £195 17s. 21d. At the close of the year there was a balance in hand of 9s. 3d. Mr. Bamforth added that one small account had since come in.—Mr. Joseph Taylor proposed the adoption of the accounts and stated that the wardens had every retson to congratulate themselves upon producing a satisfactory balance sheet.—Mr. Green seconded the proposition and it was unanimously agreed to..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>REVIEW OF THE YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>REVIEW OF THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said he would like to congratulate the church-wardens upon what he believed was the best balance sheet they had had since he had been vicar of the church. It showed an increased income of a little over £2O upon the previous year and it was the largest income they had had in any previous sear by some £7 or £B. (Hear, hear.) During the year they had built the boundary wall and the whole of the cost, with the ex- ception of a sum of £7 14s. had been raised during the past year. When they remembered what had been done, they had every reason to congratulate themselves. He would like to thank heartily all those who had so generously contributed to the funds of the church. Their own people gave freely of their small substance, for be believed that they had the largest number of coins in their offertorythan any other church in the town. He believed they numbered something like 25,000 or 26,000 coins per annum. They owed a great debt of gratitude to all their people who had so generously contributed to the offertory and rendered assistance for the wall. He would like also to. thank those outside the parish for the help they gave year after year. They conld not carry on a big church like that without outside assist- ance. The Vicar proceeded to thank his colleague, the Rev. D. James for the way he had worked, and also Captain Shipton, of the Church Army, for his labours in connection with the Mission. The churchwardens had worked loyally with him. In parochial matters it was not always possible for workers to see eye to eye on every point, but it was a great mis- take when people made grievances out of such small trifles. He had never had any trouble with those in (Ace and they had all worked loyally and earnestly with him. The Easter offering was the best he had had. It was not a large offering when compared with others ' but it was large for St. Peter's, and was four times larger in amount than the usual offertory. He took it as a testimonial to himself as it bore real testimony to the kindly thoughifulnoss of the parishioners. The number of communicants was the largest since he had been in the parish, thus show- , ing that the temporal and spiritual side of the I work was going on satisfactorily. The Vicar proceeded 1 to thank the choir and.the organist (Mr. P. Edgar) and also Mr. Webster for his services at the Sunday after-1 noon class for men. He also thanked the sidesmen for I their regular attendance at church and all who had assisted to make the past year one of their most success- ful years.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OP WARDENS AND SIDESNIEN.</p>
        <p>The Vicar said he had spoken to Mr. John Roberts who had been such a valuable helper and always a gener- ous contributor to the funds of the church and-he had kindly consented to become vicar's warden for another year. (Hear, hear.) Mr. John Roberts, in reply, said he had always taken an interest in the work of the church and pointed out that he was the first warden. He eulogised the services of Mr. Bamforth, people's warden, and the sidesmen. It was very pleasing to know that there was a little balance in the bank, but it was still more pleasing to see the Vicar in such good health. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the Vicar would be spared for many years to continue his work in the parish. Mr. Roberts proceeded to pay a tribute to the excellent work done by the Rev. D. James during the absence in Switzerland of the Vicar. Mr. John Roberts then noihinated Mr. Kay as his sidesman. Mr. Stanley proposed Mr. Bamforth as people's warden.</p>
        <p>Mr. Burdett seconded and the proposition was unanimously agreed to. . _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. BamfOrtii thanked the vestry for their renewal of confidence in him and r.ominated Mr. Stanley as his sidesman. The following were also appointed sides- men: Messrs. Joseph Taylor, Cowdell, Knight. Hands, Heron, James, Taylor, Armstrong, Green, flurdett,Steel, Lashford, John Taylor, and Thomas Reddish. Mr. Alderttutn Heaton and Mr. Kay were appointed lay representatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LATE MILS. BEAMONT.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MILS. BEAMONT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edgar said he would like the vestry to place on record its "deep sense of sorrow athaving lost one whom he considered was one of the best friends that St. Peter's ever had. He alluded to the late Mrs. I3ea.mont. (Hear, hear). Her generosity was unknown and her liberality to this parish and to other parishes for church work was extremely generous. They felt they had lost in her a very good friend and one whom it would be difficult to replace. There were several monuments to her generosity in that parish, and he thought the vestry ought to place on record thew appreciation of what she did for the parish and for the town generally. (Hear,hear). The Vicar said the late Mrs. Beamont contributed £2O towards the cost of the new wall which was one of her last gifts. She left a legacy of £2OO for the schools to be spent in whatever direction might be thought best.</p>
        <p>Mr. Councillor John Roberts proposed a vote of thanks to the Vicar for presiding.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bamforth seconded the wile of thanks.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edgar, in supporting, endorsed what had been said with reference to the Vicar, and spoke highly of the great services rendered by Mrs. Percival during the Vicar's absence in Switzerland. The curate did his level best but he had a very good helpmeet in the Vicar's wife who did all she could to assist him in the work of the parish.</p>
        <p>'rhe vote of thanks was unanimously carried,</p>
        <p>Tie Vicar acknowledged the compfiment and hoped they would continue to work harmoniously together.</p>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>C mon Penrhyn presided at the annual vestry meeting on Monday, and Messrs. J. W. Knowles and P. Longton resigned the office of churchwardens. Mr. W. Massey was nominated by the Rector, and Mr. Thomas Cooper Shaw was elected by the vestry, as wardens for the ensuing year; and Messrs. 'rhomas Warburton, jun., William Peanington, Philip Greenway, and Edward Worsley were appointed sidesmen. The accounts were pre- gented and passed, showing a balance in hand of over l',23.—The best thanks of the vestry were given to the voluntary choir for their services in the past year. Messrs. G. Co-über, aad Robert Stonevera re-elected lay representatives. FRODSHAM PARTSH CHURCH. A MISSING CHARIyY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held in the Kingsley Chapel on Easter Monday morning, the Rev. H. B. Rlogg pre- siding.</p>
        <p>PfNANCIAL.</p>
        <p>From the summary of the accounts for the year it appeared that the income, including balance brought forward, was £360 3s. 9d., and the expenditure £332 10s. 2d. The Iron Church income was £6l is. Id., and the payments £55 4s. 9d. The accounts had been audited, and were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>OFFICERS 01' THE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>OFFICERS 01' THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he was overwhelined with grati- tude for the manner in which his warden, Mr. Thomas Riley, had done his work, and it was a great pleasure to re-appoint him for another year. Mr. C. E. Linaker nominated Mr. E. G. Howard as the people's warden, in the place of Mr. G. Gleave,who did not seek re-election.</p>
        <p>The following aidesinen were then nominated : Vicar's, Messrs. J. G. Davies, J. J. Wilkinson, W. Diggle ; parishioners,' Messrs. J. H. Davies,W. Burrows, and Evelyn Sutton.</p>
        <p>A MISSING CHARITY.</p>
        <p>The Vicar's warden (Mr. T. Riley) presented acheque for £32 to the vicar as an Easter offering, and in an interesting report upon his researches into the deed chest of thechurch said he had found the lost deeds of the Endowed Grammar School, 1667-1690, and also dis- covered that there were the remains of a Moore's Charity for the second master of the Grammar School in the Frodsham S tvings Bank. The original investment was made in 1845.</p>
        <p>Gastrill's Sunday School Charity of £lOO, leftby Mrs. Jane Gastrill, in 1791, Mr. Riley said, had altogether disappeared. Tt was originally Invested with Liverpool Corporation, and in 1858 was transferred to the Mersey</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>000400100001.101( Mitt '4‘</head>
        <p>000400100001.101( Mitt '4‘</p>
        <p>I IMPROVIM INJBOTION RYRIINGR.-4 speciality I. Partners and Vets This Syring with Deteobf Nozzle, should l.e on every farm. as it is useful for ordlioin injections Into the vagina, for the prevention cy Abort Wale Cows. Useful also for Horses, Cattle and large Pigs. rr 0.„ o, 35 6d ; rootage 4d extra. Sturdy Instruments, 1.... IHs 941, 14s 9d per set. Bull Nose Outten, 69 3d. 11)0..".,h0s Cash with orderiiroot Jong 8 MoFFAT,Oattle Market. ise'—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>_ . _</p>
        <p>Docks and Harbour Board. The bond was renewed from tiine to time, and the last payment made under itwas in 1889.</p>
        <p>The bond had disappeared, and unfortunately so had the £lOO. He thought the governors of the Grammar School had regarded it as their property and had spent it in extending the school. Mr. Riley reported a number of other important and interesting documents missing, and a committee was appointed to deal with the whole question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S, WALTON.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S, WALTON.</p>
        <p>The Easter vestry meeting was held on Monday morn- ing, the Vicar, the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, presiding. There were also present :—Messrs.W Weir, John Cooper, W. Turner,J. E. Seeker, and A. Moulsdale (clerk). Mr Seeker apologized for the absence of Sir Gilbert Greenall.—The Vicar re-appointed Sir Gilbert Greenall as his warden for the ensuing year, and on the motion of Mr. Turner, seconded by Mr. Cooper, Mr. Weir was re-elected parishioners' warden. The accounts, which showed a balance in hand of £3 16s. 7d., were adopted. The Vicar said certain improve- ments in the lighting of the church, which at present was not at all satisfactory had been suggeste d. He would submit the matter to Sir Gilbert Greensll. He also said that it was very desirable that better order should be maintained in the chucch during divine ser- vice, and the wardens promised to attend to the matter. A suggestion was also made that a hymn board should be provided.—Mr. Cooper said he thought they ought to pay a debt of gratitude to their deceased friend Mr. T. S. Burgess, who had passed away since their last meeting. He Was sure they all held his memory very dear and remembered what an able-officer he was. Per- sonally he had lost a dear friend and companion, and he moved that a letter of condolence be sent from that meeting to Mrs. Burgess, and that the same be recorded on the vestry 'minutes.—Mr. Turner seconded, and it was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WARGRAVE CHURCH.</head>
        <p>WARGRAVE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The annual vestry meeting of the parishioners of Wargrave was held in the church on Tuesday evening, the Rev. J. Carson presiding. There was a. small attendance, amongst those present being Messrs. H. Sweetlove, T. Cave, T. Molyneux, J. Burrows, T. Harri- son, &amp;c. The statement of accounts showed the receipts from all sources to be £l7B 16s. 91d. and the expendi- ture, after paying off an adverse balance of £9 16s. from last year, was £169 Os. 9,11, leaving a balance to the credit of the wardens of £3 19s. 9d. This was con- sidered a most satisfactory state of things, and the accounts were adopted. The Rector warned the parishioners as to the finances. When he first came to the church, the reoeipts from the church yard amounted to £3O is., but this year they were only £l5 Bs. If it were not for Mr. Turner paying his twopenny rate, and the church yard receipts they, would be nearly £27 out, so that he hoped the congregation would keep up their contributions, and even increase them. Mr. H. Sweetlove was appointed Rector's warden and Mr. T. Cave, parishioners warden. The following were appointed sidesmen. Messrs. J. Moss, R. Molyneux, T. Molyneux (Wargrave), G. Sutton, James B. Porter, Joseph Williams, W. VVright, J. Smith, J. Shepherd, and T. Harrison. Messrs. W. Thompson and T. Molyneux were appointed lay-representatives to the diocesan conference,—A voluntary rate of twopence in the pound was laid. The salaried officers were appointed on the usual terms. The usual gratuity of £l5 to the choir was recommended and the best thanks of the vestry was accorded the choir for their past services. Votes of thanks to the Chairman, the wardens and sidesmen con- cluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Golborne-street.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, at the above Church, the pastor took for his text, Yet it pleased the Lord to bruise Him, and the pleasure of the Lord shall proper in His hand." Three of the leading thoughts dealt with in the sermon weie, the pleasure of the Lord revealed in th e bruising of the Christ, the strong hand that comes forth out of the bruising and the pleasure of the Lord that is to prosper in the strong hand. Apt and forcible illus- trations were used to explain God's pleasure in, and the necessity for, the bruising of the Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Cmgregational Church, Newton.</head>
        <p>Cmgregational Church, Newton.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, the Rev. Hugh C. Wallace of Bristol will occupy the pulpit at the Congregational Church, Newton on Sunday next, on the occasion of the Sunday School Anniversary services. Mr. Wallace has already estab- lished a reputation ass powerful preacher, and he needs no recommendation. There is certain to ben full church at each service. In the afternoon a sacred cantata entitled " Moses" will be given by the choir and chil- dr en.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>Owiqg to the holiday season, there was a smaller at- tendance than usual at the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Smiley pre- sided, and in the course of his remarks he said they celebrated that day one of the grandest events in the history of the world—the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dei,d. He proceeded to relate the scene at the sepulchre as recorded in the scriptures, and said they had reason to thank God that we had got a living Christ who was willing to make intercession for us.—Mr. W. Light delivered a very thoughtful address on the Words "I know in whom I believe." He pointed out that Christ was not a mythical Christ, but a personality and that Christianity was an experience which everyone must find out for himself. He asked his hearers to try the eiginiment and not to be led away by what they read in reference to the mythical, the intellectual or historical Christ. There was a Christ, he said, who "saved men from their sins.—Master Toni Barton played two violin solos in a highly creditable manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Sharp-street Primitive Methodist Mission.</head>
        <p>Sharp-street Primitive Methodist Mission.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the.Mothers' Sewing Class, in connection with the above place of worship, a very successful tea and miscellaneous entertainment was held on Good Friday. The following ladies presided at the tea tables :—Mesdames Thompson, Parkes, Meachin, and Higham. After tea the chair was occupied by Mr. E. Horton, of the Ellesmere-street Mission, who urged the young people to consecrate" their lives to the glorious service of God, as it was much easier in youth than when more advanced in years. He was a great advocate of child-conversion, as it was by the instru- mentality of one of his own children, that he when bound by the fetters of sin in all its hideous forms, was brought to a saving knowledge of the Lord Jesus Christ Recitations were creditably given by Mrs. Thompson and Mrs. Brandwood, and the Misses Elsie Brown,Hettie Meachin, Doris Thompson and Lily Brandwood. Miss Elsie Parkestat little girl of four summers, delighted the audience by her recitation "Ten little servants "which received well-merited applause. A trio entitled "Three modest quakeresses," was well rendered by the Misses Brown, Davis and Brandwood. The duet "Angels are whispering" was very effectively rendered by Mrs. Parkes and Mrs. Watkin. Mrs. Horton rendered the solo "The vacant chair "and Mrs. Carrick "The fisher- , 1 man s child with pleasing effect. Mrs. Parkes was heard to advantage in her interpretation of the solo "God shall wipe away all tears" and Mrs. Watkin tastefully rendered the solo "0 Rest in the Lord." Mr. W. H. Parkes presided at the organ with his usual ability. Great praise is due to Mrs. Parkes and Mrs. Brandwood who had arranged the gathering. A hearty vote of thanks was acoorded to the chairman on the motion of Mr. E. G. Barmtt seconded by Mr. W. Newton. The proceedings concluded with the singing of the Doxology and the pronouncing of the Bene- dict ion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Fast Motoring at Lymm.</head>
        <p>Fast Motoring at Lymm.</p>
        <p>At ,Lymm Petty Se-ssions, on Wednesday, Mr. Harold IVilliam Smith, Annestyle, Ashley - road, Bowdon, was summoned for driving a • motor car at a dangerous speed in Booth-road, Lymm, on March 22nd. Evidence was given to the effect that the de- fendant failed to sound his gong when passim-, another _motor in a narrow part of the road and collided with the other motor, causing it to swerve and nearly knock down a man walking along the road, while a child in a bassinette was also in danger. The defen- dant admitted that it was an error of judgment in passing the car, but he was not going more than ten or twelve miles an hour. The Chairman said an extra staff of police had to be kept at Lymm. on Sunday to look after cyclists and motorists, and they were determined to stop furious driving. Defendant was fined altogether a Os.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</head>
        <p>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</p>
        <p>TAKE VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. This marvellously successful remedy surpasses anything in the healing art and has the largest sale in the world.</p>
        <p>The remarkable rapidity with which Veno's Lightning Cough Cure absolutely cures the worst cases of Cutrons, COLDS, BRONCHITIS, ASTHMA, INFLUENZA, CATARRH, and CHILDREN'S COUGHS has gained for it scientific recognition throughout the world. 'GRANVILLE H. SHARPE, F.C.S., Analyst, 11 and 12, Great Tower-street, London, says in his certificate of analysis :—"I fun of opinion that Vene's Lightning Cough Cure will prove a valuable remedy in the cure of coughs, colds, asthma, and all bronchial complaints." GEO. KNOX, M.D., 256, Second Avenue, Pittsburg, writes :—" Veno's Lightning Cough Cure excels in chronic coughs and bronchitis, and is especially good for children." Ask for Veno's LIGHTNING COUGH CURE at Chemists, price ls. 10. and 26. 9d., or post free from the Vent) Drug Co., Hulme, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>A Journalist's Successful Claim. At the East Grinstead County Court, the case el Dean v. Hartigan was heard. The plaintiff was Godfrey Dean, journalist, formerly of Warrington, but, now in the employ of the Southern Publishing C 433 Ltd:; and the defendant Mr. J. 'l'. P. Hartigan, medie'lL practitionor at East Grinstead, and the claim was ler £lBs. for services rendered. • Plaintiff stated that the services rendered consisted of shorthand notes take)! for defendant at two meetings at the East Griusteaa Board of Guardians. Cross-examined: He admitted the agreement (produced) entered into betweeo him and his employers, hut denied tle ue the agreement had . any bearing upon t claim. He also admitted that in consequence of hal" ing pressed his claim he had been given a month's notice to leave. Mr. W. 11. Hills, journalist, of Elt,° Grinstead, supported the claim, and spoke as to custom in such cases. Cross-examined:- Plaintix had every right by custom to the fee charged, and took it that the claim in the agreement referred to meant that plaintiff should not work for any other newspaper, and especially for any newspaper in our, petition with,his own. Mr. Hughes contended that,' anything was due, the Company. and not the plaintu" was entitled to the amount. His Honour, in giviq judgment for the amount claimed, with costs, there was only one claim against the defendant. agreement between the plaintiff and his employers ha u nothing to do with the matter whatever.</p>
      </div>
