      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>, Cowes will have its fashionable gathering as usual next July and August, but the chief event in the yacht, ing world this year will be the contest for the American Cup. Great interest will be excited over Sir Thomas Lipton's third attempt to restore the trophy to this country, even among people who do not know one end of a yacht from the other. The splendid form and pace which Shamrock 111 has shewn in hertrials, encourages our hopes to run high, and there seems little doubt that she is a better boat than her predecessors. It is a common saying and belief, that the third time of trying is sure to be successful, and everyone on this side of the Atlantic will rejoice if Sir Thomas succeeds at last in beating the American boat. Many people have taken advantage of the Easter recess to spend a few days on the Continent, and this is not surprising, when, we consider the cheapness and varied attractions of foreign travel. No doubt it is natural for the landlady or hotel keeper to get as much as they can out of the individual visitor, but though this mly be characteristic all the world over, it is certainly possible to obtain more for one's money say in Normandy or Switzerland, than at many of our holiday resorts. This is the case even in Paris,but in Normandy one may live luxuriantly upon ss. or Os. per day, and obtain not only an entire change of diet and scene, but also the opportunity to acquire or polish up one's know- ledge o!. French. No doubt similar ways of spending an inexpensive holiday may be found in our own villages and farmhouses, but all the same, those who have the courage to go abroaq, certainly obtain advan- tages that are 'often difficult to flnd at home. Miss Ellaline Terriss has rendered a service to the public by the caution which she has given to aspirants for the stage, that they should satisfy themselves by some surer guide than self-confidence that they are not ; attempting to follow a path Which can lead only to dis- appointment. In order to help them in that inquiry she details some of the e.ssentia/s of success on the stage. ; The first of theme is magnetism, which at once disposes of the dictum attributed to a great painter that, except in poetry, is man can be anything that he likes. Let the aspirant only be possessed of magnetism, says Miss Terriss, and he or she can afford to forget all the rest.. And there can be no doubt that she is right to the extent that this magnetism is essential to success on the i . i stage, as t s essential to a public speaker, and in the : highest degree helpful to the preacher. Magnetism in i this sense may be difficult to define, but it is something ' which places the audience in sympathy with the actor or speaker, and it is something which he or she cannot altogether command. To-day it may be present in large measure, to-morrow it may be nearly altogether absent. Stories of romantic marriages seem more in accord- ance with the old pastoral days than those of the modern, factory, but one that has recently come to light is of interest to all the world. The girl, who worked in a felt hat manufactory in Lancashire, put a tiny portrait of herself with her name and address into the lining of one of the hats, and this, in due course, was shipped to South Africa. The purchaser,whO happened to be a well- to-do mechanic. discovered the portrait, and coming home on a visit to his relatives, sought out the sender of the missive. The acquaintance was of the happiest kind, with the result that the girl no longer works in the factory, but is going to make her home in South Africa. It is probably not Often that this chance Wooing of fate ends so happily, though it is believed that it is a common thing for girls working in the factories to put messages into the linings of the men's hats. Bridesmaids will be grateful to Lady Sybil Grant, for deciding the so often vexed question with regard to the shape of their headgear. Hitherto it has been an un- writt,sit law that this 'shall be the same save for the children, and consequently girls have often been forced to wear what certainly has not suited them. Lady Sybil's bridesmaids were individually studied, and each wore the shape that set off her charms to the best ad- vantage, the materials being the same, of course. At the several smart weddings which have taken place lately this excellent idea has been followed, with the best possible results. Tussore silk is quite restored to favour, and is ap- pearing in the form of bleuses, entire gowns, coats, and dust wraps. Alpaca, amether old friend, has also ap- peared among the many fascinating spring fabrics, this time in maw new forms. It may for example, be had spotted, or striped, or backed with a contrasting colour. It is moreover so beautifully soft and supple, that modistes or home workers will have no difficulty in the draping process, a trouble familiar to many of us in days gone by. Voile, and the pew open meshed canvas, are much seen, and taffetas, softer and better wearing than before, is being made up to a considerable extent by leading dreEsmakers this spring.</p>
        <p>Most of the lace, and 14ce-like crochet used for the embellishment of dress just now, comes from Ireland, and as no toilette is considered complete without such adornment, the Irish workers-nnist,be doing splendidly. Milliners are showing a great affection for Irish crochet lace, this particular fabric forming both crown and brim of many of the newest spring creations. For the deep shoulder capes with long stole ends, Irish crochet is agnin employed, also for the early Victorian capes, coming down into a point, back and front. The craze for serving luncheon, and dinner, on a highly polished mahogany tlble, with mats for the dishes, and plates, has not quite died out, but happily the idea is not often indulged in nowadays. Nothing in this way cell ever'equal the pure white linen cloth ex- quisitely dressed and folded. Table centres of silk and chiffon are !Loin to. the fore, alact women who take a pride in their daility table appointments, will appreciate. Many old associations centre round the cheerful hearth, but nevertheless no one can doubt that the open llregrate is doomed to pass away. The recent discovery of a metal that gives out constant heat and energy, without conbustion, or diminution, has led to some fanciful notions, on this subject, but though these may be absurd, yet coal with all the dirt and work it makes, will sooner or later he banished from our houses. There is little doubt that electricity will before long enter very largely into the heating and cooking arrangethents of the household, and when that improvement comes it will very materially relieve the pressure of the domestic servant difficulty in a great many homes. To the dainty series of books which Mr. Grant Richards is publishing under the title of "The World's Classics," has been added Hawthorne's " The Scarlet Letter," and Thackerays "Henry Esmond." This series is designed to embrace the most.- famous works of the most famous authors and to form a well chosen library of inexpensive though attractive volumes. Among the many fascinating novels published by Mr. Richards is "The Weird Olt," by the author of " The Yellow Danger ;" " The Leopard Spots" by T. Dixon ; " The Sentimental Warrior 'by Edgar Jepson, and "'Aspira- tions "by Miss Constance West. The latter—a keenly interesting work—compels one's attention, tuid admira- tion.</p>
        <p>MADGE. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
