      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <p>. . • - -</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, April 17, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>April, all smiles and tears, is not to bo trusted, for, in spite of the frequent bursts of brilliant sunshine with which we are favoured, it is hardly safe to venture abroad in a delicate - toned toilette without taking an umbrella as a companion. The sky so rapidly becomes overcast and the heavy showers which ensue bring destruction to a dainty gown or hat.</p>
        <p>Voluminous skirts and full shoulder capes of varying dimensions, together with sleeves which show a growing incli- nation towards greater enlargement be- low the elbow, are accepted on all sides, and appear in more or less exaggerated stylesJ</p>
        <p>A LACE GOWNJ</p>
        <p>Indeed, care must be taken not to allow that which is picturesque and graceful in dress to degenerate into floppiness. The lace gown sketched is very charm- ingly made, and is suited for dinner, res- taurant, or theatre wear, while it would even- tually make a lovely toilette for garden par- ties or similar festive occasions during the ensuing summer months. It is made of string- coloured lace, with a floral pattern on a fine net ground, over white chine silk, and a cluster of deep yellow roses is arranged on the corsage. The yoke is of the lace, unlined, and the throat above is encircled by a hand- some pearl necklet, fastened in the centre with a brilliant clasp. Straps of ribbon ex- tend across the skirt above the kills, and are finished off by a twist of cream silk cord and some pretty tassels to match. The sleeves have a full lace frill falling over the under- sleeve, which is caught in to a band of the ribbons The whole toilette is eminently chic.</p>
        <p>. , r, /, oot ,I - v.,-.,. . 'f a-•?.''1";(\. i i 0 4 •,... 4 f:,.r't--</p>
        <p>i • ' d -... 1/4 ii k j;II 7'l/ )13C ‘\'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>PRETTY BLOUSE:</head>
        <p>A PRETTY BLOUSE:</p>
        <p>Lace is as popular as ever, and appears on every article of dress. There are bewitching little coffee coats made of fine or coarse lace, or even of the two intermingled, while every form .of blouse shows lace employed either in motifs or in the form of insertion, inter- spersed with small fine tucks. Hats have their wide brims swathed and softened with folds of delicate lace, and toques of fragile tulle show motifs of contrasting lace in either black, white, or string-colour. I have seen 6ome very lovely garments, half• cape half coat, made out of treasured heirlooms in the shape of thir,vls, which up to hitherto it had been dift-^,-11 to put to practical use without the misfortune of cutting _them, which, of course, reduces them seriously in value.</p>
        <p>I have sketched a charmingly pretty blouse, which is in reality a veritable creation, as it owes its origin to sundry oddments of lace edging, lace insertion, and silk picked up at the early season sales, and now evolved into a thing of beauty. The lace, full in front, falls in loose pleats over the silk foundation underneath, while a softly -folded band of silk is brought from under the arms at either side, and.nished in front by a chou. The yoke and upper part of the sleeves are of tucked silk. It is wonderful how one can, with a little ingenuity and taste, aided and abetted by a good pattern, convert pieces of silk, satin, and lace into smart little blouses or</p>
        <p>• :--:.-N l, ,i; _ .. ,) . il (.. :f f f ,' • ~)‘' ) • • ' Li 'N-2 / V Orta:100P. % , 1 1, I' SI 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CHILD'S DRESSJ</head>
        <p>A CHILD'S DRESSJ</p>
        <p>boleros. Indeed, so mny materials are often used by the best modistes in the construction of their models that one need never hesitate how great the variety employed, provided real good taste is used in the arrangement. Old bodices, too, can be transformed into wonderfully smart and tasteful little arrange- ments by the addition of a bolero, with sleeves reaching only to the elbow, made out of a few yards of coarse lace, or some of the hand- some open work or embroidery one sees. The latter, however, is rather expensive.</p>
        <p>Mothers who make their children's clothes themselves, and have the good sense to prefer a simple style of dress, will find a neat and pretty frock given in my last sketch. The material used in the construction of the dainty little gown from which the sketch was made was a fine soft cashmere of a soft and delicate tone of apple-green. The embroidery with which it is trimmed had a cream ground em- broidered with hearts in green, the efiect being both original and striking. The skirt of the frock falls in full soft folds from the yoke, and the sleeves are #lll just above the wrists. where they are caught into bands. . Where possible, it will 1)3 found a gensible plan to make a growing child's frock with a good deep hem, and one or two tucks on the skirt to allow of letting it down when necessary. The sleeves also should have a tuck or two for the same reason. When the appearance of the frock would be spoiled if the tucks were let out, the skirt Can always b 3 lengthened by .the insertion of a band of trimming in the form of lace insertion or embroidery. I have seen some very pretty materials in the shops lately, made of a printed or woven border of an ormanental description. These can be got either in delaine or any cotton or muslin material,</p>
        <p>COQUErrst</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCILS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCILS</p>
        <p>The Wages of Attendants in the Market.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council was held in Bold-street Wesleyan Schoolroom on Tuesday evening,Mr..J. Edwardson pre- siding over a good attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>AR APPEAL FROM THE BLIND.</head>
        <p>AR APPEAL FROM THE BLIND.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY read a circular from the Executive Council of the National League of the Blind of Great Britain and Irelaild appealing for financial assistance on behalf of" blind workmen locked out by a charitable institution." The blind ship-fender makers employed at the Sunderland institute for the Blind, it was stated had been requested by the responsible officials of the institution to submit to a reduction of 27 per cent off the piece-work rates for their work. This the men naturally objected to especially since their average wages for the past year amounted to only 12s. per week The dispute had now lasted since October,l9o2 and the menvvere as determined asever to fight out their claim for the old piece-work rates. To fight and win this battle financial assistance was urgently required in order to keep these unfortunate men and conduct the dispute in an efficient manner, until by the force of public opinion they would be able to return to work under a guarantee from this charity of receiving just and humane treatment in future. _ _ _</p>
        <p>It was decided to refer the appeal to the branches.</p>
        <p>THE WAGES OF THE MARKET PLACE ATTENDANTS. "A SCANDAL AND A CRYING SHAME."</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk wrote acknowledging the receipt of a letter from the Seoretary protesting against the wages proposed to be paid to the attendants at the public con- veniences in the Market Place. He added that the communication would be laid before the Market Com- mittee. Mr. HAMBLETT considered that the Council ought to press the matter and moved that the Secretary write a further letter asking for a definite reply to the Council's resolution. Mr. WILLCOOKS thought the Council might leave the matter over for some time to see how the system worked. They had not obtained any information as to the rates of wages paid in other towns. Mr. HAMMETT suggested that the secretary should write to the secretaries of the Trades Council of Man- chesterand Liverpool asking for information as to the wages paid to the attendants in those-cities. The SECRETARY said he did not think they ought to consider what was paid in other towns but only whether the wages paid in Warrington were sufficient for the work done. If they were not satisfied that the wages were adequate they ought to protest against the pay: meat of them. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. FARGHER thought the Council should protest against the action of the Corporation and use what power they had to see that the Corporation set an ex- ample to other people in providing men with sufficient money to enable them to lead honest and upright lives. He seconded the resolution.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. MALONE said they required representatives on the Town Council to see that a living wage was.paid to workingmen. The action of the Corporation was a scandal and a crying shame, and the sooner.the Coutpcil was altered the better it would be for the working classes. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The propositi on was unanimously agreed to, but the Secretary was not instructed to write for information from Manchester and Liverpool.</p>
        <p>LORD PENRHYN AND HIS EMPLOYEES.</p>
        <p>The Council endorsed a resolution drawn up by the Bradford and District Trades and Labour Council, expressing regret at the failure of Sir Edward Clarke, K.C. and Mr. Llewellyn Williams to bring about a settlement of the dispute between the North Wale:, quarrymen and Lord Penrhyn, and urging the Govern- ment to enter a protest against the conduct of Lort Penrhyn "in thus refusing all overtures to a settlement either by conciliation or arbitration. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CORPORATION PRINTING CONTRACT..</head>
        <p>THE CORPORATION PRINTING CONTRACT..</p>
        <p>Mr. German, secretary of the Newton-le-Willow: Typographical Association, attended to address the meet- ing on this subject. As he declined to make any state meat in the presence of press representatives. it was decided to consider the matter in private, and the reporters withdrew.</p>
      </div>
