      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Tow.: "After all, what are kisses good for?' Kitty (demurely): "Their face value." "WHAT a sanguine man Cooper is !" "San- guine!" "He advertised for a middle-aged Opine .and expected to get applications." 44 No, sir," said a passenger on a steamship to the captain, "I am not seasick, but I am disgusted with the motion of this vessel."</p>
        <p>Mamma (sternly) : "Now, Rodney, have you anything to say before I whip you?" Little Rodney "I wish you'd let me have:laughing-gas before you begin the lickin'." MIIGGINS "Do you believe. it Us unlucky to have thirteen at table?" Jugging (who had callers at dinner-time): "Yes, if you've only made preparations for two." ELLA : "Fred and I wished with a wishbone last night." May: "Who won ? " Ella: "I don't know, and we didn't care particularly. He was vr;Thing for me, and I was wishing for him." TEA,:EIEE : "Did any of you ever see an elephant's skin?" Bright Boy : "Please, sir, I have." Teacher: " Where was it, my boy ?" Bright Boy : "On the elephant, sir." "I V:OUGHT your doctor forbade you to smoke?" "He did." "And yet you're smok- inQ." "Certainly." "How does it happen?" " Why, I changed doctors, of course." DR. SQUILLS "What was the matter with that %u driver you were called to see last night ? " or. Kallomell "As nearly as I can describe what isale him it is antomobiliousness."</p>
        <p>"I DON'T quite like tile shape of those shoes." "They conform precisely to the shape of your foot, madam, and nothing could be more beautiful than that." Sold.</p>
        <p>AFTER an inexperienced man has rowed a boat for about three-quarters of an hour, almost any kind of scenery is good enough for him to stop and admire,</p>
        <p>PUTS HIM IN A RAG.—MTS. Prosy: "Reading is quite a passion with my husband." Mrs. Dresser: "So it is with mine—when he Leads my milliner's bills!"</p>
        <p>"Is there any way of making that horse go?" inquired the sad-looking citizen. "Well," answered the dealer, after some reflection, "you might hitch him up to a motor-car." "James, how is it that my butcher's bills are so large, and I always have such bad dinners?" "Really, sir, I don't know, for I am sure we never have anything nice in the kitchen that we don't eend some of it up to the dining-room."</p>
        <p>LITTLE Deumirrra : "Mamma, can folks be put In prison for cheating a baby?" Mamma: "Why do you ask such a question ? " Little Daughter: "I saw Mrs. Nextdoor filling her baby's bottle, an, she put some water in the milk." Dix: "I understand that Winding, the counsel, is seriously iii." Hix ; "Yes, this morning, I met his physician who told me he was lying at death's door." Dix: "That's net like a lawyer. He'd lie anywhere." • WHEN aloud mother hears her offspring lisp its first articulate word, she does not, in her joy, stop to consider what a bore the child may be- come when he has fully become addicted to the talking habit. Wrza pleading eyes she looked up from the piano and sang: "Call me your darling again 1" But he refused, as there were witnesses about, and there is no telling when a man will be introduced to a breach of promise suit these days.</p>
        <p>MOTHER.: ."I wonder what we can do with Johnny? He has such a way of exaggerating everything. He is always making a mountain oat of a molehill." Father:. "I think, my dear, we had better make him an auctioneer."</p>
        <p>" Wmr do the roses fade slowly away?" she in- quired poetically, "Well," replied the bald-headed young man with wide ears, when you think it over, it's all for the best. It's more comfortable to have them fade slowly away than to go off all of a sudden like a torpedo." LADY (in employment office) : "As there are only my husband and myself in the family, I think you ought to be willing to come for less than you ask. There are only two persons to cook for." Domestic: "But, mum., when I'm wid you there'll be three."</p>
        <p>Amemous MAIDEN: "I think it is awfully mean. The editor sent my beautiful and pathetic story back without reading it." Fond Mother : "Dear me! How do you know?" Ambitious Maiden: "I've looked all through every page, and there isn't a teardrop anywhere." "WAS the wedding a success?" "I guess so. The bride's mother was in tears, the groom's mother went further and had hysterics, you couldn't hear a word of the ceremony, and the church was so crowded that three women fainted. Yes, it was a great euoce3s." QUAD: "Poets are born, not made, you know." Space: "Yes, of course. No person would think of disputing the assertion. Quad: " Why not I" Space: "It would be folly to manufacture an article when the supply was already greater than the demand."</p>
        <p>MISS HOJAcK (to the milkman): "Look here, Mr. Chawk, you warranted your milk pure, yet the lactemeter shows water in it." Mr. Chawk "Well, ma'am, I guarantee that the lactometer shows that the water is pure, if it says anything about it."</p>
        <p>OscE upon a time an American taunted an Englishman: "How can you endure to be taxed to support your idle nobility?" exclaimed the American warmly. Then the American paid £3 a ton for his coal in order that the directors of the company might procure titled sten for sons-in-law. This fable teaches that there are almost as many ways of paying taxes as of dodging the same. A CONSW4UENTIAL-LOOKING little man enterep the commercial-room of a hotel and gave a vigorous pull at the bell. As no one answered, he rang again more loudly than before. The maid-servant then came bustling in, and the following colloquy took place: Servant: "Who rang that bell ?" Little Man (making the most of his height) : "I did." Servant (scornfully): "And who lifted you up to it?" A VERY calculating young man has kept for two years an accurate account of every penny he has _spent in theatre tickets, carriages, flowers and gloves in connection with each lady he has invited out, inclusive of chaperons. On the credit side of that day book of pleasure is recorded his estimates of the satisfaction tue lady gave him in return for the money expended. This prudent man ;ust turns to his account-book before inviting, and by comparing the record sees from which charmer he gets most for his money ! "So you refuse to give me the money?" said the prodigal sod. "Not another penny do you get," replied the stern parent. "Then here goes," said the youth, as he seized a silver- mounted pistol from his father's desk. " Un- happy boy?" cried the old man, "would you tak-.; your life?" "No," replied the eon, "I'm going to lend this to my ' uncle.' " "I THOUGHT you used to say that you wouldn't care to be tied to Miss Wheeler, if she were the laet girl in the world; but I see that you're paying a good deal of attention to her lately," said Brown to his friend Green. "Yes," said Green," my opinion of her has undergone a com- plete change. I used to think that she would not make a good wtfa for a poor man, because she seemed to be lazy." " Aud now?" asked Brown. "Why, look at her bicycle I There isn't another girl in town who keeps her wheel in such fine condition."</p>
        <p>A TEACHER, wiAing to impress upon the minds of her pupils the fall nature of faith, took them one day to the river-side, and, seeing a boat in the middle of the stream, said: "Now, my little dears, if I were to tell you that there was a leg of mutton in tbac boat, would you believe me?" "Yes." "Well, then," she said, " that is faith." Seme time after the same children were going through an examination, when the question was asked, "What is faith?" and all the class, as with one voice, shouted out: "A leg of mutton in a boat."</p>
        <p>Hz stopped at the corner, gazed into the sky, scratched his chin, and pulled his whiskers. Then he fumbled ie his pocket, wrinkled his brow, pursed up his lips, and blew his nose in a manner more deliberate than the collector of bad debt. Anon he opened his mouth, tugged at his lower lip, muttered to himself, and fixed his eyes on the chimney•tops in a vacant, stony stare, that soon collected a crowd, which blockaded the pavement. He was a married man, trying to think what it was his wife had charzed him to be sure and get.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>TELEPHONE 355 Y.</head>
