      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS. Interesting Manceuvres at Frodsham.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEERS. Interesting Manceuvres at Frodsham.</p>
        <p>The country in the vicinity of Frodsham was the scene of some very interesting Volunteer Manceuvres on Saturday, the volunteers taking part being two com- panies of infantry of the 2nd V.B. Cheshire Regiment, and one company of mounted infantry, one company of cyclists .and two companies of infantry of the Ist V.B. South fancashire Regiment. The "general idea" was that a western force was at Chester with an advanced postat Frodshara.MAn,eastern force was known to be advancing from -Manchester, and its advance guard was reputed to have reached Warrington at nine p.m., on the 10th April. The ',Warrington ',Vditmteers were the attack- ing force and were divided into two sec- tions. Captain Crosfleld was in command. The Cyclists, the I. Company, and a Provisional Com- pany assembled at the Drill Hall at eight o'clock, and rations were served out for the day. At 8.30 the men left for Walton Lock, where the boat was in waiting to take them to Ice. The cyclists were under the com- mand of Captain Haelam, the I Company under the command of Captain Roberts, and the Provisional Com- pany under the command of Captain R. Smith. They were embarked at 9.5, and were put in the hold, the ladder being removed, scethat the men could not show themselves. Ince Pier was reached at about 10.45,when I the cyclists immediately pushed on through Alvanleyto , Shepherd's Houses to occupy a wood, covering the,ad- ' vance of the infantry. This they held until the infantry I arrived, about 1.30. when they retired and made a big ' turning movement by Frodsham Golf Links, and took up a position covering the whole, waiting a general ad- vance. In the meantime Captain Crosfleld steadily pushed his men forward, sending half his command to take,up a position by Mersey View. The remainder made a frontal attack on the quarry. As soon as the general advance was sounded the attack proceeded from all sides. The infantry were extended, and each section was steadily pushed forward. The cyclists from the other side, pushed forward one section, which was un- fortunately putt out of action, and the remainingsection, which was waiting in 'reserve, then advanced on the quarry. Major Harrison, believing that all the cyclists were out of action' left the hill in charge of a few men, and the result was that it was easily captured by the cyclists, who were supported by G Company (Newton) under Second-Lieutenant Pillion-Smith, which had journeyed to Halton by train. The Mounted Infantry, numbering 32, left the Riding School at Warrington at 8.45, and, under the direction of Lieutenant E. Ridgway, proceeded to Frodsham by a circuitous route down the Northwich road. The object was, of course, to deceive the enemy as to the direction of attack. The company arrived at Sutton Hall Farm, a mile from Sbtton turn-bridge, at 10-45. The men dis- mounted, the horses were hidden from view, and orders issued to the various section commanders. G Company (Newton) of 25 men, under the command of Second- Lieutenrnt Fulton Smith. proceeded through the woods near the farm in the direction of the river, their object being to make a feint attack on the enemy's front. They micceeded in gaining the river bank, and were opposed by a considerable number of the enemy, who were supposed to have blown up the locks, which prevented them being crossed. Lieutenant Ridgway then sent three sections of Mounted Infantry under the command of Lieutenant P. N. Avery, in support of G Company, whom they joined at the river. The crossing was a very difficult matter, owing to the bridges "having been blown up," and on account of the Weaver and the whole canal joining at this point. After making several feint attacks within the radius allowed it was decided to endeavour to cross the water at a point about 800 yards from the Sutton turnbridee. A large boat was commaneleetred by the Mountedlnfantry Company,and the G Cempany was then put across the river, the Mounted Infantry opening a heavy fire as they advanced inopen order across open fields to Frodsham hills. As soon as the party landed a maxim, holding a good position on a hill at a distance of 700 yards from the river, opened ft heavy fire on the advancing party. The Mounted In- fantry then crossed at the same point and took up a good position on the opposite side of the river, almost immediately silencing the advanced posts of the enemy, which were on the right and left of the attack. The advance was steadily carried on in open order, the Mounted Infantry showing a great tact and presence of mind in taking ac4antage in every cover in passing through the,epen fields towards Frodsham hills. They succeeded in gaining a position within half a mile'of Frodsham hills by one o'clock. G Company were then ordered to make the best of their way to the golf links and were Instructed to join in the main attack when the .advance was sounded at two o'clock. The Mounted Infantry worked north-east towards Frodshairt Bridge and Sutton Bridge, their object being to make the enemy retire from that position, and thus enable the horses, which were stationed at Sutton Hall Farm, to recross and join the main attack. The result of these manceuvres was that from 15 to 20 of the Cheshire Volunteers were put out of action, the loss forcing them to retire on their main position. The losses of the Mounted Infantry and the G Company were calculated at one in four. The attack was made very difficult indeed owing to the G and the Mounted Infantry Companies having to cross the rivers, the bridges having to be taken by force before a crossing could be effected. Unfortunately that took up a great deal of tithe, and consequently the Mounted Infantry, were about half an hour too late to join in the main at- tack, although at the time it was delivered they were well within firing distance of the enemy, and had,appar- ently, not been seen by them. Captain Goren, of the Sonth Lancashire Regiment, was the chief umpire, and his assistant umpires were Major Edelsten and Captain B. Fairclough. Addressing the officers at the termination of the manceuures on Frodsham Hill, Captain Goren expressed himself well satisfied with the disposition and behavi- our of the men on both sides.</p>
        <p>The result of the manceuvres was left an open ques- tion.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN COUNCIL. Annual Meeting. The annual meeting of the above Council was held on Thursday at the Town Hall under the presidency of Mr. S. Taylor. Present :—Messzs. W. Handley, D.Bisbrown, .T. H. Salkeld, M. J. Sweetman, J. J.Shaw,T. Hewitt,T. Jones, E. Imison S. Walton, J. H. Lightburn,E. Gatley, .J. H. Kirkham, S. Wilson, R. H. Posnett, F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (secretary), J. Wilding (surveyor). ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN.' •I Mr. R. H. Posnett said it was his privilege to move that Mr. Taylor be re-elected to the position of chair- man of the Council. It gave him great pleasure to move the resolution. Everyone would feel that in honouring Mr. Taylor they were honouring themselves. The last ' year had been a very trying one, but Mr. Taylor had led the town with credit to himself and satisfaction to every right-minded person. Many important questions would have to be faced during the coming year, and the Council had done the wisest thing in allowing Mr. Taylor to guide them. Mr. Imison in seconding said that he seconded the motion with the greatest possible pleasure. Last year was an anxious time but he hoped Mr. Taylor would this year be guiding the ship through smoother waters. Mr. Taylor did not want the position, for he needed recreation and rest. They were therefore all the more ' grateful to him for accepting the position. He hoped he would be spared for many years to occupy a seat on the Council even though it were not the central one. } Mr. Pritchard supported and eulogised the business ability of Mr. Taylor. The Council had departed from its usual practice of electing a new chairman but cir- cumstances warranted the departure. Mr. Taylor thanked the Council for re-electing him. Had he consulted his own pleasure he would not have taken the seat again. He promised to do his best for ' the town and the Council. (Applause.) THE VICE-CHAIRMAN. On the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. • Wiswall, Mr. Posnett was re-elected vice-chairman. THE COMM ITTEES. The members of the Standing Committees were , re-appointed. ' The Technical Committee was reappointed en bloc the Rev. Don Levi taking the place of Father Cham- bers. The Library Committee was re-appointed, Mr. Couche, Weston-road, taking the place of Mr. Once and Dr. Ormsby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EDUCATION COM MITT E E.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION COM MITT E E.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilson said that his Committee (Technical Instruction) had been in communication with Mr. Ward, of Crewe, with the result that the following resolution had been framed for the Cpuncil's approval "Resolved that the Board of Education be Informed that the Urban District Council of Runcorn have decided that they will avail themselves of the concur- rent powers given by Section 3 of the Education Act, 1902. Further resolved, that the Education Committee is for the present unnecessary, but that the Council reserves the right to make a scheme for the approval of the Board of Education if necessary or desirable. Dr. Lightburn seconded,and the metion was approved rl'aman'r SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE. olsolemah</p>
