      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, April 17, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, April 17, 1903.</p>
        <p>ing him intimately he (Mr. Gunnell) could not but conclude that if Mr. Garnett had had his choice he would have preferred so to pass away. Mr. Garnett was full of occupation almost up to the last hour. The morning was spent in visiting a number of his friends; in the afternoon he had had his usual rest, then a quiet time], in the Conservatory with his New Testament, followed by a stroll among his figiwers, then a drive into the country that he loved !to intelligently all through his life; then came the end. He (Mr. Gunnell) did not claim that Mr. Garnett was perfect. He was a good master, 'considerate to his workmen and intensely interested in everything that touched their welfare. Scores of times in his (Mr. Gunnell's) hearing he had made most appreciative remarks about his old ser- vants who served him so loyally and so long. They had in him the embodiment of a lofty Christian principli with an earnest endeavour to serve his God and his generation and now that the end had come they would place him in his quiet resting place, leaving his spirit with the God who gave it. Thehymn " Give me the wings of faith to rise "was sung and as the mourners left the chapel the organist played " 0 rest in the Lord." The oorthge which was headed by 'Vies 4rs. Robert Garnett and Sons' employs, the employ&amp; of the Penketh Tanning Company, Lt 4. (of which the deceased was at the time of his death chairman),andthe teachers of the Wesleyan Sunday School then proceeded to St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, where another large con- gregation had assembled. In addition to those whose names have already been given there were present Alderman Hutchinson, Councillor Rogers, Mr. William Owen,Mr. W. D. Jeans, Mr. T. Tunstall, Mr. C. Jackson Hohnes,Mr. Geo rge Clarke,Mr. Thomas Birtles,Mr.Tonge and a representative of the United Kingdom Alliance., A short service was conducted by the Rev. J. R. Jones and the lesson from the Church of England burial ser- vice was read by the Rev. John Gunnell. The hymn "Brief life is here our portion " was sung, and Miss Standish who presided at the organ played the Dead March in" Saul." After the last rites at the graveside the deceased's favourite hymn "Rock of Ages" was sung. The grave, which was lined with greeneiy and flowers, is situated at the north end of the graveyard on .the side nearest the station. The coffin v4s of fumed oak with brown oak panels, and it bore the simple in- scription :—" Robert Garnett, died April 9, 1903, in his 73rd year." All the bearers engaged were either in the service of Messrs. R. Garnett and Sons or the Penketh Tanning Company, and the whole of the funeral arrange- ments were carried out under the direction of Mr. Wm. Nicholson. The carriages were supplied by Mr. Ed. Connor.</p>
        <p>In addition to beautiful floral trib-utes forwarded by relatives and friends, others had been sent by the teachers and officers of Penketh Wesleyan Sunday School, the members of Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel, the committee and workers of the Warrington Ragged Sunday School, employs in the sewing room at Messrs. Garnett's, the Penketh Tanning Co., and the clerical department of Messrs. R. Garnett and Sons, namely, Messrs. P. H. Brown, D. Hodge, I. Newton, G. F. Cousens, W. Johnstone. W. Nicholson, and R. Sewell</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. ROBERT GARNETT, J.P. Biographical Sketch.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. ROBERT GARNETT, J.P. Biographical Sketch.</p>
        <p>It is with deep regret we record the death on‘Thursday last week of Mr. Robert Garnett, J.P., of Hall Nook, Penketh, at the advanced age of 72 years. The deceased gentleman had been in failing health for some time. Ten years ago he was seized with a stroke, and although he practically recovered, he had been more or less of an invalid ever since. On Thursday, he took advantage of the bright and warm weather to visit some friends, and dined at a friend's house. On returning home, however, he was suddenly takenill, and passed away peacefully between 8 and 9 o'clock in the evening. _ _</p>
        <p>The deceased gentleman was the eldest son of the late Mr. Robert Garnett, of Penketh, founder of the well-</p>
        <p>known firm of Messrs. Robert Garnett and Son,cabinet makers and upholsterers. His life was spent at Penketh and Warrington and he had been a well-known figure in the district practically the whole of his life. In 1885 he was appointed a justice of the peace for the Borough by the Chancellor of the Duchy, and for some time he was a member of the Board of Guardians for the War- rington Union and also of the now defunct Highway Board. A strong believer in the temperance cause he was always ready to lend his assistance to the movement. In all work which had for its object the uplighting of the people, he was always a warm-hearted supporter, but it was for his work in connection with the Wesleyan body that he was best known. In Wesleyan circles his name was a household word for more than a generation and both financially, and in other ways he was a most loyal supporter. He held practically every office a layman could hold in the Warrington Wesleyan circuit, and to Penketh and Sankey Bridges Wesleyan chapels in par- ticular, he and his family have been de- voted friends. At Penketh Chapel he was a class leader and held several o ficel in con nection with that place of worship. He was also an ardent worker in the Sunday school until his health broke down. His deeds of charity in Penketh were many and he was probably the most familiar figure in the village. Not only does the Wesleyan Chapel bear evidence of his gifts to beautify and embellish that place of worship, but the village itself has on many occasions benefited by his generosity. He was exceed- ingly. fond of travel and accompanied by- his wife he visited the Continent Egypt and Palestine. Both he and his wife delivered highly interesting lectures on their journeyings. He leaves a widow two sons and four daughters. Mrs. Garnett is a very ace ept- able speaker on religious, temperance and,' political topics and her second daughter is the wife of Mr. C. E. Parker,the president of the Liberal xssociation.Wide and varied is the circle from which will flow towards Mrs. Garnett and family, a stream of heart-felt sympathy in their sorrowful bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Impressive Scenes at Penketh and Sankey.</p>
        <p>The impressive scenes witnessed at the funeral of the late Mr. Robert Garnett, J.P., which took place in the peaceful churchyard at Great Sankey on Monday after- noon, were a striking tribute to the respect in which the deceased gentleman was held by all classes of the com- munity and the profound regret occasioned by his death. By few will Mr. Garnett be more greatly missed than by the inhabitants of Penketh—the village in which he had resided for so many years and where so much of that good work which hadl endeared him to all was done. On Monday afternoon an air of sadness hung over the village and on every side were signs of mourning, blinds being lowered and black being worn by many people. As we reported in our last issue, the deceased gentleman's death came with startling suddenness. He drove to town with his coach- man on Thursday morning and dined at Hall Nook at one o'clock. During the afternoon he spent some time in his much loved garden, and at four o'clock went out for a drive. Very shortly after leaving Hall Nook the coachman noticed that Mr. Garnett was seized with an attack of apoplexy. He was taken home and lingered unconscious until nine o'clock when he passed away. It Was three years ago that the deceased gentleman was first seized with apoplexy, andnot ten years as westated last week. Mr. Garnett did not dine out at a friend's house on Thursday. The first part of the funeral service was conducted in Penketh Wesleyan Chapel with which Mr. Garnett had been prominently identified for many years. The building was filled with sorrowing relatives, friends, and residents of Penketh, and the scene was once of impressive solemnity. The rostrum had been draped in purple and black, whilst some choice white flowers, palms, S:c., somewhat relieved the sombre appearance of the chapel. The funeral procession reached the chapel at about two o'clock, and as the coffin was borne along one of the aisles the Rev. G. H. GLrnson read the opening sentences of the burial service. The following is a list of the mourners :-Ist carriage, Revs. G. H. Carnson and R. Wilkins Rees, and Dr. Murray; 2nd carriage, the Rev. John Gunnell ; 3rd carriage, Mrs. Robert Garnett (widow), Mr. R. E. Garnett (son), and Mr. and Mrs. C. Doeg (son-in-law and daughter); 4th carriage, Mr and Mrs.C.E. Parker (son-in-law and daughter), Mr. and Mrs. R. H. Posnett (son-in-law and daughter) ; sth carriage, Mr. and Mrs. C. Heal (son-in-law and daughter), Mr. and Mrs. P. C. Garnett (son and daughter-in-law); 6th carriage, Mr. and Mrs. David Garnett, Mrs. Coop and Mrs. Fildes (sisters); 7th carriage, Mr. Riley, the Misses M. and R. Parker, Master Sheffield Doeg, and Miss Rosalie Garnett; Bth carriage, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Smith (Chester), Miss Moses (union), and Mr. J. G. Withinshaw ; 9th carriage, Mr. Gray, Mr. Bradshaw, Mr. Carrick, and Mr. Thomp- son ; 10th carriage, Mrs. and Miss Hurter, and Mr. and Mrs. T. Gandy. Amongst others in the chapel were the Rev. J. R. Jones, the Rev. John Yonge, the Rev. J. Howard, Mr. F. Monks, Ald. Monks, Councillors A. Bennett, Wm. Roberts and James Dodds, Mr. and Mrs. H. Roberts,Mr. and Mrs. John Fairclough, Messrs. Sydney, Alfred and Ernest Garnett (nephews), Mrs. Buckley, .Miss R. Garnett, Miss D. Garnett (nieces), Mr. and Mrs. Janies Almaden (Wigan), :Mrs. Bowyer, Mr. C. Mitchell, Mr. Peter Thompson, Messrs. Edwin Richardson, R. G. Henshall, A. H. Dunn, George Bayley and .T. Parkinson (Wesleyan officials), Isaac Johnson, Alfred Damplugh, S. B. Stringer, M. Osborne and W. Light (local preachers), W. J. Wigfield, W. Wigfield, J. T. Ambler, J. A. Smith, J. Watson, Highley, J.Coradine G. James, W. Furness, John Gandy, J. Hall, Wilkinson, B. Aitken, John Dignum, C. E. Roylance, Isaac Mills, W. Hodgkinson, J. Atherton, R Hobson, F. Dixon, T. Swift, Isaac Hough, R. Sewall, W. Bennett, John Bennett,.John Jackson, F. Hughes, E. Seed, John Taylor, A. 13. Moore, of Urmston (representing the Man- chester Field Naturalists and Archmological Society, of which the deceased was a vice-president), Henry Plummer (secretary of the Wesleyan Methodist Trust Assurance Company, Ltd., Manchester, in which the deceased had long taken a deep interest), Ed. Brunner (vice-consul for Russia), J. P. Leach, J.P. (Huyton Park), Dr. Murray (chairman), Mr. E. W. Clarke Mr. J. S. Snoddy, Mr. James Millington, and Mr. E. 'Foster (clerk), representing the Penketh Parish Council, of which the deceased was a member at the time of his death; Messrs. J. W. Howarth and Marsh, and Miss Monks (representing the Ragged School at Warrington, , of which the deceased was a vice-president) • Mrs. James Dodds, Miss Bessie Dutton, Mrs. John Hartley,: Miss Hartley, Mrs. W. J. Dann, Miss Benson, &amp;c., &amp;c. The Warrington County Borough Police were repre- sented by Inspector Rowbottam and seven men, and the: County Police by Constables Houghton and Dugdale. A psalm was read by the Rev. G. H. Carnson and the ' Rev. R. Wilkins Rees read the lesson commencing with the words "Let not your hearts be troubled." The hymn "0 God, our help in ages past" was then sung, Mr. , Wigfield, jun., presiding at the organ, after which The Rev. John Gunnell delivered a brief address. He said another opportunitywould be afforded some of them before very long of doing fuller justice to the memory of their dear departed friend. He would, however, like to direct the thoughts of the bereaved relatives to the bright lining to the cloud which had overshadowed their lives. They did not sorrow without hope; they dared not do so. That was no occasion for hopeless sorrow. There was very much, when they looked into the char- acter of their friend who had gone from them, to lift up their hearts in thankfulness to God for so great a gift. They thought for example of his honourable and suc- cessful business career, stretching over a period of nearly 60 years. They 'thought, of his close and loyal attachment to that church for well-nigh half a century, of that bright, happy home life of more than 40 years duration. They thought also of the services he had rendered to the district as a justice of the peace for nearly 20 years, and they called to mind his innumer- able acts of unostentatious kindness on all sorts of occa- sions to all sorts andconditions of man. As they glanced at these things they dare not sorrow without hope. It mattered little at what hour of the day the righteous man fell asleep. Death could not come to him untimely, because he was fit to diet Mr. Garnett had fought a good fight, he had finished his course, he had kept the faith, land there was laid up for him a crown of righteousness, I a crown, indeed, which he had already received. The Ispeaker allnded to the deceased's illness, extending over a period of three years and a half, an illness which, he I said, had its bright side. Though there was very much I of weakness and weariness there was very little acute I pain. What he had was not prolonged, and he had the use of his mental faculties, and occasionally there were flashes of the old pleasantry and humour to brighten the family circle and make life a sweeter and I a brighter thing. Only last Sunday week he was in that place of worship morning and evening taking as intelligent a part in the entire service, as any other member of the congregation. Death came with startling suddenness and onecould not but be in tender sympathy with those who witnessed the last scene. But it was not without its mercy. How much harder it would have been for those who loved him had Mr. Garnett been shut up within a sick chamber for months and even years, semi-conscious, in constant suffering. No, translation of the sort he had named was better than that. Know-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Reference at the Police Court.</head>
        <p>Reference at the Police Court.</p>
        <p>Before the commencement of the business at the War- rington Police Court on Saturday, the Chairman (Mr. T. Birchall) made an allusion to the death of Mr. Robert Garnett, J.P. He said Mr. Garnett was a very worthy colleague of theirs, and had been foremost in all good works in Warrington. For many years—much longer than he (Mr. Birchall)—he had been a member of that Bench, and he was sure it was with great regret that the magistrates heard of his death. It was not unexpected —Mr. Garnett had passed the allotted span—but he was a man whom they would miss, a man whom they esteemed, and even at this time they regretted to have to lose him.</p>
        <p>Mr. It. T. Fairclough endorsed all that Mr. Birchall had said. He had known Mr. Garnett for many years and he thought he was a most estimable and a very worthy man. He had done a great deal of good for the town—more than most people knew of. He had not only done it publicly but had done very much privately.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>PULPIT REFERENCE'S.</head>
        <p>PULPIT REFERENCE'S.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees made a reference to the death of Mr. Robert Garnett at Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday morning. He said it was but appropriate that from that pulpit a trib,ute of respect and affection should be paid to the memory of amen who for so many years had been one of the most valued workers and prominent officials in the circuit, at the head of which stood the church in which they were then assembled. It could not possibly be doubted that Methodism gener- ally, but especially in the locality of Warrington owed much to the gifts and laboars of Mr. Warrington, but he (the preacher , felt sure that such was the high esteem in which their departed friend held the church of his choice that he would have been the first to have said that he owed far more to the influence of Methodism than Methodism owed to him. His genuine pride in and unflinching loyalty to Methodism could not for a momentbe questioned, andhe had never failed to show with unmistakable clearness his attach- ment to the cause to which he had readily given his hand and his heart. He was certainly a most intelligent and enthusiastic Methodist. People who did notreally I understand him might have thought at times that he was stern, hard, and even perhaps, exacting, but thosa who got into touch with him found that he was not only a man of strength, but also a man of real sincerity, of generous sympathies, and of a. warm and loving heart. Of late years, too, it must have been evident to those who knew him that a beautiful mellowing influ- enee had been at work in his character and disposition. He had many admirable traits which they could not hesitate gladly to recognize. He was a man of true simplicity of life, and that fact deserved to be noted with emphatic approval, especially in days like the pre- sent. He had a keen and thoughtful love for nature, which showed itself in many delightful ways ; he also loved children, and was loved by •Ithem • and any man who cared for nature and chit' dren, as he undoubtedly did, possessed some most desirable characteristics. He had a deep and abiding affection for every means of grace, never failing, when it was at all possible, to attend with the utmost punctuality and regularity every service of his church. Notwithstanding the great bodily infirmities from which of late years he suffered, he was constantly found in hisaccustomed place in the sanctuary,devoutly and intelligently joining in the worship, and even as recently as the two last Sundays before his death he was present at each service. To the cry of distress he ever lent a willing ear and gave a helpful response; his gifts indeed were such that people who only knew of generosity when it was not publicly announced could not possibly be aware of their nature and extent: Above all he had an _absorbing love for and a whole-hearted and simple faith in Jesus Christ. He was not a perfect man—he himself would neverhave claimed that he was—but, surely, he was in many res- pects a good Christian. He was prepared for the end; ready to see the Master Lace to face; and his death was an urgent appeal to them, so to live that not even the most sudden manifestation•of the nearness of God should ever take them by surprise. At the evening service the Rev. G. H. Carnson spoke in somewhat similar terms of the character of Mr. Gar- nett, and the loss the circuit had sustained by his death. The Rev. R. Wilkins Rees also referred to the same sub- ject at Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Local Druidism. Bro, James Ray (Grand Master) presiled at the quarterly meeting of the Warrington Equalized Dis- trict of the Order of Druids which was held at the ' Registered Office, Corporation-street. In opening the meeting, the Grand Master, referred to the loss the members of Sir Gilbert Greenall Lodge had sustained by the death of Bro. Smith Burgess, P.N.G.A. He spoke highly of the valuable assistance Bro. Burgess had been to the lodge since its inception, and felt sure all would approve of the suggestion to forward a latter of sympathy and condolence to the widow ond family on behalf of the district. The Vice-Grand Master seconded the motion, wl4ch was carried .in silence. The names of new members initiated during the quarter were submitted by the District Secretary, being a total of 47, at an average age of 22 years. The balance sheet for the quarter and the auditor's report were read, and the officers thanked for their services. It was pointed out that the sickness had been heavier than the average for . some years,. , £222 10s. having been paid out, and :£7O - for • death claims. Still when the receipts for the quarter were taken into account it left the funds with- out a loss. A. letter was read from the Town Clerk inviting the district to appoint representatives to attend a meeting at the Town Hall on the 20th inst. to elect a member for the. Education Committee. Bro. Pooley explained how the misunderstanding appeared to have Arisen in the nomination of the Friendly Societies Council not being accepted by the Town Council. It was resolved "That this meeting con- siders the action of the Friendly Societies Council right and proper, but being most anxious that the friendly societies should be united and work in har- mony, we proceed to appoint otir representatives to attend that meeting." The following were appointed —Bro. -Ray, G.M.,8r0. John Bibby, V.G.A. Bro. D Houghton, D.C.&amp; , Bro. Wm. Bibby, P. D. M., and Bro. Pooley, P.D.G.M. It was decided that two dele- gates be sent to the annual meeting of the order to be held at Hull in Whit-week, and Bro. Houghton, and Bro. Pooley were appointed. Several members wished Bro. Houghton success in his candidature for the office of Vice Grand Master of the order; also in his resolution that the annual delegation b. 6 held in War- rington in 1901. Bro. Houghton thanked the meet- ing for their good wishes, and promised that they should know the result as early as possible. A. hearty vote of thanks was accorded the Grand Master for presiding. FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KiNG OF TONI CS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. By —O-r-a—list."</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. By —O-r-a—list."</p>
        <p>Warrington have, after all, failed to fulfil expecta- tions. The Easter programme finished disastrously, for no other description will apply to a defeat'at home by Wigan. I did expect .Warrington to assert their superiority on this occasion, if only to prove what they could have done at Wigan on, a dry ground. Instead, however, they failed to score and were beaten in the last minute by a dropped goal. They deserved their fate too, for the wearers of prim- rose and blue have seldom put up a worse exhibition.</p>
        <p>The locals apparently shot their bolt on Good Friday when Halifax were conquered. \At any rate the for- wards had just about done all they were capable of. Against Widnes on Saturday they cut up very badly, and if the visitors had not been short of Hughes during the major portion of the game they might have proved too good for the locals. -As it was, Warrington won by the respectable margin of 8 points to nil, 'Harris and Fish getting the tries, though the latter was really responsible for both. Widnes played with con- siderable dash, but. were inferior to Warrington behind. The home backs, however, did not impress one very favourably, and °lithe day's forrn,the Wigan engagement did not appear the sweet thing it should be.</p>
        <p>Wicines had the advantage of the wind in the first half, and both forwards and backs soon gave I taste of their quality. • A round of passing by the Widnes backs would in all probability have resulted in some- thing tangible if the pass to Peacock had not been thrown forward. Hughes was nest prominent in a dribble which looked exceedingly dangerous until Hallam saved cleverly. Widnes were unfortunate in. losing the assistance of Hughes, who retired in conse- quence of a twisted knee. • Prior to his retirement, Widnes had done the whole of the pressing, but when Aspey was brought out of the pack to play on the wing, matters were made a little easier for the Warrington vanguard, who up to now had been overplayed. The Warrington backs got down to the Widnes end and Harris and Fish were each grounded when near the line. When the teams I turned.round neither side had scored. With the wind in their favour and only fourteen men to face the issue seemed a certainty for Warrington. It was not, however, until the second half was ' well advanced that Fish got.awa,y nicely on the wing and kicking across tO Harris that player scored a try. Fish added two more points with a well-judged kick and a little later he crossed the Widnes line himself after a clever dodgy run. This settled the match and WarringtOn retired victors of a game in which they had been largely assisted by good fortune.</p>
        <p>The result of the Wigan match was as muck a surprise to the Warrington spectators as to the visi- tors from Coalopolis. I believe it is something like fourteen years since the Wigan team registered a victory on the Wilderspool enclosure. Few expected that they would get three points at the expense of Warrington this season but the unexpected happened arrd the Wiganers returned home rejoicing on Monday with a brace of points which were all the more wel- come as they secured :or the team a place in the Northern League Senior Competition next season. Neither Brighouse nor St. Helens can now dispossess the Wiganers from the Northern League. The only score was a dropped goal and this only came in the last minute of the match. The Warrington backs disappointed everybody. The spectators hardly expected that the forwards would be a match for the burly Wigan vanguard, but few anticipated that the backs would cut such a sorry figure. The passing wa.4 a pitiful exhibition and Wigan had little difficulty in keeping their lines intact. Even when playing against the wind, Wigan made brief incursions into the Warrington half, and the last one proved fatal to Warrington's hopes of making a draw of the match, for Bothwell picked up in the Warrington 25 and dropped a capital goal. In the forwards, Wigan had a decided advantage, and as the Warrington backs played below par, the Wigan men did not compare unfavourably with them.</p>
        <p>On Monday the home front rank was pretty well smothered by the burly colliers, and the backs were also poor. Their combination was quite at sea, the men missinthe ball in most unaccountable fashion. Perhaps their hands were cold! It is more likely, though, that their feet were heavy. Wigan were the better team on the day's plfo awl merited the two points they somewhat luckily obtained'. N 3 doubt they were very ivelcom'3, for the ("nitro' Park club's name figures unpleasantly near to those of the candi- dates for the second division.</p>
        <p>The moral of the two games was obvious—and as usual. Better forwards will have to be secured for another season or else the team will assuredly. go down. It is remarkable how well they have recovered in the last two months, and the bulk or the credit goes to the backs,who have won games when the forwards were hopelessly beaten. The ~Hunslet match is an instance of - this. The backs are altogether too severely w wked, and it Must be disheartening to them to have to play against such odds week alter week. They cannot be expected to show their .best form in any other than a spasmodic fashion, ' and, as I have said, the wonder is they have done so well.</p>
        <p>• The only consolation for the Warringtonians just now is that, as a result of recent SUCC3SseS, the Club occupies a creditable position in the League table. The prophecy of those who were hopeful when things where going badly appears certain to be fulfilled. Warrington have again boDked the middle of the. League as their final resting place. Just now they are one place better than the middle, having eight clubs above them and nine below. The number of points obtained is also greater than ever before in the League history of the club. For the first time War- rington are in the "thirties," and the aggregate works out at exactly a point per match. This is another Wilderspool speciality in League statistics.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows For Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Salford 31...20... 7...4...30 58-234...20 19— 97...41 Halifax 31-21—, 8...2...28 39-173...10 10— 80...11 Bro'ght'n R 51-217_20 19— 97...39 Swinton 32...16... 9...7...37 52-230...25 23-119_31 Oldham 31...19...12...0...25 41-182...21 20-102...38 Runcorn 31...17...10...4...33 52-222...22 28-125...38 Hunslet 32-16-13_3_44 29-175...31 48-206-35 Bradford 32...13_13_4_32 44-196-28 30-146_31 Warrington 32...13...13...0...22 29-131...27 32-150...32 Hull • 31...14-15-2-23 46-184-29 35-103_30 Batley 31_13_14_1_31 31-164...33 41-189...30 Huddersileld32...l3-17-2...13 29-113...31 41-185...28 Leigh 31...11...15...5...29 19-115...23 37-157...27 Widnes 32...13...18...1...21 25-125...23 38-154...27 Hull lt. R. 31...12...17...2...18 32-132...35 41-202_23 Wigan 31... 9...16...6...25 21-113-27 35-159...21 Brighouse R 31.. 7-22...3-17 12— 70...42 53-250...17 St Helens 32... 7...23...2...29 18-112...39 75-303...16 As my readers are well aware, the (tames with Widnes and Wigan counted in the South West Lanca- shire Competition. By the results obtained,Warring- ton have left no room for doubt as to the destination of the Cup—it goes to Runcorn. The defeat by Wigan destroyed Warrington's last chance, and it was only a slender one. The Linnets em n now afford to lose their last match—also with Wigan-,and . still remain champions. They are not likely, however, to do anything so silly as to drop . points in either League table if they can help it. •</p>
        <p>The positions of the clubs in the South-West Lancashire' League table now stand as follows Points. P. W. L. D. For Agst. F'ts 9 ... 5 ... 1 3 ... 61— 7...1: 10 ... 5 ... 3 ... 2 ... 55 42 ...12 8 ... 3 10 ... 3 ... 9 ... -1 . .. 5 ... 8 ... 1 ... G 1... 19 -- 75 ... 3</p>
        <p>R tincorn Warrington Wigan Leigh Widnes St. Helens</p>
        <p>Warrington 'play their last away match of the sea- son this Saturday, Bradford baing the destination of the team. • Broadley's men triumphed somewhat easily at Warrington, but they will need to play well to win this time. Having got over the strain of the holiday matches Warrington should be in form again, and if so they will extend their burly opponents con- siderably. There is only one change in the Warring- ton side from that which has recently done duty, Naylor taking the place of Harmer in the forwards.</p>
        <p>The Club benefited financially to a most gratifying extent during the holidays, the takings for the three matches amounting tl 4L155, a sum which has relieved the club treasurer of any anxiety as to the report at the end of the season.</p>
        <p>Cycling. , As usual North Wales was visited by the members of the Warrington Cycling Club during the Easter holidays. One section made Barmouth their head-</p>
        <p>quarters, and spent a very good tim. laother see tion went all round North Wales in motor-cars, and- although so much snow and hail fell on Sunday and Monday still the roads were most enjoyable to travel on. The largest section made Llangollen their head- quarters, and in spite of the extreme severity of the weather, managed to get some good fun out of the holiday, and visited most of the picturesque spots in the lovely vale. Good Friday was spent at Corwen, and the weather being glorious,a jolly time was spent, the presence of several of the Manchester Wheelers helping to swell the merriment. Saturday was devoted to Llangollen and district. Sunday was spent •in going over the Golden Valley side of the Dee, and returning by the high road, and it can be safely asserted that it was the first time in the Easter tour of the club that the. members gathered pritnroses and pelted themselves with snowballs on the .same after- noon: Monday saw the party back in Warrington, they having left the Berwyn mountains .covered with a snowy wreath to the depth of several inches.</p>
        <p>At New Brightin, on Monday, C. •Berry, of Wai- ringtou, won the mile bicycle race by a foot from the 175 yards mark in 2mins. 31secs,</p>
        <p>Angling.</p>
        <p>The great change which clne ov3r the weather during the Eastertide holidays, put a stop entirely to trout-fishing. Local rods who visited Trevor, Llan- gollen, Berwyn and Corwen report poor sport indeed, but plenty of snow, hail, sleet and boisterous north- westerly winds. At Mickle Tra cc 3 rd, hr. rhieph. Allcock • managed to creel a few small. trout. Some anglers - report having had fair sport amengst the flounders in the tidal reaches of the river Dee. On Friday and Saturday morning last, the river Bollin fished very well ; but the latter portion of the holidays was spoiled by the " Arctic " climatic conditions of the weather. Mr. T. Smith recorded foar trout, two of which scaled over a p-yund each. Mr. W. Robinson creeled a trout ' of 11b. 2.-tozs., and M. Henry WoOdone of I.lb. nozs., thelatter being the best spec,,imen recorded during the week end. Messrs. Pearson and Winning registered three trout each of good size and Messrs. Entwistle, Waywell, Baker, Brown, and Yearsley, a brace of trout each. Messrs. Parker, Brown jun., Shannon, Whittle, Harrison and Mather all took good fish. Mr. H. Baines hooked a big perch and Mr. Fred Bonnetl a nice dace, but these two fish were, of course; put care- fully back. The good results above mentioned yielded by the river Boffin, have given great satisfaction, and it is to be hoped that the good sport Will continue throughout the season. The re-organisation of the Anglers' Association is exciting some comment in local angling circles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MENIORIAL WINDOW IN ST. TFIONI AS'S CHU Reif.— On Tuesday morning a service was held in St. Thomas's Church for the dedication of the beautiful stained glass window which has been placed in the church in memory of the late Mr. and Mrs. Robert Davies. The window which has cost about £lOO represents "The Annuncia- tion" and beneath it is a brass plate bearing the following inscription "This window is the gift of parishioners and friends, in memory of Rdbert Davies, of Birchtleld, who died February 24th, 1902, and of Agnes his wife, who died July 19th, 1900." There was only a small attendance at the service which commenced with the singing of the Easter hymn "On the Resurrection morning," Mr. F. Rose presid- ing at the organ. A number of prayers were read by the Vicar (Rev. H. V. Pigot) and the Curate (Rev. C. A. M. Evans was also present. TRAP ACCIDENT.—A somewhat serious trap accident occurred in Grappenhall-road on Monday afternoon.. Two men, named Herbert H. Whitehead and Walter S. Dutton, were driving in a gig when the horse was frightened by a steam roller belonging to the Runcorn Rural District Council, and bolted. The animal dashed along at a furious pace, and both occupants were thrown out of the trap. Whitehead received a deep scar on the right elbow, Dutton sustained a fractured rib, and also suffered from contitiions and shock. They were attended by Dr. Parker. BAND OF HOPE MEETlNG.—Success attended the annual tea meeting in connection with' Hill Oliff and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope which was held in the Providence Schoolroom on Easter Monday. After tea a public meeting was held, the chair being occu- pied by the President, Rev. J. S. Hughes, who was sup- ported on the platform by the Rev. W. C. J. Neville, of Warrington, Mr. A. H. Dunn and Mr. George Whitfield (secretary), who presented a very encouraging report, showing the society to be in -a very flourishing con- dition. Special mention was made to the unabated in- terest.of the President in the work during the nine years the society had been in existence. The Chairman spoke upon the brighter outlook of the temperance cause' and also referred to the loss sustained by the death of seine of the old leaders. The Rev. W. C. J. Neville spoke of the usefulness of the Band of Hope, and appealed to all young people to remain firm to their pledge. A capital progra nine opened with a pianoforte solo by Miss Lena Duckworth, which was brilli- antly executed. She also sang very tastefully "The Better Lend," and was awarded an en- core. Miss Clara Kenworthy gave a very effec- tive rendering of "The Gift." "Killarney" was very tastefully sung by Master Stanley Jones, and• Miss Winnie Jones was well received in her song " Zeyder Zee." Mr. R. Pemberton delighted the audience with his humorous soup's, and a trio "A little farm well tilled," by iVlessrs.'G. and. S. Moss and J. Worrell was much enjoyed. Miss Pooley was encored for a well- executed violin solo "Ever of thee," and the selections on violin, mandoline, guitar' and piano by the Misses Pooley and Warren were well given and highly appre- ciated. Miss Edith Cole and Mr. G. Whitfield contri- buted recitations, and the dialogues "Who will be brave for temperance? " by the juvenile members and "Miss Diddle's Lodging g "by the seniors were creditably per- fornied. Daring the evening certificates were awarded to Miss Nellie Cliffe and Master G. Arthur W'nitfleld on behalrof the Wirrinqton Band of Hope 'Union for success in the" New Century Recruiting Campaign." Votes of thanks were accorded to the artistes and ladies who had presided at the tea, on the proposition of Mr. •A. H. Dunn, seconded by Mr. H. Brereton, and a very enjoyable gathering was brought to aclose. Mr. Hughes, Miss Pooley, Miss Povey, and Mr. A f. Dann acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>" Countess ." Fined Again.</head>
        <p>The " Countess ." Fined Again.</p>
        <p>Agnes tlalroy, otherwise known as the " Countess of Stamford and Warrington," was summoned to appear at the Stockport County Police Sessions on I_llturstlav Inc having travelled on the London and North-Westerti litiLwav without paying her fare. The defendant did not appear, and Mr. Lynde, who repre- sented the Company, asked for the ease to he heard in her absence as they did not Nri`ill to issue a warrant. The ease \yds one on with, and it was stated that on the ritli of :kpril, on the arrival of the 1.3 train from Macclesfield at Stockport, the tickets were examined. The defendant had no ticket. She said she had come from WarriTirt•in and had paid for travelling as far as Macclesfield. Afterwards when asked hr the fare she tendered -Id., and said she had etme from 13ramall. Tlte money was refused. Mr. Lynde stated that the. defendant was convicted for a. similar offence on April 7. She was a very peculiar woman. She had three furnished houses in Colvdeton, and they never knew which one to find her in. She could pay the fitw, the only difficulty being to ,f4et the money-. A fine of 20s. awl costs was inlp teed, the alternative being. a moutit's imprisonment.</p>
