      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-04-17_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>GREATER RUNCORN. Proposed Inclusiol of Weston and</head>
        <p>GREATER RUNCORN. Proposed Inclusiol of Weston and</p>
        <p>Halton.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Urban District Council has unanimously resolved to present a memorial to the Cheshire Ceuaty Council asking that the parishes of Weston and Halton shall be united with the urban district of Runcorn. The memorial sets out that the area of Runcorn is 1,215 acres, and the population according to the last census is 16,491. The rateable value, according to the countY basis, is £61,470 and the assessable value £60,785. For - the past year the general district rate was 3s. 6d._rates , and the poor rate 2s. 6d. Those rates include special for the provision of a free library, technical institute, elementary and secondary education, and cemetery. The area of the parish of Weston which it is pro- posed to absorb, is 1,025 acres; population, 2,115; rate- able value, £13,286 ; assessable value, £12,707. The net value of the agricultural land is £1,158, and the other properties £12,128. The rates for poor and sanl' tary purposes have averaged 2s. 10d. during the past , three years. The area of Halton is 1,852 acres ; popollT— tion, 1,238, rateable value, £6,413 ; assessable value' £5,309. The net value of agricultural land is £2,201 and of other properties £4,206. The rates for poor and sanitary purposes have averaged 2s. Bd. for the 131.ist three years. Both parishes adjoin the urban- district. and form part of the rural district; and, like Runcorn, form part of Runcorn Poor Law Union. - The Urban Council of Runcorn own waterworks supplY" ing their own district and Weston and Halton, the water being obtained from a well at Runcorn Heath. The Council have also a right to a supply from the Liver- pool Vyrnwy main. The water from the well is -certified by the county analyst as suitable for domestic use, but its hardness renders it unfit for manufacturing -purposes, and the manufacturers in the three parishes have asked for a supply suitable for their purposes. This could be ! done under the Council's agreement with the Liverpool Corporation. But as the water is mainly required bY manufacturers outside the urban district, it is not reasonable that the urban district should alone incur the expense and risk attending the necessary extension of mains and connections. The Rural District Council of Runcorn have given notice to the Urban Council, under section 28 of the Runcorn Commissioners' act, 1893, of, their desire to purchase such of the pipes, fittings, and apparatus for or connected with the distribution ot wafer as lie in the parishes of Weston and Halton. Such a division of the plant and area of distribution, besides being Most unfair to the Urban Council, as purchasers of the waterworks, would render the supply of a soft water to Manufacturers impossible on any terms. reasonable</p>
        <p>Part of Weston is dependent ution Runcorn for its sewerage System; the other part has no sewerage System. Halton has no system of sewerage, and the Local Government Board and County Council have pressed for the provision of a system. The Urban oouncil have made ample facilities for dealing with the sewage of Halton. In both Weston and Halton roaas and streets are unlighted, and no provision is made for the removal of nightsoil and ashpit refuse. The occu- piers have to ,undertake and pay for this work themselve.s at a cost equivalent to a considerable increase in their rates. Weston has no provision for the burial of lt.' dead,.and Weston and Halton have no local provision for dealing. with infectious disease. Runcorn has a residens _</p>
        <p>medic;il officer, an inspector of nuisances, and an infec- Vous diseases hospital. It is proposed to increase thig hospital to provide for both Weston and Halton. There is also at Runcorn a cottage hospital for non-infectioug cases.</p>
        <p>As to education Runcorn Council hai provided a free library and reading-room, a technical institute with 7, large number of evening classes suited to the industry). needs of the district, and a secondary day school. Tho technical institute would form a suitable building for 5 pupil teachers' centre and scholarship examinations. Runcorn has public slaughterhouses, a public market and fair ground a well-equipped tire brigade and recrea•- tion ground. Weston and Halton are without thes! advantages, and are dependent upon Runcorn for their use. Runcorn is the business centre for the parishes' The roads from Weston whether to the railway station or to the river, or to the surrounding country, Pass through Runcorn. The transporter bridge now in cours: of erection over the River Mersey will probably lea' to a great increase of traffic. Runcorn Council are prepared to offer tO Weston ancl Halton a differential district rate of 2s. in the for a period of ten years. Runcorn is divided into six wards,w each represented by three councillors. In case of tl!t incorporation of Weston and Halton with Runcorn, Al it proposed to create each added parish a new war" with three councillors each. The memorial prays the County Council ttcPct, cause a local inquiry to be held, and make an order unite the parishes.</p>
        <p>Easter .at Warrington. Easter Day and Bank Holiday were not favltire with the warm and spring-like weather whioh was eYi pe.rienced on Good Priday. During Sunday a cOILL wind and occasional showers of snow and, bail made, the conditions most unpleasant, and on Monday morn " ing there was snow and sleet.. The afternoon wa much brighter and many people §-night recreation in the country although the cold "wind continued. Easter Day is the festival of the year in the Anglicart and Roman Catholic Churches and the services on Sunday were of a jlyous character, befitt- ing the occasion. Most of the Churches 4 were very prettily decorated with spring flowers and presented a beautiful appearance. A-5/ usual the number of communicants was very large. During the week end there were numproug bookings from Bank Quay and Cheshire Line 5' Stations to seaside and other resorts. From the Cen- tral Station about 2.000 persons were booked to Mao' chester and Belle Vue, 2,000 to Liverpool and Nel(, • Brighton,' 300 to Southport and a numbe r to the P' of Man. There were more bookings to Mancheste: than there have been for some years past. Frora Bank Quay Station about .600 went to Frodshaiii, 91 to , Chester, 73 to Blackpool, 71 to Helsby, and 78 t° :Birmingham and Wolverhampton. Others booked to North Wales.</p>
        <p>A REMARKABLE REPORT.—The %tuts Report of the Eagle insurance Company seems to be the great even in the Insurance world this year. The "Post Maga" zine," the leading authority on the subject, speakiy;- highly of the steady progress made by the Company the past five years, and its distinct advance in finan-cia' strength and prospects, says that "Although the purt: ticipating policy holders can on an average drawincas' one-third of the premiums paid during this period the Eagle adds materially to its reserves, so tts to place the future beyond the reach of accidents. In this way the Company ought to be especially attractive to young lives, who in some instances have an amount added t; the face value of their policies actually in excess of their total payments during the five years. Tn the° days of a mania for amalgamations it is refreshing t,c; find an office like the Eagle, af teran existence of 'teal a century, so well able to stand on its own. Its "Pt , Century prospectus is a model of simplicity and clear BOSS."</p>
